      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>POULTRY PEN.</head>
        <p>POULTRY PEN.</p>
        <p>HINTS AND INTERESTING FACTS AND CHATS FOR AMATEURS.</p>
        <p>SUMMER SHOWS.</p>
        <p>It is a noticeable• fact, says "The Feathered World," that such shows as have had the courage to continue as usual have in the majority of cases reaped a fair reward, while some have even experienced record results. It behoves all good fanciers, therefore, to keep the ball rolling, and if they possess likely birds to exhibit them as much as may be. It is riot, of course, very likely that a number of winners will be claimed at startling prices this season, but it is a fact that the Fancy is- still gaining many recruits at the present moment, and that these recruits must be in the niarket for good stuff. I do not for one moment believe that anyone who exhibits a really choice bird dur- ing 1915 with a catalogue price of, say, £lO upon it need fear'that there will be no claimants. In addition to the newcomers there are plenty of us still as anxious as ever to be in the front rank, and if we see bargains going we shall claim them, never fear, even if only to avoid being leit out when the good time does come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>EGGS OR TABLE CHICKENS?</head>
        <p>EGGS OR TABLE CHICKENS?</p>
        <p>The problem with which many poultry keepers at the present time are faced that is, those whose plans have not been pre-arranged—is what birds to keep, and what is to be the mode of pro- cedure. This is somewhat of a difficulty, since the majority of farms are wel.l stocked with both chickens and adults. there are few poultry keepers who do not hatch more chickens than can be safely accommodated, and they only realise the fact as the chickens grow, for in the early stages overcrowding is not so apparent. Their minds are exercised with the momentous question which birds to keep. The chief point for consideration is, therefore, what is the local demand—eggs or table chickens? Egg Production.</p>
        <p>When a large number of breeds are on hand of different ages, ranging from January to May hatched chickens, the question resolves itself into what to retain, and for what particular purpose. If the object is to supply .the district with eggs, there is then practically no limit to the number of breeds from which to select. If the breeds on the farm consist of any of the following varieties—Anconas, Leghorns, Cam- pines, Houdans, or Andalusitins—a mistake can- not be made if reliance be placed upon any of these to keep up the egg supply. Furthermore, if any of the heavier breeds, such as Orpingtons, Wyandottes or Plymouth Rocks, are also among those from which selection is to be made, it is an excellent plan to select a non-sitter and one of the general purpose breeds—a white Wyan- dotte or buff Orpingtim for preference. It will thus be found that there is a greater chance of getting a more regular supply of eggs through- out the year than were one type only to be kept.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>Table Chickens.</head>
        <p>Table Chickens.</p>
        <p>If, on the other hand, the demand is for the best quality of table chickens, the selection is more limited. Not only so, but for the produc-' tion of a first-class table delicacy, the conditions for rearing must be favourable. A light and dry soil is very conducive to rapid growth, which is of first importance for this branch of poultry- rearing. Under favourable conditions nothing will give better results than dark or silver grey Dorkings. They may be relied upon either pure or crossed with an Indian or an Old English Game cock. It is, however, only under excep- tionally favourable conditions that this breed or cross can be reared, and consequently when these favourable conditions do not exist, an excellent substitute will be found in either Faverolles or Sussex, both of which may be suc- cessfully reared in conditions quite unsuited to the Dorking.—Expert, in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>DUCKS. Hints for Beginners—How to Commence.</p>
        <p>There are three ways of starting egg-culture from ducks :—(1) Purchasing a setting of eggs, (2) buying day, week, or month-old ducklings, and (3) ordering stock ducks, full grown, ready for laying. The first-mentioned from January to June, ducklings March to Skeptember, and the latter during autumn. Nos. 1 and 2 have the advan- tage in that they require the smaller outlay,. but No. 3 method brings in an immediate return. _ _</p>
        <p>It should he impressed upon the reader that White Runners are essentially land egg-produc- ing ducks, only requiring sufficient water to *kink and bathe in. Duck eggs require 28 days' incubation. Day-olds should be fea on luke- warm milk or water, and soft bread; hard- boiled egg is like leather to digest. Do not disturb layers. A light at night up- sets them and stops egg-laying. There is an opening for 80,000 women and partially disabled soldiers to occupy the waste lands of Great Britain to advantage. Think it over.—" The Poultry World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>MINORCA AND THE COMING BOON.</head>
        <p>THE MINORCA AND THE COMING BOON.</p>
        <p>All utility breeds of fowls, says a writer in "The Feathered World," will be in great re- quest after the war—which, with all its horrors, must come to an end sooner or later—and the Minorca holds a high reputation for its pro- ductiveness. The Minorca will not suffer in anything like .the ratio of those breeds with nothing but purely fancy characteristics Apart 'from its attractiveness in this direction, it has old and sustained recommendations for large eggs and a high rate of production. It is these breeds that will be in demand when hostilities are brought to an end and when wastage has to be repaired, both at home and within the devastated and German-pillaged areas on the Continent. The economic side of poultry-keep- ing is sure to receive such a stimulus as it has never before experienced in this country, and the Minorca is one of the breeds which is bound to play an important part in the boom, in con- junction with other popular Mediterranean varieties. _</p>
        <p>The future is more important than the pre- sent, and those who may be making some sacri- fice now—which we engaged in live stock breed- ing must do—have the consolation of knowing that in producing our usual numbers of stand- ard-bred apecimens, whatever our immedilte losses may be, we are acting and -svorkirg in the country's interests during its - period of stress, and are filling .just a's necessary, patrio- tic and useful positions, in a sense, as should- ering a rifle or the making of shells. That the country must be fed is the first important factcir, and our national .neglect of agricultural pursuits has never been more evident than it is at the present time, and during the next few years we are justified in prophesying that the results of that neglect will become more clear.</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, Sze.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
