      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>FARM OD GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM OD GARDEN.</p>
        <p>THE MIGRATION OF FARM WORKERS. One hears on every hand of the constant migration of farm workers from the country districts to the towns, and there appear to be several reasons for the gradual, but steady, influx to the industrial centres. Some say the reason is the low wages paid in the country, and urge that there would be no shortage were adequate wages paid. The contention thus held appears quite sound, but there is really little to support this argument, for the price Paid for agricultural labour is no index to the shortage. Although the shortage is greater in some districts than in others, it does not by any means confine itself to places where wages are lowest. For example, in Lancashire far- mers are offering anything up to 26e. or 28s. per week, in some cases more, and the shortage is practically the same as in districts where the wage is barely .R 1 per week. How is it that farmers who offer 28s. per week cannot get men, who will work for 20s. per week in the towns? There are dozens of such men, many experi- enced in farm work.—" Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>TO AVOID LOSSES OF YOUNG PIGS.</head>
        <p>TO AVOID LOSSES OF YOUNG PIGS.</p>
        <p>Handle your sows and accustom them to handling, but do not worry them at farrowing time. Be on hand when the litter arrives and as each pig is born put it into it box that has been well bedded with straw so as to be warm and comfortable. When the sow settles down .and is resting easily let the pigs nurse. Later if she becomes restless return them to the box. Be careful that you do not allow her to crush half the litter while you are at 'dinner or are doing work at some other part of the farm. The first four or five days are the most critical for the young pigs, and a little time with them at that period pays big dividends later. Most breeders allow the sow only water for twenty- four to forty-eight hours after farrowing. A few give one good feed to fill the sow up and keep he quiet. We prefer the first plan. In- crease the amount of food gradually and avoid any sudden changes in kind of food supplied. Have the sleeping beds dry and fresh, keep the troughs clean, and see that all the pigs get plenty of exercise and sunlight.—" Country Gentleman."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>USEFUL INSECT.</head>
        <p>A USEFUL INSECT.</p>
        <p>Ladybirds and their larva) should be regarded as most useful insects, and should _never be</p>
        <p>destroyed, writes D. R. Stewart Macdougal, in the Transactions of the Highland and Agricul- tural Society for Scotland. Both on herbaceous plants and on trees they can be found at work, amidcolonies of green fly, e.g. on rose-bushes and on broad-leaved trees, and on conifers the ladybirds can sometimes te found in such numbers as to bring the aphides into subjection. The insect-eating habit of the majority of lady- birds has led to ladybirds from other countries being imported to a new country, in order that they might war on some aphid or scale enemy. One of the most successful of such importa- tions has been the introduction of a red and black ladybird of Australia, named Vedalia cardinalis, to Californa. The orange groves of California were being ravaged by the fluted scale, an accidental introduction from Austra- lia. When the orange groves seemed likely to be ruined a number of Vedalia cardinalis beetles were imported from Australia to Cali- fornia, and many more were bred. These lady- birds were then set free here and there in the orange groves. They started destroying the scale, and they have now quite mastered it and successfully kept it in control.</p>
        <p>UNEXHAUSTED VALUE or MANURES. Compensation for manurial residues has al- ways been a difficult and intricate subject for valuers to deal with, and more so with manures than with feeding-stuffs. In the former case soil and cropping affect the matter so much as to make many Complications. Some soils, for example, give a much better response than others to some phosphatic and other manures, and the effect, again,, is different on arable and grass. Bones work very differently on different soils, decay varying in duration and conse- quently affecting the residual yearly values, or even, it is said, may give no result at all, just as other manures have even been found harm- ful—:-potash and nitrogen occasionally on grass, for example, and acid manures on soils desti- tute of lime. Manures for fruit, hops, and other special crops, again, if applied to other crops have somewhat different values.—" Agri- cultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CONDITION OF BRITISH CROPS.</head>
        <p>CONDITION OF BRITISH CROPS.</p>
        <p>The Crop Reporters of the Board of Agricul- ture state that the month of June "Ls every- where very dry, nearly every district experienc- ing a drought until about the 25th or 27th, when rains were fairly general. The drought had a more or less injurious effect on all crops except wheat, and the rains in the last few days of June were of great benefit, especially to roots and pastures. The wheat crop is generally healthy, and was little injured by the dry weather; nevertheless it will hardly prove an average, except in the south-west. Barley and oats have both wanted rain badly, and neither looks like yielding more than nine-tenths of an average crop. Barley is much the better of the two in the North. Straw is in nearly all districts very short. Beans are more satisfactory, and with wheat, approach more nearly to the average than any Other crop; there is, however, blight in some districts. Peas are less satisfactory. _</p>
        <p>_ Potatoes are generally backward. In the first part of June frosts did .a certain amount of damage among the earlies ; the main crop, however, took little harm. The recent rains will probably be of much benefit. Turnips and swedes have suffered much from fly, and many fields ,have had to be re-sown, a large number of farmers have waited for the rain before sow- ing, and the recent rains should enable much land to be sown, and give the crop a start. Mangolds are backward for want of rain; but appear quite healthy. Very good progress has been made with the clover hay crop, the bulk of which has already been secured in very good condition, although the quantity is small. Harvesting the meadow hay is in full swing in the south, but has hardly commenced in the later districts; the yield is twenty per cent. below average. Pas- tures, with few exceptions, became very bare, but great improvement is expected, from the rain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>OVER-FEEDING TOMATOES IN THE OPEN. Many people have an idea that tomatoes are gross feeders, and in their early stages treat them too well, with the result that the growth becomes coarse and the blooms fail to set. Some few years ago a well-known gardener showed us some tomatoes growing in ashes only. The plants were sturdy and short-jointed, and the fruit had set freely at every joint. This is the time to feed, and the grower above referred to used to give alternate waterings of liquid man- ure and clear water, the result being a heavy crop of medium-sized, handsome fruit. Too much moisture is also injurious, as it is very liable to encourage disease. Some few years ago we recollect having seen a heavy crop of toma- toes from plants grown in pots. These were plunged, the restriction of. the roots checking the growth and leading to a free set, which, when the fruit was swelling, was assisted by feeding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>TRUE BEAUTY IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>TRUE BEAUTY IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>It would be trite to say in these days that there is no true beauty in a garden adorned only with the orthodox spring and summer bedding plants, for that has been repeated almost ad nauseam. , Yet there is still a good deal , of Stiff and elementary work done, espe- cially in small gardens. Plain bedding has its advantages. Almost anybody can pick up what there is to learn in the public parks; but to acquire the more subtle knowledge of advanced artistic gardening is another matter.—" Agri- cultUral Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY IT, 1915,</p>
        <p>the town, accompanied by Crosfield's Scouts Band, taking a further collection for wounded soldiers next Saturday, July 24th, the weather permitting, and it is hoped that a "bumping" amount will be collected. All proceeds will be equally divided amongst the four hospitals for the purchase of comforts for wounded " Tommies."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>ROSES WITH GREEN CENTRES.</head>
        <p>ROSES WITH GREEN CENTRES.</p>
        <p>Judging from the many examples which come to hand these seem to be very common this year. Such malformed blooms are generally due to a check of some kind, such, for instance, as. a severe spring frost. When on examination it is possible to detect these malformed blooms it is a good plan to remove them early, and so encourage the smaller buds to develop pro-</p>
        <p>Perly. Another reason may be that the soil in which the plant is growing is exhausted, in which case a mulch of rotten manure and fre- quent applications of water would greatly help. Some kinds are addicted to this habit, and all such should be discarded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>Continued in Next Column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WALKING DAY.</head>
        <p>WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>PARISH PROCESSIONS.</p>
        <p>How The War Affected Church</p>
        <p>Walking Day.</p>
        <p>SOLENIN APPEAL.</p>
        <p>The object that We have in view is to make a solemn appeal to all to do their duty at this great crisis of the nation's history. We do not think our people in England or in War- rington have yet realised as a whole the awful solemnity of the present moment. . . . Therefore, we are going to make this effort as representative of the Church in this parish to stir everyone up. We are not out to show ourselves off, but to make a very solemn appeal, and so we shall walk—with just a banner or two to mark each school, six abreast—in our simple Sunday dress, through those parts of our parish which seem to need our presence most.... We look to all to help us; for it is for the honour and glory of God, for the true faith of Jesus Christ, and for the salvation of our country that we are fighting in this awful war.</p>
        <p>So wrote, in his Parish Magazine, the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis), in explanation of his Church's decision to abandon the usual gaiety and show. of Walking Day and substitute the simple and dignified appeal above detailed. The Parish Church Sunday School scholars did, of course, have a treat—though of a con- siderably modified form. It took place a fort- night ago, and was given by the officials of the Church and its Missions to those who had made the necessary Sunday attendances. Despite the war-cloud which was hanging over—and the existence of which, one is compelled.to say, had been emphasised (however worthy the object) in .a manner hardly calculated either to impress or please the children—a happy time was spent in the Training College Field, Salisbury- street, by kind permission of Canon Stevenson. For two hours in the afternoon and for a simi- lar period in the evening, games and dancing respectively were indulged in, the bhnd of the Blue Coat School playing for the latter. Ex- cellent teas were partaken of in the various schools of the parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WHAT THE WEATHER DID.</head>
        <p>WHAT THE WEATHER DID.</p>
        <p>The processions should have taken place the following day, Sunday. Unfortunately the weather proved wet; and in accordance with the original arrangement this second section of the programme was postponed for a week. This time fortune favoured the function, with the result that a most • impressive and inspiring display took place, on Sunday last. Headed by the surpliced choir, the Rector and other clergy, and the Church officers, large and well-marshalled concourses marched in proces- sion from their various starting places to the Parish Church by the following lengthy and carefully planned route: Manchester-road, Church-street, Greenall-street, Ellesmere-street, Napier-strtet, Mersey-street, Church-street, Fennel-streVt, School Brow., Dalton Bank, Claud-street, Hume-street, Marsh House-lane, Padgate-lane, Alexandra-street, Manchester- road and Church-street, to the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>The girls left their Fairfield Schools punctually at two and marched to the Church. where, the choir, clergy and officers awaited them. At Greenall-street the procession was joined by the boys of the National School. Greenall-street, Ellesmere-street and Napier- street were then traversed, and the St. Clement's Mission scholars entered the proces.- sion in Mersey-street. The children walked the whole distance in neat and good order, and although there was no band, monotony was dispelled by the banners and specially pretty "Sunday dresses" worn by most of the chil- dren.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>• SERVICE-CENTRES.</head>
        <p>• SERVICE-CENTRES.</p>
        <p>The monotony of the proceedings .was also relieved by the fact that at four centres the procession made a halt to sing suitable hymns and offer short- prayers "asking God's blessing on the cause they had at heart." When—at the junction of Mersey-street and Napier-street-- the St. 'Clement's scholars joined in, those at the head of the Parish Church girls and the St. Clement's girls sung the first hymn, "Onward, Christian Soldiers." At the point where Hop- wood-street meets School Brow, the St. George's scholars met the stream; and, the choir stand- ing aside in the open space by the Bricklayers' Arms, there was rendered " Jesu, Lover of My Soul" by those leading Parish Church boys and St. George's girls. The top of Gorsey-lane next being reached, the St. Clement's boys' leaders sang the third hymn, "Holy Father, in Thy Mercy" (for absent friends). The . procession then passed on once again, the choir leaving and re-joining at the Cemetery Gates, Whence it headed the whole procession back again to the Parish Church. Here in the open space, with all the schools grouped round, was sung, "0, God of Love, and King of peace" and "The Church's One Foundation." \ As at all the centres, prayers were rendered for the soldiers and sailors; and the function closed with the pronouncement by the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) of the Blessing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ST. JAMES', LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES', LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon also the scholars of St. James', Latchford, walked in Procession— headed by the choir, and marshalled by their Superintendent, Mr. W. H. Stansfield. The start was made from their schools, whence they proceeded to the new St. Hilda's Mission C.hureh, Slater-street. Here they halted, hymns being sung and prayers offered on behalf of all men on active service. The procession was afterwards resumed and presently .a further halt was made by the old burial ground in Knutsford-road--where another short service was held. Thence the scholars proceeded to Wilderspool-causeway. After the singing of "0, God, Our Help in Ages Past," and Bene- diction by the Vicar (Rev. H. B. Firth) they dispersed in quiet manner. Both processions were witnessed by large and impressed crowds.</p>
        <p>BUCKLEY-STREET FREE CHURCH</p>
        <p>The Methodist Free Church School, Buckley- street, a fortnight ago, gave their scholars a well-enjoyed field day. Despite the generally unfavourable weather a few hours' sunshine were experienced. A substantial tea was much appreciated, and races were indulged in. The Morris Dancers paraded a few streets in the neighbourhood and collected for our wounded soldiers the sum of £3. Of course, the Chil- dren's Cot has suffered the loss of the collection usually made on Walking Day for the help of its upkeep. The Morris Dancers and friends will parade</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.—Continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>BLIND STRAWBERRY PLANTS._</head>
        <p>BLIND STRAWBERRY PLANTS._</p>
        <p>This term is applied to plants that do not flower. prom th,e number of letters that come to hand this trouble seems to be very prevalent this season. These blind plants are, as a rule, of very vigorous growth and produce tempting and. healthy runners, but to plant these is only ,courting failure, as "once blind, always.blind." They should be at once grubbed up and thrown on the fire heap, and runners secured from plants that have borne fruit, or, better still, from an entirely fresh stock. Readers would do well to examine their strawberry beds .and at once destroy barren plants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TURNIPS AND CARROTS.</head>
        <p>TURNIPS AND CARROTS.</p>
        <p>Now that rain has fallen in sufficient quan- tity to moisten the soil well, a breadth of turnips should be got in. After the drills have been drawn, strew in them either wood-ashes or some artificial manure to ensure quick growth and prevent the attacks of the turnip flea.' Where but small quantities are grown, this pest may be kept in check by sprinkling the plants as soon as they appear with a fine-rosed pot, or heavily syringing them if the weather be dry. This should be continued until the leaves have become fully developed, afte4.which the turnip flea, as a rule, gives but little trouble. Horn carrots may now be sown also, and if the weather is favourable nice roots-Will be forthcoming by the autumn, and will be highly prized.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL AND WALKING</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL AND WALKING</p>
        <p>DAY.</p>
        <p>Men's Officials Say Workers' Views</p>
        <p>Were Ignored.</p>
        <p>The matter of the curtailed observance of Walking Day again formed a theme of discus- sion at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, held at the Socialist Hall on Tuesday evening, Mr. F. Berry pre- siding. At a previous meeting the Secretary (Mr. Chas. Dukes) had been instructed to write to the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) asking why the proposed public meeting to decide re the question of holding Walking Day as usual, or otherwise; had not been held. A reply was now read from the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) to the effect that owing to the religious bodies in the town resolving not to hold_ the usual procession, his Worship did not deem the projected public meeting necessary. "A Bad Stroke of Business" Says the Chair-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>man.</head>
        <p>man.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he thought that the in- formation forwarded by the Trades Council, con- veying as it did the opinion of thousands of Trade Unionists, should have convinced the Mayor of the necessity of calling a public meet- ing. To his mind, the Trades Council, which was the mouthpiece of the organised workers of the town, had been absolutely ignored. The Trades Council were asked to ascertain the opinion of the workers and they took the trouble to do so, and then Walking Day was to all in- tents and purposes abandoned in spite of that opinion. He thought it was a bad stroke of business. The children had been. duped out of their festival and the workers out of their half-day holiday, and the shopkeepers had been deprived of the harvest which the holiday usually brought them. And all to no real pur- pose.</p>
        <p>A delegate opined that the responsibility for the curtailment of the Walking Day festivities 'rested not so much with the Churches and Sun- day Schools as with the town's officials. Wit- tingly or unwittingly the Mayor had slighted</p>
        <p>the Trades Council in first seeking their advice and then ignoring it.</p>
        <p>Were the Workers "Had"?</p>
        <p>The Secretary said that he thought the workers could all agree that they had "been had"; that was the sum and substance of it all. To him the trag4dy of the whole business was that the workers declared in overwhelming numbers in favour of the holiday and then quietly succumbed just because somebody at the Town Hall said they were not going to have a holiday. (Hear, hear.) Seeing that the holiday would not have interfered with the production of munitions of war, he thought it was a dis- play of weakness on the part of the workers to have taken the matter lying down. If the Unions had said that they would have the holiday it would not have mattered what the Mayor or anybody else said. "It is not," the Secretary concluded, "merely a matter of Walking Day, but on it hinges .the vhole ques- tion of a Wakes Week and Of many other holi- days. Still. I suppose, if we protest they will feel sorry for us and do the same again next time. . . . If the workers want a definite Wakes Week—as they have said they do—let them declare it through their Unions and enforce it there, and then we shall not have to go cap-in-hand to these people. No action was taken in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>LIBERAL WORKERS'. ROLL OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WORKERS'. ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>The following names have been added to the Liberal Workers' Roll of Honour which is being compiled by Mr. Sanders at the Liberal Club:—</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD. Daniels, Walter, Rifle Brigaie. Drury, J., South Lancs. • - .Tolley. Ben, South Lanes. Lee, James, Royal Garrison Artillery.</p>
        <p>' WHITECROSS. Fletcher, Percy, South Wales Borderers. Fletcher Charles King's Royal Rifles. _</p>
        <p>Richardson, Thomas, 3/4th South Lancs. Whitby, R., 7th King's (Liverpool) Regt.</p>
        <p>HOWLEY. Barry, John, 9th Battalion, Lancs. Fusiliers. Barry, Thomas, 4th South Lancs. Territorials. Challinor, Ed. Percy, Royal Fusiliers. Jenkins, Sapper T. J., East Lancs. Division, Signal Co., Royal Engineers.</p>
        <p>ORFORD. Jolley, James, South Lancs. Joseph Ma'son, R.F.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>TILL FATHER RETURNS.</head>
        <p>TILL FATHER RETURNS.</p>
        <p>Pathetic Case Of Family Of Neglected Children.</p>
        <p>A sad case came under the notice of the Warrington Guardians, at Wednesday's meet- ing, when it was reported that a soldier whose wife had been sent to prison for neglecting her children had called at the Clerk's office and made a request • that the Guardians would adopt his children until he returned froM the Front.</p>
        <p>The Clerk added that the wife had pa vned everything in the house. The woman was not a fit person to have the custody of the.childr,ra, and it certainly seemed a case in which the Guardians should take care of -them.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Mather agreed that something ouvit to be done for the children, but he would prefer to see the Guardians hoard them out in some respectable home, instead of bringing' them into the House. He thought it was only the fair thing to do by any man who was fighting for his country and placed in such regrettable circumstances. The children ought to be looked after by the Guardians and protected from the taint of pauperism. The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) : The man himself has asked that we should adopt them. Mr. Mather: Yes; but that's no reason why we should not do as I suggest. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I wish Mr. Mather and other members would drop the word pauperism. The Chairman: We should all like to drop the thing, but we can't. It was decided that the children be adopted till their father's return.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WANTED A UNIFORM.</head>
        <p>WANTED A UNIFORM.</p>
        <p>Guardians Refuse Workhouse Officers' Request.</p>
        <p>The House Committee reported at 'Wednes- day's meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians that. they had decided to refuse the request of a number of officers in the employ of the Guardians to be allowed to wear some sort of uniform, as they were engaged, to a certain extent, on Government 'work-. The Staff at Padgate Homes.</p>
        <p>The Padgate Children's Homes Committee stated that they had given consideration to the question. of the staff at the Homes, and had come to the conclusion that the present staff was sufficient. The question of schools was put off till the new school at Warrington had been completed. The same committee advised that Mr. Wor- rail, porter at the Homes, be recommended as a suitable man to take charge of the Homes in the absence at any time of the Superintendent. Cast of 'Keeping Catholic Children.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather mentioned that the cost of keen- ing the Catholic children at the special Catholic Homes, to which they were recently sent, from •Pailgate, would be £1,140 per Unnip.a. 'Labour 'Master Joins Up.</p>
        <p>It was reported that Mr. Robert Riding, 'labour master, had enlisted in the 4th (Terri- torial) Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment. Mr. -A. J. Alley was 'in the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>IN THE CIWURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE CIWURTS.</p>
        <p>REGISTRATION OF LODGERS.</p>
        <p>A Warning To Lodgers And Lodging-</p>
        <p>House Keepers.</p>
        <p>At Saturday's Borough Police Court two women were charged under the Aliens' Re- striction Act, 1914, with giving false informa- tion as to their identity when staying at local lodging-houses. Two " Husbands "—One "Wife."</p>
        <p>The first to be put • into the dock was Rose Evans, alias Amy Mather, of no settled abode. She was first charged on remand with having been drunk and disorderly in Bold-street, the previous Saturday, and also with having given false information at the lodging-house, 12, Ship- yard, on June 29th. She pleaded guilty. Detective-Sergeant Reddington gave evidence to the effect that the previous Saturday night, July 3rd, information reached him that pris- oner and a man were in a Ship-yard lodging- house and that the woman refused to heave when ordered to do so. He went to the house and found the woman in a drunken state, to- gether with a man who was dealt with at the previous Monday's Court. Witness haled to eject the woman, who then created a scene by quarrelling with the man and trying to stab witness in the face with a hat-pin. Her " Real " Husband.</p>
        <p>Inquiries at the lodging-house showed that prisoner stayed there, with another Man, on June 29th, passing as that man's w:fe and signing the registration form as " Alice Rogers." On July 3rd she came 'ham- tc the lodging-house with another man an] repre- sented herself as his wife. When Ler atten- tion was called to the fact that she Pa.(' a few days previously represented herself as the wife of another man, she said: "Oh, this is my real husband."</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable told the Bench that the woman, who was a stranger to the town, had been convicted between thirty and forty times in Manchester. _</p>
        <p>She was sentenced to a month's imprison- ment, and as she left the dock remarked to the Bench: "Oh, thank you! God bless you all; it's very lenient of you</p>
        <p>3.30 on Saturday afternoon he went into a public-house and found defendant selling "deaf and dumb postcards." He was handing the cards round the room, and collecting them, or the money for them, on the return journey. He made the customers believe he was deaf and dumb. He was making signs, and never spoke a word. All the customers seemed to believe he was deaf and dumb. Witness pulled him up and prisoner tried to deceive him, too. When witness revealed his identity to prisoner, the latter admitted that he was not dumb, but stated that he was deaf. He admitted, when taxed, that be had no certifi- cate. He had .had one, but it had expired, and he had neglected to renew it. Did Not Know a Licence Was Necessary.</p>
        <p>Prisoner, whose speech and manner gave the impression of a man of education and some refinement, said, in reply to the usual questiou as to self-extenuation, that he w;v afraid he was guilty of the small offence. " You see," Jw added, "I take these cards round and earn !icy living bip selling them. I didn't know a licence was necessary for selling tie cards in rublic- huUSCE.,;l thourfi,:- ;t wos only t..-.....?!-ary for calling at private houses." The Chief Constable said that prisoner went from town to town selling the cards at a penny each. He (Mr. Nicholls) thought that a good talking to and a fine of 7s. 6d: would meet the case. It was obtaining money by fraud. Prisoner went into public-houses and did not speak. That was the whole thing. The Chairman (the Mayor), in announcing the decision of the Bench to inflict a fine of 7s. 6d., said: "Warrington has no room for 'spongers' like you. There's a sufficient num- berof genuine cases, without people like you." _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable, who had to shout the Bench's decision into prisoner's ear, told him that his cards would be confiscated, and that if he printed any more, he would• probably lose those as well. Prisoner: Is it illegal?</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: Absolutely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DRINK WRECKS ANOTHER WOMAN.</head>
        <p>DRINK WRECKS ANOTHER WOMAN.</p>
        <p>The second prisoner gave the name of Ada Rirnmer, and, in addition to the charge of giving false information on June 24th, she was charged with having been disorderly on July 3rd.—She. denied the disorderliness and in reply to the other charge said that she did not know that she had committed any offence.</p>
        <p>The. police evidence on the first charge was to the effect that she conducted herself in a disorderly manner with a man, using obscene language and struggling with him. Detective-Sergeant lieddington stated that he found that the prisoner came to McCabe's lodging-house, 5, Stanley-street, on June 24th, with a man named Kelly. She gave her name as "Bridget Kelly," but that was incorrect., The Chief Constable said the Bootle police, to whom the prisoner's photograph had been sent, reported 35 convictions against her in Liverpool and Bootle. Prisoner stoutly denied these, and the case was adjourned in order that further inquiries might be made. Prisoner was again brought before the Court on Wednesday, when the second charge was gone into, and when she said that the, man, with whom she went to the lodging-house told her to give the name of Bridget Kelly. Why Sister Had To Turn Her Out! The Chief Constable now said that it had been found that most of the convictions which had been 'assumed to be the woman's were not hers, hut she had actually been convicted six times for drunkenness. It was thought highly desirable by the authorities that magistrates should make an example in the cases of these people who went about giving false names and descriptions when called upon to register under the Act. No doubt, as the prisoner had said, drink was the cause of her ruin. She had a sister in a respectable position in the vicinity of Bootle, and prisoner had been living with her, but the sister, had been obliged to turn her out because of her drinking habits.' The woman was sent to gaol for a month with bard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>LODGING-HOUSE KEEPER'S NEGLECT.</head>
        <p>LODGING-HOUSE KEEPER'S NEGLECT.</p>
        <p>Arising out of a case of an unsavoury de- scription which was heard at Wednesday's Court and which resulted in a female prisoner being sent to gaol for a month's hard labour, Mary Ann Halliwell, who keeps -a lodging- house at 12, Dolman's-lane, was summoned for failing to obtain from the woman the necessary signed declaration under the Aliens' Restric- tion Act, 1914.—She pleaded that she was "no scholar."</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that the woman who had been sent to gaol Went to lodge at the defendant's lodging-house and defendant failed to ask her to fill in the necessary form. This was an offence which was looked upon with a good deal, of seriousness, because the Order in Council under' the Act was being treated with impunity by quite a lot of people. , Much publicity had been given to the requirements of the Act, and all that was required of defend- ant was that she should have handed the woman a form to fill in, but she failed to do that.</p>
        <p>Detective-Sergeant Reddington said that the defendant was duly supplied With forms, and when he called upon her with reference to this case she still had three blank ones in her possession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>First Case Of Its Kind.</head>
        <p>The First Case Of Its Kind.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. H. Thornton) said that as this was the first case of the kind to come before the Court, the magistrates had decided to deal leniently with the defendant, who would only be fined 10s. He pointed out that defendant was liable to a fine of .2100, and urged her, for the future, to be sure that she saw to it that her lodgers filled up the re- quisite forms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>PUGNACIOUS PATRICK.</head>
        <p>PUGNACIOUS PATRICK.</p>
        <p>How Drink And An Irishman Made An Unhappy Combination.</p>
        <p>Patrick Egan, of no fixed abode, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Monday, with being drunk and disorderly at the Central Station on Saturday night, and with assault- jag the police. Asked if he pleaded guilty or not guilty to the first charge, prisoner said: "It's the fault of the man who brought me here." The Clerk: You are also charged with assaulting the police. Do you plead guilty or not guilty to that? Prisoner (with a-rich Irish accent): No, sir. No such thing, at all!, That's the fellow (as P.C. Joynson entered the box). Juggling With Luggage.</p>
        <p>P.C. Joynson said that at about 9.30 on Saturday night he was on the Central Station, when he heard an unusual noise coming from the general waiting room. Proceeding there, he found prisoner throwing luggage about. Witness remonstrated with him, and he said he would have witness's heart out, and that any Englishman who fought against Germany ought to be served the same. On the way to the Police Station 'prisoner was very violent. He acted like a madman, and swore and kicked witness and P.C. Clarke,, who assisted in the troublesome process. .</p>
        <p>Prisoner: I wouldn't speak a wrong word to anybody. P.C. Clarke corroborated his 'colleague's statement. •</p>
        <p>Asked if he had anything to say' prisoner answered that it was the policeman's fault.. The Clerk pointed out to prisoner that it was not the policeman who made him _drunk. •</p>
        <p>_ Prisoner: No. But I wouldn't do any wrong, and that's a fact.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that he had just received information that prisoner had been "stabbing a wall, with a knife." . The Bench, over whom the Mayor presided, passed sentence of two months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>DEAF AND DONE.</head>
        <p>DEAF AND DONE.</p>
        <p>XVllere Soloman's Wiles Failed To</p>
        <p>Prove Effective.</p>
        <p>Alfred Soloman, of 17, Baker-street, West Derby, Liverpool, was charged at the Warring- ton Police Court on Monday, with peddling without a licence.</p>
        <p>Detective-Sergeant Reddington stated that at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>"SHIPS OF THE AIR."</head>
        <p>"SHIPS OF THE AIR."</p>
        <p>Man Who Joined The 4th South Lanes.</p>
        <p>By Means Of Fairy Tales.</p>
        <p>A middle-aged man named William Green- wood, a Private in the South Lancs. Regiment, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Thursday, with making a false statement on attestation papers, in order to enlist. Military evidence was given to the effect that prisoner enlisted at St. Helens on July sth, in the 4th South Lancs. Regiment. When on parade, afterwards, prisoner was observed to be drilling very reluctantly. The drill-sergeant went up to him and asked him why he wasn't putting more energy into his drill. Prisoner replied: "At my time of life it's too much for a man." He was told that as he had enlisted he would have to "do his bit." He was ques- tioned and stated that he had not been in the Army before, but had been to sea in a number of ships, the names of which were unknown. It was subsequently ascertained that he had been discharged from the Loyal South Lancs. Regi- ment. When taxed with making a false state- ment, prisoner said he was drunk at the time he sigried the attestation papers, but the signa- ture was not that of a drunken man. Besides, no recruiting officer would swear in a drunken man. Prisoner came up on the Friday, was drunk on the Saturday, in the guard-room till Monday; and could not do anything on the Tues- day. The Chief Constable said that the names of the ships prisoner had given had been searched for, but did not exist; they were on no register. "They must be ships of the air," Mr. Nicholls added.</p>
        <p>• The Bench, o4er which Mr. J. Rymer Young presided, sentenced prisoner to a month's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>BAULKED OF HIS BEER.</head>
        <p>BAULKED OF HIS BEER.</p>
        <p>Forcible Expression Of Displeasure Costs Fifty Shillings.</p>
        <p>William Wright, of no settled abode, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Thurs- day, before Mr. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), Mr. A. J. Jolley and Dr. Naden, with being drunk and disorderly in , Market-street on Wednesday, and with breaking a pane of glass valued at 30s. in the door of the Vine Tavern. P.C. Cameron told the Court how he arrested prisoner when he was being chased up Market- street, after leaving the Vine Tavern. The Chief Constable said that prisoner went into the Vine Tavern at 8.30 on the night in question and asked for beer. The beer was drawn, but. it was then noticed that prisoner was drunk, and the drink was consequently withheld. This annoyed prisoner, and he Went and deliberately smashed a window. Prisoner, who said he was sorry, was fined 20s. on the first charge, and ordered to Pay 30s. damages on the second. The alternative was 14 days in the first instance, and 21 days in the second.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>" TEETOTALER'S " TRIP.</head>
        <p>THE " TEETOTALER'S " TRIP.</p>
        <p>Pugilistic Carter Who Forgot HimseH</p>
        <p>in The Dock.</p>
        <p>John Foster, 1, Mersey-street, a. carter, and Martin McGinn, a newsvendqr, of 34, Rose and Crownitreet, were charged at the War- rington Police Court on Thursday, with fighting in Bewsey-street on Wednesday. Foster was further charged with assaulting Albert Dutton, a vanman in the employ of the Cheshire 'Lines Railway Company. The charge against McGinn was withdrawn at the outset.</p>
        <p>Albert Dutton gave evidence as to seeing Foster attack McGinn, who merely defended himself from Foster's ferocity. As McGinn appeared to be in danger of being badly hurt, witness interfered, with the result that a quarter of an hour later, Foster waylaid him and made a. savage assault on him, banging him against a wall and causing , his head to be badly cut. It was stated in the police evidence that Foster was drunk.</p>
        <p>Foster indignantly denied being drunk, stating that he was teetotal. The Chief Constable said that Foster was "a scrapper all. over." At the time of the strike he was one of the most troublesome and noisy. McGinn and Dutton were both perfectly sober on the evening of the assault. Prisoner: They were both of them drunk, as well as me.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: A minute ago you said you were teetotal! The Chairman (Mr. J. Rymer Young): Well, Poster, I think you are scarcely fit to be loose, And you will be secluded for one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>BOY'S LAPSE.</head>
        <p>BOY'S LAPSE.</p>
        <p>Lenient Treatment At The Hands 0</p>
        <p>Magistrates.</p>
        <p>At a Juvenile Court, before • the Borough Bench (Mr. J. Rymer Young presiding), on Thursday, a Blackburn lad was charged with the theft of an American dollar piece, the Property of Hannah Cook Evidence was given to the effect that the boy (who admitted the theft) came. to Warrington five or six weeks ago and was found a job at the Whitecross Works by a man who put him up for the night on the evening of his arrival. He stayed the week with this man and then cleared off without paying anything. He re- peated the process at several other houses, and finally stole the dollar from the place where he was -lodging in Pig-hill, and exchanged it for a watch. The watch and coin had both been recovered. Altogether, the lad owed £2 108. to the various people he had stayed with since he came to Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Father's Offer.</head>
        <p>Father's Offer.</p>
        <p>The boy's father, a respectable-looking elderly man, was present in Court, and when invited to make a statement, said he was very sorry for what had happened, and promised to look after the boy carefully if the magistrates would be lenient with him, and to pay all the debts he had contracted.</p>
        <p>Asked if he had had any previous trouble with prisoner, the father replied that he had on one other occasion, but the boy was not then taken to Court. The Chief Constable stated that the father was a very respectable man and there was ro</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Fewer Vagrants—Owing To Growing Scarcity Of Labour.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Linaker, jun., presided at Tuesdas'g meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians at Dutton. AN IRISHMAN'S ILLNESS.</p>
        <p>The Relieving Officer reported the admission into the House of an Irishman, suffering from appendicitis. Some question was raised as to whether the Board was liable in respect to the patient. The Relieving Officer mentioned that - the Irishman was not under the English Health Insurance Act. He was destitute at the time he was admitted for the operation, even if he had some money in the bank. It was stated that the House Committee bad the matter in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LOSS ON BOOT CONTRACT.</head>
        <p>LOSS ON BOOT CONTRACT.</p>
        <p>An item in the House Committee minuteg was to the effect that the contractor for boots had written to say that he had lost .13 on hig contract and asked the Board if they were willing to refund that amount. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>REASON FOR LESS VAGRANCY.</head>
        <p>REASON FOR LESS VAGRANCY.</p>
        <p>The • House. Committee also reported that - there was a decrease in vagrancy, and that it. was due to the war, which had enabled men, to get work who would otherwise be unem- ployed. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>THANKS</head>
        <p>THANKS</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Lister-Kaye wrote thanking the Board for the vote of sympathy accorded him in the loss of his wife.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MONEY MATTERS.</head>
        <p>MONEY MATTERS.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote sanc- tioning the increase to £3O of Dr. (Maxwell's salary, and also the payment of £lO to Mr. 3-- Pearson for extra work done during the ill-. ness of Mr. Warburton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL;</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL;</p>
        <p>Motor Conveyance Advocated For The Isolation Hospital.</p>
        <p>Mr. Antwis occupied the chair at the fort- nightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural pis' trict Council at Dutton on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>TO HELP THE BELGIANS. The Cheshire County Central Committee wrote asking the Council's support for county scheme to help the Belgians. The Council decided to form a committee t° go, into the matter and organise a scheme of support. - The following were appointed a committee, on the proposition of Mr. C. E. Linaker, The Chairman, Vice-Chairman, and Messr6; Chadwick, Woodward, Wright, Mclntyre . Pilling.</p>
        <p>HOARDINGS "AN ABOMINATION." A complaint was received from a resident near the Latcjiford Swing Bridge to the effect that. advertisement hoardings had been erected in front of her house and that they were all abomination. • On the proposition of Mr. George Pilling it - was decided to write the Manchester S'l Canal Company, protesting in the .matter. 4</p>
        <p>SCARCITY OF 'WATER. was received f the scarcityiovedfr°il i°sePh ( Walker, t letter The writer stated that they had been without for five weeks. He had complained at Run- corn, and when the tank from which ther should have got a supply was examined, was nearly empty. He asked the Rural Court-, cil to communicate With Runcorn on the'_, , subject. water. LOCOMOTIVE AND SOME DAMAGE. It was reported by the Frodsham ParochiAl Committee that a locomotive belonging to Yr; R. Allen, of Widnes, had damaged the roa' and culvert at Marsh Green, and that instroc- tions had been given for the necessary repairs; They had decided that 111 r. Allen be notifiel that they proposed to charge him with the cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>AN APPOINTMENT.</head>
        <p>AN APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>The Matron of the Isolation Hospital re' ported the appointment of Miss Phyllis HaYOB. as probationer.</p>
        <p>HORSE VERSUS MOTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward, reporting on the activities of the Finance Committee, said that it-wg's time the Isolation Hospital had a motor ambo: lance, instead of the work of removal patients being done by horse traction. was an unsatisfactory and expensive systell!; They had two horses and both were in 101" condition. The. present system was costing,, them more than if they had two motors. I" was time the Isolation Hospital Committee were instructed to take up the question. Mr. Linaker said he agreed with the theorP but the present waif rather a bad time to WI a motor ambulance. Mr. Pilling said that a motor ambulance would cost ,tl2O. Mr. Woodward pointed out that a treniery dous amount of money was being regular 15 spent on the present system. Mr. Linaker-: When your motor isn't soric. ing, it doesn't want feeding. (Laughter.) It was resolved to leave the matter over I°' thorough investigation and preliminary n°ll- sideration by a committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Wastin..! and Weak Chest,</head>
        <p>Wastin..! and Weak Chest,</p>
        <p>Boy's Health Restored bY kr Dr. Cassell's Tablets ,42-</p>
        <p>Mrs. Russell, 8, Par- ringford-road,, West Ham-lane. Stratford, London, E., says: "My son, Harry, had in, fluenza, about two years ago, and after that he was always, ailing. Then a nasty cough came on and he began to lose flesh badly. Ile had hod rheumatism, too, and his heart was affected. We had good advice for Harry. but there was no inlllrl ment, 20 we got him into hospital. He s'ecijo'id better when he came out; but soon all the..,to symptoms retnrned. Then we sent Win the country, bet when he came home letber. soon !as ill as ever, if not worse. It AvOZ Inca we tried Dr. Cassell's Tablets, and they ne':oid Harry from the first. He grew Istrolig fore. active, and now he is better than ever bc He has no cough, and his heart ceems sound."</p>
        <p>11 Dr. CisseLE Tablet Dr. Cassell's Tablets are a genuine and tested. roetacer, for all farms of nerve or bodily weakness )°, ,t.c. Young. Compound.d r nerve-,,utrients aria indisputably proved efficacy, they are the ree modern home treatment for re NERVOUS BREAKDOWN KIDNEY DISEA4 NERVE PARALYSIS SPINAL PARALYSIS INFANTILE PARALYSIf NEURASTHENIA NERVOUS DEBILITY • PWM6I NTAAADOLL: :T-INATIENUCAS WASTING RI I :Di ITI 61 (15:11- irs:s SLEEPLESSNESS ANEMIA • pVRITEAriLATEuXgroI'EcAY, doel Specially valuable for Nursing Nfoth“s. the Critical Periods of Life. Chemists a.,a krice&amp; all parts of the world sell Dr. Cassell's Tablets.t, wet, 101/2d., 1/11/2, and 2/9—the 219 sizo heingot be oe°,,ii economical. A Free Trial Supply w" pen • to you on receipt of r.ame and cithireas and tsfEsd° etamps for postage and packing. Addre,s: 0 a., Ltd., 41E, Cbetter-road, 3liittcbc6t</p>
        <p>:1)1, • Master Ru ss,' I i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>CARDINAL-ARCHBISHOP'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>CARDINAL-ARCHBISHOP'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>Impressive Services !Nark Opening of New</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>St. Benedict's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Benedict's Church.</p>
        <p>RED-LETTER DAYS IN LOCAL CATHOLICISM.</p>
        <p>Saturday, Sunday and Monday were red-letter 'days in the history of Roman Catholicism in Warrington, for not only did they witness the Opening . of a fine new local church, but they, also saw a visit' from the only English Cardinal—His Eminence Cardinal Francis Bourne, Archbishop of Westminster—from a ..distinguished prelate, the Archbishop of idverpool, from the Bishops .of Shrewsbury and Menevia, and from many Other church .dignataries. It was the first time „since the Reformation, which was 'brought about .in the far-off reign of Henry the Eighth, that a Cardinal of the Church of ~Roine had visited the town in his ecclesiasti- cal capacity. Only once before—so far as his- tory records-7in the almost four htmdred Years since the Church of England sup- planted the Church of Rome as the State Church, had a Cardinal visited , Warrington, viz., when the late Cardinal Manning came to the town, in an unofficial capacity, to speak au temperance.</p>
        <p>The new church which the Cardinal came to Warrington expressly to open rears its lofty and graceful campanile in Orford-lane, and is dedicated to St. Benedict, the founder of the Order of Benedictine Monks,' and an eminent Saint of the Catholic Church, who died in the Year 543, having lived the monastic life for Some half century. The Church of St. • 13enedict is a striking example of the Byzan- tine style of architecture.; and must be num- bered among the few buildings of outstand- ing beauty and note in the town. It is built ef St. Helens brick, relieved with white stone bands and the campanile, which stands, at , the west end, and will form a landmark for miles around; is surmounted by a gilt cross. , There is an impressive air of loftiness and general spaciousness about the interior, Which is not yet quite complete, inasmuch as the tiled floor has yet to be laid and 'other final touches apPlied. A number` of stained glass windows have already been installed, and others are to take the places of the plain glass panels which temporarily fill the orifices. By the time the . church is quite completed it will have cost a figure estimated at sofnewhere between ~26,000 and „tB,OOO, and it was designed by Mr. Matthew Ronan, a „, • well-known Liverpool architect, who has Planned a number of ecclesiastical buildings 111) and down the country. The Cardinal, attended by his secretary and chaplain and other members of his house- hold, arrived in Warrington on. Saturday evening, and was privately met at Bank Quay Station by the Rev. Father T. 0. Swarbreck, who has charge of the new church: From the station his Eminence motored to the .Town where he was accorded a municipal reception by the Mayor and Mayoress, and Where he was respectfully welcomed by about fifteen hundred members of the Catholic community and by .many 'members of other religious denominations. • -,. On Sunday morning his Eminence, wearing the famous Red Robes denoting the Cardinalate, - attended in state the opening service of the new chtdch, which service, like all the others held 'during the day and on Monday, was most impressive in character, beautiful music sung by a specially trained choir blending with the symbolic ritual which is , ever the marked note in Catholic public wor- :•hip, and adding to the dignity and magnifi- ,An rA of the proceedings. In the afternoon the Cardinal addressed some three hundred nantembers of the local and outlying branches the h 0 Catholic Young Men's Society, and in xtelroening' he preached. a striking sermon. the s n "Y morning he attended the last of oPening service in the new ehureiitl</p>
        <p>After afternoon service on Sunday: his Bmi- Trii7ce. Paid a brief visit to the Lord Derby War "4-"Pital ..at Winwick, and talked with a. "tuber of the wounded warriors quartered there his and with various members of the surgical, medical and nursing staffs. During Stay in Warrington the Cardinal was the RuOst of Mr. and Mrs. H. Percy Densham, at Cuerdon Hall, where a suite of rooms were Placedt the disposal of himself and his uite</p>
        <p>heart than that, in all we undertake, we shall be of service to the community at large. Once more I thank your Worship most sincerely for the kindness of your welcome and take this opportunity of assuring you of the great pleasure it affords me to find myself in the midst of so representative a company." His Eminence, accompanied by the Mayor and .Mayoress, then passed on to the lawn, and after staying a few moments, gave the com- pany, who knelt -reverently, his benediction, and took his leave, together with his retinue, first 'signing the visitors' book kept in the Town Hall. Some few minutes after . the Cardinal's departure Sir Arthur and L ady Crosfield arrived, being given a cordial recep- tion by the company, among whom they mingled freely, receiving many congratulations upon the honour recently bestowed upon the popular Liberal candidate by the King. • The catering was in the capable hands of Mr. and Mrs. G. Mitchell, and in addition to the music supplied by the orchestra in the vestibule—under the conductorship of Mr. T. Blake----the League of the Cross Band also played.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>MAYORAL RECEPTION,</head>
        <p>MAYORAL RECEPTION,</p>
        <p>Itepreseniathe Company Gives Ills</p>
        <p>Eminence Greeting. eBlis Eminence Cardinal Bourne made his .t Public appearance in the town at an al sco reception given by the Mayor and aYoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peter Peacock), .111,11 the beantiful garden at the rear of. the Own. Hall,' on Saturday evening. His Worship ..."(1 issued an open iaitation to all members the Catholic community and others to be vresent, and the result was that the company 144s8etabled to greet His Eminence numbered anY hundreds' and was representative of all P:llaBes of local life, well-known men in muni- t'Pal and commercial circles, as well as those 14).11nected with the Anglican Churches and ouconformist places of worship in the town setnbling to greet the titular head of the 'ai.eat Roman Church in Britain. Those who '4ended were received in the vestibule of the Hall by the Mayor and Mayoress and 4,"Bed through the Council Chamber on to the ti:a•eious and well-kept lawn. As they entered their ears were greeted by the sweet ti.h..,a-uls discoursed by a small string orchestra, tli before they left light refreshments were nsed from a buffet situate on the lawn. •th.l-110 Cardinal and his retinue, which included Bishops of Shrewsbury and Menevia, .thrwed after all the guests had assembled, !ir coming being witnessed by a large crowd, thronged 'the 'approach to the Town Hall. %le Cardinal, who was not wearing the lam- Red Hat and scarlet habitaments, but was tt,sed in his archiepiscopal robes, was re- by the Mayor and Mayoress in the 4461 1113 Chamber, and with them, and his eudants, passed on to the stone terrace tll`l,,(li,ng down to the lawn. On the terrace to-Th',laYur addressed a few words of welcome</p>
        <p>• THE MAYOR' § GREETING. _Fe In the flame of Warrington," he said, "1 p11,41..ti1Y greet Your Eminence. I feel extremely thaud that :Warrington has been honoured by ttoe visit of a prelate so distinguished in: the ielan. Catholic Church, and I feel 'sure that fela. Present equally appreciate the honour. I th.it I could not allow this occathon to - _ 44.4 without asking Your Eminence to kindly e Your permission to allow me, as Chief strate of this old borough, to formally e YOU in the name of Warrington and 'l.ingtonians. irreSpective of creed. party, Will thing else. (Hear, hear.) This day 14.08 t stand out in my memory as one of the Lkitt .„ eventful during my eventful Mayor- (Applause.)</p>
        <p>111 E CARDINAL'S GRACIOUS-REPLY. reply, the Cardinal, in a voice of fine Ntbre, said: "It would not be fitting were I at • e o °lle t express to your Worship my loos, i11...1' grateful thanks for the kindness of your 44"Itation to come amongst you this evening, flse for the extremely kind words you have in my regard. I have come, as you ath "' to take part in a further development .4. 40o_ozgst those with whom I am most closely y,n7ted in your town. In coming m ong blaOr that special purpose, it is an among Oot lire to me to feel that I have a greeting, colt eolY from those among -Whom .1 specially "tta.te, but also from you, sir, as (Thief Magis- the e of the -borough, speaking in the name of wh; whole town. I trust that the special work have come to do in your midst will be algoee,rviee, not only to my community, but '4l.easT the whole borough, because there is no or dearer wish which we .hasa at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>OPENING SERVICE.</head>
        <p>OPENING SERVICE.</p>
        <p>Pontifical ffiuh Mass Snnu In</p>
        <p>Presence 01 Cardinal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WAR AND CONSCIENCE OF MANKIND.</head>
        <p>WAR AND CONSCIENCE OF MANKIND.</p>
        <p>"All the trampings of Germany's legions, all the thunderings of her batteries, cannot drown the cry of one little Belgian child. Such is the verdict of that neutral Power, the great American nation. The conscience of mankind has been roused, and the world stands applalled at horrors previously unknown. in history." In these words, the Rev: Herbert Vaughan opened his sermon at the Sunday „morning celebration of Pontifical -High Mass—the actual opening service in the Church. He went on ,to say that one of the most. appalling features of the war had been the destruction of churches. In this very land, we had ruins of churches—relics, of dark, ages when sacrilegious ruffians tore down the temple of God. All up and down the country, in every town and village and hamlet, old, empty homes in which Jesus once dwelt were to be found. ,</p>
        <p>A New and Worthy Home for the Master. It was an occasion for rejoicing that a new and worthy home had been found for the Master. "I feel," said the preacher, "as I look round on the worshippers gathered here this morning—and we must all feel the same—that if the old ruins of this country could speak, they would join in the glorious chorus of our praise. For centuries they have been wit- nesses to the fame that has been now they are to be witnesses to a new fame. The history' of Catholicism in Warrington, from the time of</p>
        <p>the Reformation, right away to the present day, is a glorious history—a history reflecting the highest credit upon the noble sons of St. Benedict. Already they have erected in this town two churches, and to-day we are gathered together to celebrate the opening of a third church dedicated to St. Benedict. The time, indeed, is opportune for the erection of a new church.</p>
        <p>One Striking Effect of the War.</p>
        <p>The war has had one striking effect. It is turning the thoughts of men to their God. It has , brought men to their knees. It has been well said that war brings men into touch with reality. During a prolonged peace, the minds of men are taken up with trivialities. A war comes, with grim reality, forcing a man to look death in the face and to the world beyond, and to realise what are the things that matter. Even the daily Press has become reverent. There is no paper in this country to-day that dare scoff at religion. In these 'times there is hope of better things. Men are beginning to see what a grand -thing the Catholic religion is. To restore the earlier glory of pre-Reformation times is impossible of immediate achievement, but this new church gives to the people of the locality a golden opportunity. It brings the sacrament of the Holy Man within the reach of everybody. The present is a time for heart-searching. Search and examine Your hearts. Are we not somewhat indifferent? Is it not a fact that we take 'the Real Presence somewhat for granted? Do we really strive to find .out what it really means?</p>
