        <p>FATHER T. 0. SWARBRECK. Rector of St. Benedict's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>"Wake Up, Catholics !"</head>
        <p>"Wake Up, Catholics !"</p>
        <p>"We have seen written up and lown the country, 'Wake up, England!' And this war has awakened her. feel inclined to say, 'Wake up, Catholics!' People outside the Church are waking up to the reality of Catholicism; do not let us be behind-hand within the Church. -Let us be assiduous. The soldier who drills only when he feels inclined to is a poor ,soldier; and the Catholic who prays only when he feels inclined to is a poor Catholic. The eyes of the people of this country are upon us, and we are Christ's own monica. We must pray and rally round the Blessed Master. We must visit Him often in this beauti- ful home that has been provided for Him by the sacrifices and living faith of this generous congregation.</p>
        <p>At the Sunday morning service there were present in addition to His Eminence Cardinal Bourne, Archbishop of Westminster (who opened the building), the Right Rev. Mon- signor Jackman (assisting His Eminence), and the Right Rev. Abbot Smith, of Ampleforth (who sang the Pontifical High Mass); the Very Rev. Father Whittle, of St. Alban's, Warrington (assistant priest to the Abbot), the Very Rev. Canon P. L. Bugging, of Bel- mont Priory, Hereford (deacon), the Rev. Father W. T. Rylance, of St. Mary's, Brown edge (sub-deacon), the Rev. A. J. MacDonald, of St. Ann's Priory, Edgehill (assistant priest), the Rev. Father B. Gibbons, of St. Mary's, Leyland, and the Rev. Father J. W. Baines, of The Priory, Merthyr Tydvil (assistant deacims), and Mr. John Fishwick, of Liver- pool (who acted as M.C.). The preacher was the Rev. Herbert Vaughan. There were also present in the sanctuary: His Grace the Arch- bishop of Liverpool. attended by the. Rev. Father Miller, of Woolston; the Right Rev. theiLord Bishop of Menevia. attended by the Rev. Charles Swarbreck, of Ditton; the Right Rev. the Lord Bishop of Shrewsbury, attended by the Rev. Father Tallon, of Latchford. The Mayor and Mayorcss (Aldprlnan and Mrs. P.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>Peacock) attended in state, and the congrega- tion also included Sir Arthur Crosfield, Bart., and Lad, Crosfield, Mr. and Mrs. H. Percy Densham (of Cuerden Hall, with whom the Cardinal stayed), Mrs. Nicholson (of Thelwall Hall), Mr. and Mrs. A. do C. Palmiter (Lymm), and Mr. John McElligott (Cardiff). Captain Matthew Honan, of Liverpool—the architect— is fighting in the Dardanelles with the South Lancashires.</p>
        <p>united together in the great Catholic faitl with the same end in view.</p>
        <p>Greater Work To Be Done.</p>
        <p>The young men assembled in St. 13enedict's Schoolyard and proceeded down Orford-lane, headed by the band of the League of the Cross and the banner of St. Benedict. His Eminence was again attended by a military guard of honour.</p>
        <p>HIS EMINENCE ON FALSE PROPHETS.</p>
        <p>The Cardinal concluded with an appeal to the members of the society each to carry out with zeal the work that God had given him to do. 1 "There is," •he said, "a still greater work to be done in the days to come, when we shall carry with us all the hopes and aspirations of those who have watched over your magnifi- cent society. The branches of the Young Men's Society represented at the service were as follows: St. Mary's, Buttermarket-street ; Our Lady's, Latchford; St. Alban's and St. Benedict's, War- rington; St. Mary's and • St. John's, Earlestown ; St. Mary's, Widnes; St. Michael's, Ditton. Many of the members wore sashes and medals.</p>
        <p>had no evil intent; he might be a false prophet although he did not consciously conceal his wolfish character in the cicithing of a sheep. He might be misleading mankind because he was, himself, misled; he might be misled through no fault of his own and he might be carrying on his misleading influence• without any sense of the terrible harm he was doing.. Men with insufficient knowledge who yet attempted to pass judgment on this thing or that without any, or proper, preparation for their task became false prophets. in the' daily newspapers, in the magazines and in the rt, views they were constantly meeting with the writings of men who most certainly were false prophets, though very often they were quite unconscious of the fact. To illustrate his- point the Cardinal referred to the recent scare articles in a certain widely-read newspaper t, the effect that there were shortly to be born into the community many eltifdren of unmar- ried mothers, and went on to point out that the writer alleged that it was the ecclesiastical laa=s which would make the lot of those mothers and their offspring harder to bear, whereas the fact was that it was the civil law, for. the ecclesiastical law allowed of legitimation. , Another Misrepresentation..</p>
        <p>Another eminently respectable paper, "The' Guardian," which was the •main' organ of the Established Church in this country, the other day pointed out that there were in India; a very large number of Catholic missionaries who were of German origin and who had had to be interned, and added that owing to, the pro- German bias of the Holy See it was pratticall;• impossible for any Englishman to obtain an, prominent position in the Catholic Church i: India, or that, at least, no loyal Englishmal would agree to undertake work under sucl conditions. All that was put down quit seriously, but there was not a single word o: truth in the artiele from' beginning to end. L was known that some of the missionaries in India had caused' considerable anxiety to the Government, but. they- were the German Lutheran missionaries and not the Catholic missionaries. That was the first mistake. The second mistake was contained in the statement that there was no chance of any Englishman obtaining any important position in the Catho- lic Church in India, on account of the supposed pro-German bias of the Holy See. Scarcity of British Priest Missionaries.</p>
        <p>The truth was that the Holy See woUld only be too glad to find intelligent Catholic mission- aries not only for England, hut also for any other part of the world. Unfortunately, how- ever, there were not enough priests of British ,origin even for the work at home, or for the British colonies, let alone India. The result was that the Holy See had had to appeal to religious organisations of various kinds without reference to nationality, and those who had been found for the mission field had done their work •magnificently. The whole of that article in " The Guardian" was written by a man who knew absolutely nothing of the subject; he was most distinctly a false prophet. Some time ago in London, he (the Cardinal) had to speak on the supposed silence of the Holy See, and he then called attention to a certain pamphlet which had been circulated all over England; indeed, it had been sold in thousands. It was written by Francis .Tyrrell. The day after he (His Eminence) had spoken the author wrote a letter to "The Globe," saying that his explana- tion would not be accepted by thousands of English and American, Catholics. The speech was made on the Sunday the letter appeared on the Monday! How could the writer possibly have learned the opinions of Catholics in America and in various parts of England on something that had only been said the day before.</p>
        <p>Unintentionally False Prophets. The truth was that very often well-meaning</p>
        <p>warbreck's</p>
        <p>men were compelled, by the exigencies of their profession, to write on all kinds of subjects with insufficient time for sufficient preparation, the result being that they became false prophets, misleading those who read their written words. There were many men writing in the daily Press who had lost all hold of Revealed Reli- gion, and who looked at everything from the materialistic point of view. His Eminence mentioned, as instances, the advocates of easier divorce and of the limitation of families— writers who, he said, were spreading abroad in the world, most insidiously, false doctrines, which were destructive of human society and family life and were contrary to the law of God. Another. set of writers to be avoided were those who accepted the Revelation up to a certain point, but had their own special views and phrases whereby they whittled away the effect of God's teaching to make it conform with their own special point of view. Again, they often read in the newspapers and maga- zines words which were based upon the prin- ciple that one .religion was as good as another; that it did not matter in the least what a man believed so long as his conduct was right. People who talked like that were confusing two things which were entirely different—the facts that a man could be true to his conscience and that God had given a,Divine Revelation which a man was bound to accept as soon as he learned it.</p>
