      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Music.</head>
        <p>The Music.</p>
        <p>The musical setting of the Mass in use was by Max Filke, and it was sung by the com- bined choirs of St. Benedict's and St. Mary's (Buttermarket-street). The bass solo in the Credo was taken by Mr. John Bannon. The ,Rev., J. E. Turner's "Ave Pater Sanctissime " was rendered as the offertory was taken. Mr. John Eckersley conducted the choir at all the services, and Mr. W. Turner presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>THE CARDINAL'S GUARD OF HONOUR. As he entered the •church to- attend this ser- vice the Cardinal passed between a military guard of honour, and the priestly procession passed up the nave to the strains of Sadler's setting of " Ecce sacerdos magnus." The -pro-, cession was headed by. acolytes, after whom came the Abbot of Ampleforth, arrayed in cope and mitre and attended by several priests. Preceded by the bearer of a crucifix, the Car- dinal followed, robed in red, his long train being borne by two sUrpliced boys. The Archbishop of Liverpool and the Bishops of Shrewsbury and Menevia, ivho wore purple robes, also took part in the procession. Car- dinal Bourne was immediately conducted to a throne on the left of the high altar, the bishops taking up their positions on the right. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Ref-Ore the close, of the service the Rev. T. 0. Swarbreck announced that Captain Ronan, the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>People Who Consciously or Unconsciously Mislead.</head>
        <p>People Who Consciously or Uncon- sciously Mislead.</p>
        <p>There was an exceedingly large congregation at the evening service, it having been an- nounced that the Cardinal was to preach. Like its predecessors the service was most impres- sive in character.</p>
        <p>His Erninerre arrived just after the Service of the Rosaiy had been gone through, and proceeded to a canopied seat in the sanctuary,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CARDINAL LEAVING THE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>THE CARDINAL LEAVING THE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>architect of the church, was now serving his country in a very dangerous zone at the Dar- danelles. He had particularly asked for the prayers of the congregation, and all present knew what a debt they owed him. The preacher at the morning service is the nephew of the celebrated Father .Bernard Vaughan.</p>
        <p>being accompanied by a stately procession, the People kneeling as he and his retinue passed up the aisle.</p>
        <p>THE CARDINAL'S ADDRESS</p>
        <p>Speaking from the steps of the High Altar, the Cardinal said that there was, he thought, nothing that men prided themselves so much upon as the independence of their judgment. They claimed to think and judge for themselves and to arrive at opinions solidly formed on evidence which they themselves had considered, and as to the sufficiency of which they had assured themselves. Nowhere in the world, he supposed, did men pride themselves more upon the independence of their judgment than in this country. The Englishman carried the independence of his opinion and of his judg- ment to such an extent that, unlike the people of most other nations, he applied that indepen- dence even in religious matters. With the Catholic there was no room for opinion, but only for whole-hearted belief in the authority of his Church and the teaching of the Man of God Himself, but the ordinary Englishman who did not accept the Catholic Faith claimed that he was independent in religious matters and that he could accept and reject what was pro- posed for his belief, or any authority, as ap- peared best to himself after he had considered the matter. They talked of independent judg- ment, yet, he supposed, that if they were honest with themselves they must admit that it was very seldom, i,ndeed, that they were able to form a really independent judgment. Day by day matters of all kinds were brought to their notice.</p>
        <p>The Danger of Taking Opinions from Others. They were told about things that had hap- pened in the world recently or long ago and on the presentation of those things by other people, who might or might not be competent to present them, they took to themselves opin- ions of every conceivable kind and then gave them forth as their own considered judgments. From the fact that they were exposed continu- ally to the risk of taking their facts-a,nd views from other people and of forming therefrom opinions which ultimately Might influence their conduct, so were they liable, all through their lives, to. be misled by those whom the Divine Saviour had called false prophets. These false</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>YOUNG MAN'S DAY.</head>
        <p>THE YOUNG MAN'S DAY.</p>
        <p>Cardinal Bourne's Appreciation 01 Tlic</p>
        <p>Calibre 01 British Youth.</p>
        <p>The Cardinal, in the course of his address to the members of the Catholic Young Men's Society, on Sunday afternoon, said it wa- always a pleasure to visit the North of Eng- land, and to see the evidence of the splendid' work being done by the society. But it was par- ticularly gratifying at the present time. There had never ben a moment in the memory of any of them, never a time in the history of the Catholic Church of England, that had been so closely associated as the present time with the young man. If ever there was a day that be- longed to the young manhood of the country, it was to-day. It was a moment when young men were ableto show to all the world that the Catholic could set an example of courage and self?-sacrifice such as many of them would not have given them credit for a few months ago. it seemed to many that the young manhood was so taken up with pleasure and amusement, that the more important things of life were being forgotten. They doubted that, without the compulsion of the law, the young men would do what was expected of them, and take up arms in defence of civilisation. (Gallant Catholics.</p>
        <p>Never, perhaps, in the whole history of our race, had its young manhood shown itself to better advantage--more willing to sacrifice everything, even life, in the defence of King and country. Catholics had been second to none in the work already accomplished and in the readiness with which they had responded to their country's call. So far as he could un- derstand, the proportion of their Catholic young men who had gone to the Front—whe</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>s • •-..c':.•.‘...'•::.•t,•,,:-,:,:;if'?'•:11•2....,•P,'.:.*.*,•,.::::..-;-:,,m,•-•.,.-••.,-:..---.,--:•.. . • . . . . , , .•, , , • . , „. , ... • , . • - „ &lt; . • c ..z.,4,?!'.,',•-•,.;,i.5........ t•;.Yl';';''''''.'•o"••:::Z.'•':.,,;::-.„, , .Z..f • .',1`.«4.:•,,....tti:'11. / . ... , . ;.; ifAtlr::,7.• .., ..... • ••.. ' ~ , , ::::. .-, ..., . • :,.....,-•.....,:i:Eiv:•.c. i '-gi,,,.:4,--"7. --i,,5:.7,,,. 1 •I . ‘ t •5i...,..•}!„,:a4.4.4,-,.•.:. •', . ;4 .-• ', ' ' . , \ ::;.,':).-:;;-•;"-i,47',# 1 . ' ' . ...,,,,,:.::....',...: 1. € ;•• ''' t ''. I.' . °, ..,,,' i: - ::.:-..'s''. It 4.'.4,...i‘:t..• '': ' ~'‘ • 'vk, ~::•.',..-.....-. . ''''' ' ..:',fc.::''•:-"q'f'l'..''''''''. ' .„ I! I ' ' "------- ~._ .1. ~ ~ . ;:i , „,:. •••••!•-,.„.:,., .:::. ...,. s*, i ...., , • , , i ~ , ~ • , . ~,A. ..:...f. ~ - , , t ... ..... .•• • ..„..., ,4:. -;-, 1 ' 1 I . 1,0, ~ . • x•k., ,k :k. •,',"): ..k: ,'''''. . ............... —,-- .., „ .... ~,,. • .". '''''' '''' , ''',, '... . ' -.. ' . , ';'..,' ~. '•;. ::;.. --' ' ,;'' '',. ' , 4: ''' ' s 77, , % f • '''''''.."•;* '''N'`.' ..' . ..,' '‘.t.....'• .s!t :•,,. ~A i's. - . ~-..‘, ,tt* . ..-:„.,'...... .;.4.w.....,..-z.,!,:-...',.., - - • ~, .55.-.,,, .',:',,. .ft „ ...,......,;...., -: '.-'fifFt- -.- '..1-tr,.,..:,:,,,k,. ~. ..;,...-' sf,&lt;, i,,.. ~. 5 ~.:': ...44 ' .. , 5 , . ,:.k....,. . ,-; -.„,,t. ~, , ~...,.... .4,..::.:.:,, t-,.. ..,_ ~.,... ... 5 w igO• ,' .t i ~,,.. ~ ,i,: .. ki,...Zti • -"'"•7.,741 - ' :?, ....,:.,,..,,, ...„,z ..... " ' '..,'2,-", " 41,1" ' •,''''f''',,, 11it504,...4.+1,141111"/ -z, , ' t . &lt;... i- . t....., I -&lt;—,:..71..,; , ,- -... • ' -' ~..-.--•---.,- , • ~..:441..., ?m • :. fie, -.;...,.• ~...e.....,.. ~....... ~ , ..... ._ -, , .::" • .F,.:7. , ,•,... .' ,?..:.);4.4. .', ' -.''' -,4.:‘,..,:., .77.,-,:-:•, ~.-••.:- .'..-'7*'t:".',. 777ria1m!'4...,..., - , -• . :,,,,,,, ~". ' * -,.-••••,.,' ••• :,„''',1‘...".i':..., . 't;' . - •'t.'. •-: .-- - ~.&lt; . " • ::.:. • ~..., :i•".•',':':" ' &lt;- ' '2.,;:-..;.•:••.•'..•,•...:.- -,:. ~• • ••••..•i•!. . •- . t4' 5.,' '. •,,,,,•••‘•:,'•i•'*N•;'.',...•,,,:z.:'4,•;,:•• •.' " ' ' „,,... . ,:•,,,,,:•,,,„,,.-,4",.z.,e,,i".-.,, ,•44-.• , : •'' . '''• '.....1...;,....',..,2:::.7'..„..,.::!.• '. • ~„ ~,,,......,-:-..,,,..1.;;;:i..,.iii...:.r.•.'''' '-'--.:',:' '' , •: ~ ‘....,',..:.',4&lt;.-..- • • • • . - ~. '-',:::. ::-,..,:vii:.".&amp;.:,',,,-....:::-.:'?...--.,:.-..,':4•,....--,-,-.• ~....,...- -,- ' ,:-,.&lt; ~,,,kki‘t,kopmt.„*. : -. '''• :,..:.,:s ‘,.,i....4-4&gt;:,,-.4.zr. ~,,,,,.-.5.•.-,:::r:-........,-.:,...:„:,,...:,,,.:.:....-.;.:.,....:-.:••:•.:,•,.- • . • •;'..,s . ,::: ..•::.,,,,,,•-'.•?,.•..•-:•,;.4..,\&lt;, ,-.,,,.....4•••••.•---,•,•:.,.., -.3 ,t :..,,,,,,,,,,f 41 t-....il,„;:.:?iotktisi.i,Ati'QT.,:iii.oavi*;:.'..,•::.::::,:`..---.....,.: . . .1tj.•..:,:,','....,..1-'','::,,,,':,.':::::.--.". '- - - . .. , ..., .. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>NEW CHURCH.</head>
        <p>THE NEW CHURCH.</p>
        <p>had shown what the Catholic spirit was capable of---stood unequalled by any other Church. And - the Catholic Young Men s Society, by holding the youths and men to- gether, had played 'an important part in the obtaining of such an honourable record, and such a spirit of earnestness. He thought the Catholic young men were to be heartily e.,n- gratulated on their splendid response. Within the past ten days, he had been talking to a young corporal who had won the V.C. and also to Sergeant O'Hagan. Both were Catholics. They were young Men who had used opportuni- ties that came their way, and what they had done because they had had the opportunities, thousands of others were prepared to do and would do, sh6ld the occasion arise. It should encourage Catholic young men, whether fighting abroad or carrying' on good work at home, to have such examples and to know they were all</p>
        <p>I .1111, 7 .111110, Mr</p>
        <p>prophets came to mankind speciously, in the clothing of sheep, but in reality, in their effect upon the people—though not by any means in their own intentions they were merely ravening wolves. To beware of false prophets was a warning which the Divine Lord had given to the whole world until the end of time; it was a warning against the innate tendency of human nature to take up without sufficient scrutiny opinions made by other people, and accept and adopt them as one's own and base one's conduct upon them. There were about them on every side those who were false pro- phets, who appeared to be men of learning and intelligence, and who all the while were leading men astray and destroying the work that the Saviour came down to the earth to do. .</p>
        <p>The False Prophets of the Press. A man. Might be a falße prophet although he</p>
        <p>.......... um ■• sr '...m. 7 WM, ft</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>More False Doctrines.</head>
        <p>More False Doctrines.</p>
        <p>They forgot and put out of court the fact that Christ came upon the earth to leach a definite doctrine, and sent forth His Apostles to teach something very definite indeed.' Once that definite doctrine was put before a man and he understood that God had given it, there could be no question whatever as to. his obligation' to accept it. Another false doctrine that was put about in various forms was that the whole of the revelation of Almighty God was contained in.l.he.Bible, and that the Bible was .left to the free interpretation of those who read it. That doctrine still .had a tremendous hold upon the people of this country and it was a doctrine which was abSolutely false—a false setting forth of What God had taught: He had never taught them that in the Bible they would find all the truth He had given to His people; still less that•the people should inter- pret it as they -themselves pleased. Frequently, ,in papers and magazines, they w,euld 'see it suggested that religion was a national matter, and if there was one thing that was absolutely clear it was that religion had nothing to do with nationality. Religion was super-national; over all nationalities, and yet they found that, their fellow-countrymen—the mass of them— found it impossible to accept •Lt ruler in spiritual matters who was nct an Briglitlman. That same theory was put forward .constantly in various English papers; it permeated the English /Apple and constituted one of the ob- stacles to their acceptance of the Apostolic See. Other people—writers and teachers—set before the world false and imperfect standards of con- duct; indeed, this was probably the most in- sistent and general form of false teaching that Catholics had to guard themselves against.</p>
        <p>The Cult of Materialism.</p>
        <p>Those people regarded business attainment, pleasure, self-satisfaction, the gratifying a ambition and the making of money as the only things that men need concern their he'd&amp; about. There was more of that sort of dOe- trine about before the war than there was at present. One great effect of that terrific awali-- ening of men's souls was to make them under- stand that there were things greater than the making of money and the satisfaction of ambi- tions. They had been led to remember that</p>
        <p>........ .. ... ... =re MIMI .1111 a WW W</p>
        <p>there was another world and that men must be prepared to sacrifice many things in order to obtain happiness hereafter. Again, there was that whole region of false prophecy connected with the Holy See; that region in which it was taken for granted that the Holy See must make mistakes and that the Holy Father must neces- sarily be wrong. Things were distorted and misrepresented, and when, at last; the truth was told no sort of reparation was made and no cancellation of the false• statements. He believed that the number of those who deliber- ately set out to mislead the world was very small, but there were many who mikled through error. In face of the false prophecies, the mis- statements which beset Catholics, their duties were plain, and the first one rested upon parents so to know and to practise their reli- gion that they might never be false prophets to theirehildren.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Parent and the School.</head>
        <p>The Parent and the School.</p>
        <p>Those Catholic parents who did not know their religion, who did not practise it, and who (lid not impress it on the minds of their chil- dren, were, by their conduct, leading their children to think that religion did not matter. Why was it that the Catholic Church—not only in this country, but everywhere—made such tremendous efforts to set up and maintain Catholic schools for Catholic children? It was because she was wary of the %false prophet— the false prophet in the school; because, in her opinion, there was not a sufficiency of relig'ous teaching given in the Council schools to give to Catholic children the full blessing of Jesus Christ.. Catholics could not expect to assist the poisonous influences around them if they never read a Catholic book or a Catholic newspaper; if they never took to themselves the opportunity of renewing their knowledge of religion and of opportunity of renewing their religion and of building up within themselves that spiritual sense which was such a great protection against error. There was one Supreme Protector against the dangers enumerated--the Div:ne Lord and Master, who, Himself, had warned them against that danger. The beautiful enoroii in which they were gathered that evening was set up to be a source of true prophecy for Him. Might it be a source of strength to those who sought to follow the Master; might it be a source of protection to all the people of the town; might it for many generations to come guard them and lead them ever nearer to' the throne of Jesus Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Cardinal's Blessing.</head>
        <p>The Cardinal's Blessing.</p>
        <p>At the close of his address His Eminence and the prelates and other church dignitaries in attendance upon him made a procession of the main aisles of the church, the C•Ardinal 1:--sow- ing his benediction upon the kneeling congre- gation as he passed among them. .Following his retinue came the procession of the Bishop of Shrewsbury and those in attendance upon him, His Grace also ,conferring his blessing upon the devout worshippers. The blue smoke of the burning incense issuing from the censers swung by the attendant acolytes made its way in fading wreaths to the vaulted roof, a peal of joyous music burst forth from the organ, and one of the most memorable services in the history of Warrington Catholic- ism was at an end. Cardinal Chats With His Guard of Honour.</p>
        <p>Before departing to lunch with the clergy who had taken part in the opening services, His Eminence spent several minutes chatting with the officers and men of his guard of honour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LADDER TO HEAVEN.</head>
        <p>LADDER TO HEAVEN.</p>
        <p>P,khop Of Menevia Dilates On Purpose</p>
        <p>Of Catholicism.</p>
        <p>At the service on Monday morning, Ponti- fical High Mass was sung by His Grace the Archbishop of Liverpool in the presence of His Eminence the Cardinal, and a striking sermon was preached by the Right Rev. the Lord Bishop of Menevia. ' A particularly large gathering included: Very Rev. T. A. Burge, Rev. Fathers J. P. Willson, C..E. Shepherd, T. A. Hind, S. Ryan, G. B. Cox, E. J. Tunieliffe, T. B. .McEvoy,, C. Ansell, J. .Dawson, J. B. McKinlay, 8.. Mawson, R Primavesi; Rev. Fathers T. 0. Swarbreck and .6.. C. Jackson (rector and assistant Priest of the new church), Right Rev. Monsignor Pinnington, Very Rev. Canon Snow, Very Rev. Dean Clarke, Very .Rev.e• Dean • Rothwell, Dean Newton. Rev, Fathers Charlton, Davidson, Murphy (of Sker- ton, Lancaster) and Murphy (Sacred Heart; Warrington); Rev. B.• L. Smith (Lee), and Rey, R. Downey (Mission House, London). Amongst the laity present were; The Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock), Hon. Mrs. - and Miss Stapletln- Bretherton, Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mrs., Miss, Mr. J. and Mrs. G. Ireland Blackburne (Hale), Miss Wolff (Liverpool), Miss Spencer (Liver- pool), Mr. and Mrs. L. A. Voisey, and six officers and fifty men from the Lord Derby War Hos- pital. Also a small number from " Oaklands," the Preston Brctok Military Hospital, were in attendance. In all parts the church was filled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>"THE LORD IS IN THIS'PLACE."</head>
        <p>"THE LORD IS IN THIS'PLACE."</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Menevia took his subject from the words in Genesis xiviii. 16: "Indeed the Lord is in this place." One evening as the sun went down the patriarch Jacob, who was travelling to Mesopotamia, being weary after his day's journey, laid himself down on the ground, and with a stone for a pillow fell asleep. And as he slept he had a wonderful vision. He saw, as it were, a long ladder, .which reached even to the very heavens; and God was leaning on the top of the ladder. And he saw angels in all their beauty ascending to Heaven and descending to earth. And the :patriarch, full of astonishment and reverence, exclaimed: "Indeed the Lord is in this place, and I knew it WA! now terrible is this place.</p>
        <p>It is no other than the house of God and the gate of Heaven." Church. Beauty and Man In Street.</p>
        <p>That s;i'as a beautiful figure of the Catholic Church; because the Catholic Church was a ladder, reaching to Heaven, by which their prayers and petitions ascended to Heaven cand by which God's grace and blessings de- scended upon them.. And, what was more, it could be most truthfully said of that building that "indeed the Lord is in this place"; because a Catholic Church was, in the truest sense of the word, the house of God. 'A church was a building, erected by men, where men might gather together and worship God their Creator. Churches, one might say, were as old as Christianity itself—although as now understood they did not really come into existence until the beginning of the fourth century. Since then the followers of Christ had at all time made it their business to erect churches, doing their best according to their means and making them as worthy as possible of the end to which they were erected. See all th'b magnificent churches, basilicas and monuments erected in every age—standing testimonies to the worship of Jesus Christ! And see also the number of churches erected before the time of the so-called Reformation, that still stood to show the belief and prefer- ence of their forefathers in this land!</p>
        <p>Catholics' Belief.</p>
        <p>And, in spite of persecution, Catholics had always tried to find a home for the blessed sacrament of Jesus Christ. True, those homes had often been no better than that lowly one at Nazareth; but now see the magnificent cathedrals and churches being erected on al/ sides. That day they had assembled to assist at the erection of another—of which they might be proud. The men of the world might say: "Why build churches? Believe if you like that you have a God and worship Him, but do this at home. Or, if you must build churches, why spend so much money on them? Why not erect four walls and a roof to shelter yourself from the rain and winds? Why all this beautiful architecture and work?" The man of the world asked such a question be- cause he was a man of .the world. He had no faith, and did not understand. The Cath- olic believed he had been created by God for a high and noble purpose—that he might spend an everlasting and perfect life in Heaven. And a Catholic believed that God had placed him in this world for a few short years in order that he might prove to Him —aye, and in how very poor a way—that he was in some way worthy of that great end. To that building, dedicated to Saint Benedict in the town of Warrington, they would be brought to receive the second waters of bap- tism that the stain and cloud of original sin might be washed away. There the poor sinner would come and confess his sins and receive cleansing by means of the sacrament. That building was likewise to be a place where the faithful would gather to worship God and _adore Him and to pay Him homage. Day after day would the holy sacrifice be offered up, and those graces and blessings received that they required for their souls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Father and Children.</head>
        <p>Father and Children.</p>
        <p>Remember also that as' this was a house of God it was also in a sense their house; because the house of the Father was the house of the children. Let them make it their home—a ladder from earth to Heaven by which those who made use of that building might he able to attain to everlasting happiness in the King- dom of Heaven.</p>
        <p>After Mass, the clergy and distinguished guests were entertained to lunch in the St. Benedict's Schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>END OF THE LAST.</head>
        <p>THE END OF THE LAST.</p>
        <p>Germany's Final Exit From The</p>
        <p>Outer Seas.</p>
        <p>H.M.S. Mersey and Severn, two shallow- draught monitors carrying heavy artillery, have visited the coast of German East Africa. and there. totally wrecked the German cruiser Konigsberg, hiding in the Rufigi river. The enemy ship fled into the estuary last October to escape the pursuing British Squadrons, and penetrated se far up stream* that she could not be followed, and destroyed. For. Months she remained trappc,q_ and aground, watched by, Britith eel' and occasthally shelled. In Ajil 'the Admiralty decided to send out from England small heavily gunned Craft which could ascend the 'river. The Mersey and the Severn were dispatched, and delivered two at- tacks which ended on July 11 in the complet destruction of the Konigsberg. When war broke out three monitors were .building in England for Brazil. They Were taken over by the Admiralty, and under the names of Mersey, Severn, and Humber have since done excellent service. During the -autumn they helped powerfully to arrest the German advance along the Belgian coast from Ostend. Within the last month the Humber , has appeared in the Dardanelles to lend sup- port to the allied armies landed on the Galli- poli_ Peninsula. -</p>
        <p>- Displacing only 1.260 tons and capable of net higher speed than 111 knots, the monitors com- bine small crews with large guns. They carry two 6-in. pieces, two 4-7 howitzers, four 3- pounders, and six smaller weapons. Their shallow draught allows them to approach very close to shore positions and also to evade and avoid submarine attack.</p>
        <p>• 'Tis better to have fished and lost than to have neva lied at all.</p>
      </div>
