      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>RIRTFIS MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</head>
        <p>RIRTFIS MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Notices of births, marriages, and deaths, in mornoriam ~ties, and acknowledgments of sympathy are inserted in "The Examiner" at- the following rates :—Not exceeding 20 words, Is. id. each; every additional seven words, Gd.</p>
        <p>Deaths. DACEY.—JuIy Bth, at 11, West-street, John Ducey, ageo 50 years.</p>
        <p>WRIGHT.—JuIy 9th, at 66, Hoyle-street, Lily Wright, aged 26 years.' FORREST.—JuIy 14th, at 42, Plumpton-street, Mary Ann Forrest, aged 54 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>In Memoriam.</head>
        <p>In Memoriam.</p>
        <p>ktPiilSS.—ln loving remembrance of our dear son. William Joseph Hipkiss, who died July 13th, 1914. Lodge-lane, Warrington. VUNER,ALS by J. MOUNFIELD &amp; SONS. 12 The Best in all Or,4es. Office: 15. Church - street. Tel. 99.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1913.</p>
        <p>THE WRITING ON THE WALL.</p>
        <p>Surely the occupation of the pessimist in this country Will very, soon be gone! As the columns of "The Examiner" recently showed, it has been reserved for an eloquent Servian writer to demonstrate to us how effectually we have, while silently suffering, gone about our task of relieving the oceans of the presence of the enemy, while at the same time we have built up an Army to send to the assistance of our Allies, the like of which the world has never before witnessed. Add to this General BoTHA's magnificent achievement in giving to the British Empire what was German South West Africa and the modern Jeremiah, if he is not quite hope- lessly beinuddled will realise that his case is further seriously undermined. For what does the victory in the great sub-Continent meani? It means the triumph of the ideals of free institutions and personal liberty, such as we are striving for, if we are striving for anything, in this gigantic and glorious, if horrible, strtiggle. We gave the South Africans that full citizenship upon which our own lives and liberties rest, and they have rewarded tts, by telling the Gerraans, in the most practical way at their disposal, that they prefer to live under our institutions than under their " Kultur." It is the fiercest blow that could have been levelled against our insolent foe; it is the finest object lesson we could have desired for the enlightenment of the rest of the world.</p>
        <p>To complete the discomfiture of the drivellers who try to make the outlook as dismal as themselves, a more effica?.ious way than the way in which the nation has rallied to the call of the Treasury could not he con-</p>
        <p>ceived. The Est for the new War Loan closed last Saturday, and on Tuesday Mr. MCKENNA was able to tell a delighted House of Commons that through' the Bank of England £570,000,000 had been subscribed by 550,000 subscribers, and through the Post Office £15,000000 had been applied for by 547,000 persons. This huge total of nearly £000,000,000 does not include any amount</p>
        <p>of Stock which will be issued for the purpose of conversion. The process of enlisting the savings of the working classes for the nation's need is a continuous one. It is still going on. The, CHANCELLOR Of the EXCHEQUER also ex- plained that thousands 'of people would have sold securities to put into the War Loan ir a market had been available. This sum "represents actual subscriptions of everything that is available." As Mr. McKENNA said :</p>
        <p>This has been an exhibition, a necessary exhibition, of the unrivalled financial re- sources of the British Empire. They have been thrown into the scale in this war; and the result is a declaration to our Allies and to our enemies alike that the United King- dom will prove faithful to its trust in the cause of its Allies.</p>
        <p>This is what we can do in the way of find- ing new money at the end of twelve mon:lus of the most wasteful war in history. It proves to our Allies that we not only have the silver bullets that will win the war, but that we are intent on using those silver bullets at once. When securities, apart from new money, are taken into account the loan may approach tho £1,000,000,000 point. 3.rie</p>
        <p>importanco of these figures, which will be as the breath of life to our Allies, as well as an inspiriting stimulus to possible Allies, will be well understood in Germany. The KAISER and his bankers may try to bluff with their tissue-paper loans, but they will know that this latest British loan is for them the writing on the wall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>LEST WE FORGET.</head>
        <p>LEST WE FORGET.</p>
        <p>• It Unist not be forgotten that, in spite of this great display of national wealth and patriotism, the need for saving has been in any way affected. We have great resources, but the Germans think that their better organisation will enable them to outlast our superior resources. Months ago they put themselves on war rations and we foolishly said they were starving, and went on in our accustomed extravagant fashion. That the Germans are not starving we have learnt to our cost; and we should do well to begin betimes to cut our own coat according to our cloth. So far as Warrington is concerned 'the signs are few that the people are practising any economies, at all events on a substantial soak, and we are glad to see that the Mmton contemplates calling a public meeting at an early data so that the important question of ,•tional thrift may be discussed. There is certainly room for an educational campaign, fort we fear greatly the people are not alive to the urgent need there is for thrift.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>NATIONAL ORGANISATION.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL ORGANISATION.</p>
        <p>It was the defects of our qualities which Lord LANSDOWNE emphasised in moving the second reading of the National Registration Bill in the House of Lords. We have been convitted, he declared, of prodigious in- efficiency in our national organisation. National organisation is as necessary in peace as in war, and Lord LANSDOWNE urged the value of National Registration for the tremendous problem which awaits us after the war. As to the relation of the Register</p>
        <p>_ 4611111Rti</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>to Conscription, Lord LANSDOWNE professed his personal belief that the nation would in the last resort insist upon compulsion to ensure equality of service. Lord WEABDALE made a vehement protest an behalf of the voluntary principle. There are three main schools of thoUght, as " streams of ten- dency" visible in regard to compulsion . (1) Those who think the time has come,</p>
        <p>or is nearly arrived, when compulsion</p>
        <p>is necessary. (2) Those who think that the voluntary system, which has done far more than any one could have expected before this crisis, may yet pull, us through.</p>
        <p>(3) Those who believe in " compasion " not as a 'necessity, but as something to be desired for its own sake.</p>
        <p>Of the last school of thought, what may be called the " Yellow Press " is the most vociferous champion. This " Yellow Press " asserts that very man in this country ought to be compelled to serve either as a soldier or a sailor, as a munition worker, or as a pro- ducer of beer. Its indignation at the sug- gestion that we are making or drinking too much beer is 'still on record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ENGLISH AND IRISH.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH AND IRISH.</p>
        <p>There has been such 'a „rapid succession of important, and unfortunately sad, events of late locally that we have been ,unable to attend to an interesting little point which the recent .holding of .the Druids' delegation in Warrington raised. In spite of his name, we thought we should not be far wrong if we described Grand Master JOHN LAviN, who succeeded Grand Master ALLEN as a typical Englishman. A week or so after the dele- gates had departed from our midst we received a letter from Bro. LAVIN, 29, Tunstall, Stoke-on-Trent, ' saying that his best thanks were due to the staff of "The Examiner" for the nice account' they had given of his career as a Druid, and also for the excellent reproduction of his photo- graph, and adding:—</p>
        <p>I have had to stand a little good-natured chaff from some of my friends and colleagues who had copies of your issue, wherein you stated that "As his portrait indicates, he is a typical Englishman," they knowing well that I have always, claimed to be an Irishman, having been born of Irish parents, in this town; but I take the compliment in the same spirit in which it was given, for at the present time in particular, we are all so welded together in one common aim, viz., the triumph of our Empire, that it would be bad taste for anyone to cavil at matters trivial. Again thanking you, and with many kindly recollections of my first visit to your town,</p>
        <p>I remain, Yburs sincerely, JOHil LAVIN.</p>
        <p>A real, courteous, genuine Irishman, as well as—may we repeat it in fear and tremb- ling ?—a typical Englishman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ST. ELPHIN'S PARK.</head>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S PARK.</p>
        <p>Several weeks, if not months, have elapsed since :we heard on what we regarded as reliable authority, that it had been decided to open St. Elphin's Park to the public. The place is still exactly as. it has been for years, except that the flower beds are looking remarkably well, having been nicely arranged by the gardeners and brought to a state approaching perfection by Nature. There is no breach in the harsh looking iron railings which keep ,all and sundry at a respectful distance from the flowers—perhaps to add enchantment to the view. Spring has grown into summer, and autumn is Lot so very far away. Is it a fact that the park has been marked down for opening, and, if so, why has the event been again delayed?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ONE WAY OF HELPING.</head>
        <p>ONE WAY OF HELPING.</p>
        <p>There are many ways of helping along the great work the nation has in hand. Some can fight; some can make munitions. Some can lend money. But all can say a cheerful word—if to the men who are fighting and receiving the hard knocks so much the better. We do not know a finer example of what can be done in this way than the plan adopted by the St. Helens Cable Co. at their large War- rington works. These works have a com- manding position overlooking the railway, near Bank Quay Station, along which soldiers have travelled to and fro since the outbreak of the war. On the side of one of the large buildings of the firm appear the following 'lines in large bold white lettering, which can be seen distinkly from all passing trains :</p>
        <p>All you who travel past to fight For us in foreign parts: We wish good luck and safe return And thank you from our hearts.</p>
        <p>- Compare this act of real patriotism with the dreary outpourings of the critics. A pat on the back and a "thank you" will be a /stimulus to " TommY," and to those above him, in the day of battle; while the poor stuff the critics have to offer may easily paralyse their action. The St. Helens Cable Co. have given many men to the war; they add their best wishes. It is an improvement upon the grudging gift of men by some firms in our midst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>COUNTRY HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>A COUNTRY HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>As was generally supposed would be the case, the time-honoured custom of taking a holiday by the sea has been largely suspended this year, for several reasons. The railway facilities, in the way of cheaper fares at all events, have vanished. Seasideplacesare largely occupied by the men ,of our new Armies, and prices for rooms are up alarmingly. Besides, the seaside spirit is lacking in ti:e presence of so much sadness and bereave- ment. The countryside, which always has its distinctive charms and is a powerful attrac- tion for many natures, makes a special call upon our attention- this year. The farm- house or the country cottage is an ideal place in which to spend a " quiet " holiday, suit- able to a time when, though we may need change and rest to rtone us up and make us fit to meet another year of hard work, we do not feel justified in Making our little vacation a time of pleasure-seeking. We feel sure that if our country friends would take steps to letthetownspeople know where apartments may be had—say by means of "The Examiner" "Where to Stay" advertise- ment columns—they would reap a plenteous harvest. They Would also be doing a good service to the community in more ways than one.</p>
        <p>A Joke. WoMan (about to board the Cemetery-Central ear): "Is this t' Cemetery?" Conductor "Naow; it's a ear."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TRENCH PERISCOPES.</head>
        <p>TRENCH PERISCOPES.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Crosfield Makes A Welcome Gift.</p>
        <p>MEN FIT AND READY.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Crosfield, who recently sent a supply of periscopes for the use of our local Territorials at the Front, has received a letter from Major Cary, thanking him for the gift of a dozen of them received through Major Crosfield—" the most useful present," he says, "you could have sent, which will, no doubt, be the means of saving valuable- lives. You will be pleased to hear," he adds, "that ' A ' Company have done as well as any in the battalion, and are now fit for anything or anywhere."</p>
        <p>SIR ARTHUR AND THE BATTALION. Sir Arthur, replying to Major Cary, wrote:— Dear Major Cary,—l thank you warmly for the letter of July 7th which you have kindly written me, in which you tell me that the periscopes I got for the battalion are the right sort of thing, and useful. I dare say you will Understand the deep feeling of satisfaction it gives any of us who are, perforce, stay-at- homes, whenever we are able to do some little thing to help the splendid fellows who are fighting the greatest, of all battles for Britain, the Empire, and 'even the entire civilisation of the world during these strenuous times. That is putting the issue of the great struggle very high, but, as I am very sure we all feel and realise with absolute certainty', not a whit too high. . _ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Our High Aim.</head>
        <p>Our High Aim.</p>
        <p>I was, if possible, more strongly than ever impressed with the truth and overwhelming importance of these facts when I attended the lunch given hist Tuesday under the auspices of the Empire Parliamentary Association, at the House of Commons, in honour of Sir Robert Borden.</p>
        <p>Mr. BELlfour is president of this association, which I joined at its inception. He presided, having on his right, of course, Sir Robert Borden, and next to him our Secretary for the Colonies, Mr. Bonar Law, who is himself Canadian-born. Immediately. opposite Mr. Balfour and Sir Robert Borden sat Sir George Reid, the genial, cheery, capable and extremely popular High Commissipner for Australia. Sir George Perley, High Commissioner for Canada, was, of course, present, with his fellow- countrymen; Mr. Schreiner. represented South Africa, while our splendid Indian Empire was fully represented by Lord Curzon of Kedleston, the ex.-Viceroy of India, and Lord Islington, the very capable Under-Secretary of State for our Empire of the East. Without prolonging the list further, what I have written will suffice to show how representative was the character of that memorable gathering, which Sir Robert Borden addressed in a speech which none of us who heard it are 'likely to forget. It was, as Mr-Balfour well said of it, a most admirable and moving speech. The war is a grim and terrible reality for all of us, to whichever of tht Allied nations we belong, con- tending, as we are, against so cruel and un- scrupulous and remorseless a foe. But assuredly, for those of us who are citizens of the British Empire, there. is a silver lining to this dark cloud in the very fact that we see week by week and month by month that Empire bound together more and more 'closely by ties which, as Sir Robert Borden said, can never be dissolved,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Imperial Silver Lining.</head>
        <p>The Imperial Silver Lining.</p>
        <p>It was that which added a deeper significance to his reference to the immortal courage shown by the men of Australia and New ,Zealand in the classic ground of the Gallipoli i'eninsula, to the magnificent achievement of the Cana- dians in the Netherlands, which has already won them world-wide and ,enduring fame, 'and to the indomitable courage and endurance which the men of Britain and Old Ireland have shown in both the Western and the Eastern theatre of war.</p>
        <p>Very significant was the character of the response those remarks received from one and all of those assembled at that historic gather- ing.</p>
        <p>Perhaps, though, you will agree with me that it , was not unfitting at. this juncture, having regard to all.that has 'haipened in the past, that the most ringing cheers of all given at that memorable gathering greeted the reference to Louis Botha, his conquest of South-West Africa, his complete and final triumph over German intrigue and treachery. May it not be long before that most happy omen in the Cape is reflected in yet greater victories on a vaster field of war nearer home! I hope with all my heart that the best of good fortune in war may attend your efforts, and. warmly congratulating you on the magnificent conduct of the men of our Warrington Battalion, remain, very faithfully yours,_</p>
        <p>A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>BANK NOTICES.</head>
        <p>BANK NOTICES.</p>
        <p>THE MANCHESTER AND LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>The Directors of the Manchester and Liverpool District Banking Co., Ltd., report that the gross profit for the half-year ended June 30th, after making provision for bad and doubtful debts, was £305,937. The expenses amounted to £120,198, leaving a net profit of £185,738 for the half-year, to which must be added £65,671 brought forward from the previous half-year. The Directors recommend a dividend of .21 per share (171 per cent. per annum), subject to the deduction of income tax. This will absorb £165,900, leaving a balance of .R 85,510 to carry forward to the next account. The balance sheet for the half-year shows:— Current, del)osit and other accounts, X 28,505,432; acceptances, £1,208,873; cash in hand and in the Bank of England, £4,487,162; money at call and short notice, £4,449,188; bills of exchange, £1,953,515; British and other Treasury Bills, £1,806,000; War Loan, 1925-28, £2,324,333; investments, £3,459,662; advances in current and other accounts, £13,284,088; bank property, £500,000. The total assets amount to £33,472,823.</p>
        <p>In order to make provision for depreciation of the Bank's investments, a sum of £200,000 has been transferred from the reserve fund to a reserve for depreciation of investments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>KATHLEEN-A TOL</head>
        <p>KATHLEEN-A TOL</p>
        <p>Where'er thou art the roses blush At the beauty of thy face, And lilies bend their heads in shame At thy purity and grace. Birds cease their song, content to hear The sweetness of thy voice; Thy presence fills the vale with glee, And all the hills rejoice.</p>
        <p>Where'er thou art the ocean vast Lies unruffled and at rest Except—in unison—to swell With the heaving of thy breast. E'en Boreas keeps low to see Such perfect womanhood; None but Zephyrus dares to kiss— One so divinely good.</p>
        <p>Whereer thou art there I shall be, In the spirit by thy side, To walk as 'twere in Paradise With thee, my Heavenly bride; For thou art wedded to my soul, A soul that lives for thee, Through life to death—to life beyond— To all eternity. R. OSSULSTON-RICHE.</p>
        <p>Our old friend, Mr. 11. Ossulston-Riche, the actor-soldier-poet, whose poems are well known to "Examiner" readers, has favoured us this week with the above. It shows true genius that a man in these sad times can retain his power to sing of love and faithfulness and purity. Such times, how- ever, give genius its proper place; our wars have ever produced our finest songs. "Go, lovely rose !" of which "Kathleen" forcibly reminds us, was the product of the Civil War, along With not a few other imperishable poems. "Kathlaen " has been set to really fine music, and will appear in due course as a ballad. The name of the composer is, for the moment, a -secret.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>DRINK &amp; THE WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>DRINK &amp; THE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Licensees Must Not Serve Men</p>
        <p>From Military Hospitals.</p>
        <p>THE TREATING NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>The following notice was served upon all licensees in the County Borough of Warring- ton on Wednesday : _ _</p>
        <p>"By virtue of the poivers conferred on me under Regulation 10 of the Defence of the Realm (Consolidation) Regulations, 1914, I, John Antill Moggridge, being the competent military authority for the 40th Regimental Area, in the Western Command, do hereby order that all licensed p-remises situate In the 40th .Regimental Area, and within the County Borough of Warrington, be closed for the sale, supply or consumption of intoxicat- ing liquor to or by any member of His Majesty's Forces residing in any -of the mili- tary hospitals within the said County Borough, as also Lord Derby's War Hospi-</p>
        <p>• tal, Warrington." ' We understand that _one of the reasons whith prompted the issuing of this new Order is• that manY, of the wounded men, whilst in the con- valescent stage, have been tempted to accept so many "free drinks," preferred by well- intentioned, hut misguided, admirers, that in some instances the process of recovery has been more or less seriously retarded. ," So far as we are concerned," said a repre- sentative publican, and a prominent member of the Licensed Victuallers' Association, to an "Examiner" representative, f` we are quite willing to conform with the new regulation, for naturally it is. not our desire to countenance anything that shall retard the recovery of a single wounded man. At ,the same time there is one thing which naturally rather puzzles us, and that is how .we are expected to know whe- ther a man in khaki who comes into our houses for refreshment is wounded or not. There are many wounded men walking about the town daily who wear no visible bandages and who neither walks' with stick or crutch, but, to all outward appearances, are 'as right as rain.' How do we get on if, unwittingly, we serve one of those men?" .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>THOUGHTLESS CIVILIANS.</head>
        <p>THOUGHTLESS CIVILIANS.</p>
        <p>Inconsiderate "Friends " The Soldiers' Worsi Enemies.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Exarniner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l feel sure your readers must welcome the new Order issued by Colonel J. A. Moggridge, commanding the 40th Regimental' Area, forbidding the sale and supply of in- toxicating* liquors to any member of His Majesty's Forces residing in any of the mili- tary hospitals in the borough of Warrington.</p>
        <p>If civilians would realise the harm and in- justice they do to our wounded soldiers by giving them, or encouraging them to take drink, they would surely abstain from doing so. Intoxicating liquor retards recovery, and while doctors and nurse's are exerting "them- selves to cure the patients, thoughtless so-called "friends" are hindering the work; also, to be drunk, is a serious military offence which tells against a soldier during his whole career. °Lord Kitchener has made earnest appeals to civilians to abstain, from giving drink. Lord Roberts, in his last public letter, made a similar appeal.</p>
        <p>Why, then, are some of our people so ignorant and so inconsiderate in their treat- ment of soldiers?</p>
        <p>A military Order, like the present one, is necessary, since appeals to their sense of reason and justice are unavailing.</p>
        <p>SYLVIA BROADBENT. British Red Cross •Society-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WAR LOAN.</head>
        <p>THE WAR LOAN.</p>
        <p>The Patriotic Response Of A Whole People.</p>
        <p>Mr. McKenna, the Chancellor of the Exche- quer, announcing the result of the subscrip- tion to the War Loan, told the House of Com- mons the figures were:— Bank of England subscribers 550,000 Amount of subscriptions Post Office subscribers Amount of sübscriptions Mr. McKenna explained, amid cheers, that the gigantic total of the subscriptions through the Bank of England represented only new money; it did not include any stock which would be issued for the purposes of conversion. The figures given for Post Office transactions were calculated up to Saturday last, but subscriptions through the Post Office did not close on that day. He could not at present state the number of vouchers sold. This total of nearly &lt;2600,000,000; which was far bnyraid any amount ever subscribed in the world's history, had been obtained by the patriotic response of the whole people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SOME OF THE SUBSCRIBERS.</head>
        <p>SOME OF THE SUBSCRIBERS.</p>
        <p>London City and Midland Bank, £21,000,000. Manchester and Liverpool District Bank, £5,000,000. Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank, £2,000,000. Prudential Assurance Co., £3,18.8,000. Sun Fire and Life Insurance 'Co., £250,000. United Ancient Order of Druids, £17,000. United Kingdom Commercial Travcllers' Benevolent Institution, £lO,OOO. Wigan Town Council, £22,000. National Union of Railwaymen, £107,000. Boilermakers' Society, £BO,OOO. Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation, £50,000. National Union of Teachers, £40,000. General Federation of Trade Unions, X/10,000. Sailors' and Firemen's Union, £30,000. Amalgamated Wiredrawers' Society, £21,000. Cheshire Miners' Permanent Relief Society, £17,000. Gasworkers' Union, £5,000. Mr. Harry Lauder, X 40,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>UNION BANK AND ITS STAFF.</head>
        <p>UNION BANK AND ITS STAFF.</p>
        <p>The directors of the Union Bank of Manches- ter, Limited, have intimated to the members of the staff of the bank that in order to en- courage them to subscribe to the War Loan the Bank will advance the necessary money to the extent of .2500 in the case of ordinary members of the staff, and up to ..{l,OOO in the case of the more responsible officials, on the following terms:— The money to be repaid within three years. Interest at 41 per cent. per annum to be charged, but if repayment be made in the time stipulated half per cent. per annum to be returned, thus reducing the rate of interest to 4 per cent. A bonus of half per cent. per annum on such portion of the amount as from time to time is provided by the officer concerned. In the event of the applicant requiring to dispose of his holding at any time during the first three years the bank will take it off his hands at par, unless the market price should be above par, in which case, of course, the holder would have the opportunity of selling it on the market. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>SCOTT'S EMULSION.</head>
        <p>SCOTT'S EMULSION.</p>
        <p>Scott &amp; Browne, Ltd., the proprietors of the welr-known Scott's Emulsion, have made ap- plication for a first' investment of £25,000 in the War Loan. THE NATIONAL DEPOSIT FRIENDLY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting of the Board of Manage- ment of the National Deposit Friendly Society it was decided to convert certain stocks now held into'the new War Loan, and to make in- vestment of other funds in the loan. Alto- gether the society will invest nearly £200,000 in the loan.</p>
        <p>Big Drop in the Birth-rate. During the week ended July 10th 34 births and 17 deaths were recorded in the borough, as against 50. births and 24 deaths in the cor- responding period 0 last year. Three of last week's deaths were of infants under a year old and nine were of persons between the ages of 25 and 65. The death-rate last week was 23.4, as against 12.2 for the previous six weeks and 16.6 in the corresponding period of last year. On tho other hand the birth-rate was only 11.7 last week, as against 34.7 in the same week of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Citizens' Temperance League. Another open-air meeting in connection with the pledge-signing ce.mpaign, organised by the above League, will be held in Bewsey-street en Friday evening next.</p>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices. Wheat firmer, quantities of home-grown ge.t- ting scarce, 7s. to 7s. 4d. per 701 b.; oats remain firm at late rates, viz.. 4s. 2d. to 4s. 4d. Per 451 b.; wheat straw, 375. to 375. 6d. per ton; Ga. straw, 36s to 37s per ton.' Flour is firmer with a is. per 2801 b. advance; bran is. 3d. per 2011'.</p>
        <p>Consecrating Soldiers' Last Resting-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>War Letters and Photographs.</head>
        <p>War Letters and Photographs.</p>
        <p>We invite our readers to submit to us letters received from their soldier friends. We shall also be glad to receive photo- graphs of soldiers who have been killed,' wounded, mentioned in dispatches or other- wise become a source of interest to the public. The letters will be carefully edited so that nothing will appear in print that ought not to appear. The photographs, when suitable, will be published in "The Examiner," which has an established reputation as a picture paper. All letters and photographs are returned after use, undamaged, to the owners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>More Cases of Scarlet Fever.</head>
        <p>More Cases of Scarlet Fever.</p>
        <p>The scarlet fever epidemic is still with us and during the week ended July 10th 24 fresh cases were notified. In the same period six cases of tuberculous phthisis and two of 'ery- sipelas were reported to the Medical Officer of health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>N Big Bench.</head>
        <p>N Big Bench.</p>
        <p>There was an unusually large Bench at The Borough Court on Monday. The Mayor pre- sided, and his colleagues present Were as fol- lows: Aldermen Jas. Evans, Pierpoint, Smethurst and. Bolton, Dr. 'Joseph, Coun- cillor Wilkinson, and Messrs. W. Furness, W. Roberts, T. Looker, G. Pilling and S. Wright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Weather.</head>
        <p>The Weather.</p>
        <p>Last week's mean temperature was one de- gree higher than in the preceding week, and stood at 58. The warmest day during the week ended July 10th was the 6th inst:, when the glass recorded 72 degrees, whilst the coolest was the 4th, when the temperature fell to 46 degrees. The rainfall last week was equal to 1.13 inches, and the general direction of the 'wind was W.N.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Summer Train Services.</head>
        <p>Summer Train Services.</p>
        <p>The railway time-tables for the summer sea- son came into operation on Monday, and will continue in force, under ordinary conditions, until September 30th. Since the outbreak of the war, however, the changes have been many, frequent and sudden; and the travelling public would do well to keep in touch with the guides. The Warrington A.B.C. Guide was published on Monday. It contains many alterations and additions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Deafness and Blindness.</head>
        <p>Deafness and Blindness.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ison (of Ison's Eye and Ear Dispensary, Ltd.), 71, Great George-street, Leeds, has a re- markable record of success. His nethods are up-to-date and without operation, and-he effects marvellous cures in apparently hopeless cases. Write for 'testimonials and printed form of questions, sent post free. Mr. Ison attends at the Lion Hotel, Warrington, Wednesday, July 28th, hours 11 to 2; Union Hotel, Liverpool, Wednesday, July 28th, hours 3.30 to B.—{Advt.].</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Railway Clerks' Association.</head>
        <p>Railway Clerks' Association.</p>
        <p>At the monChly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, held on Tuesday evening, a communication was read from the Railway Clerks' Association, setting forth the fact that since the outbreak of war the asso- ciation had considerably increased its member- ship,tand had, in other directions, widened the scope of its activities. The communication further pointed out that, out of 35,000 members only a hundred objected to paying subscriptions to the political fund of the association. At- tention was also drawn to the fact that during last year, despite the war, profits on the rail- ways equalled £1,000,000 per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Preston Brook Visited.</head>
        <p>Preston Brook Visited.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. • Ziba Armitage on SatUrday entertained at their residence, Newton Bank, Preston Brook, about forty members. of the Wycliffe Church Y.P.S. and Sunday School teachers. At first the weather did not look promising, but fears happily proved ill-founded and a particularly pleasant afternoon and evening were passed in lawn-games. Tea was served in the library. Before the visitors departed a cordial vote of thanks was accorded the host and hostess on the motion of Messrs. S. C. Owen and H. Eccles; and this Mrs. Armitage graciously acknowledged. After- wards the company returned home by road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Rector's Garden Party to Sunday School Teachers.</head>
        <p>Rector's Garden Party to Sunday School Teachers.</p>
        <p>In the Rectory grounds on Saturday after- noon the Warrington and District Sunday School Teachers' Association held their annual garden-party. This proved a very successful function. After two hours spent in lawn games, the members repaired to the Fairfield Schools—where tea was taken. At six they returned to the Rectory grounds for two hours, at the end of which time evensong was held in the church, a helpful address being given by the Rev. W. 0. H. Rodwell (vicar of St. Dun- stan's, Liverpool).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Brisk Leather Trade.</head>
        <p>Brisk Leather Trade.</p>
        <p>Trade continued brisk throughout the week, writes the Warrington correspondent of the "Leather Trades Review," a heavy demand was experienced for all classes, factors and merchants were scouring the district for such lots as had come round since their last visit, and the total output was very satisfactory. It seemed that the contract demand for Army sole and accoutrement leathers was more pressing than usual, and this was supplemented with a much stronger inquiry for further supplies. Apart from these lines, the demand has been very encouraging for the home trade, and fairly big business has been done in medium bends, with a demand beyond the supply for the lower priced kinds of branded and foreign. Fostered possibly by improved facilities, the French in- quiry seems to be developing, and restricted as it is to classes which are not suited for military requirements here, there is every, reason why it should reach good dimensions, and, perhaps, incidentally pave the way for an increased im- port of hides from France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Fruiterers, Florists and Fishmongers Organise. _ _</head>
        <p>Fruiterers, Florists and Fishmongers Organise. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Powell has-been unanimously elected President of the Warrington and District Fruiterers', Florists' and Fishmongers' Associa- tion, which was, formed in March last, and now has a membership of 48. This fact was reported at a meeting of members held last week, at which the following other officers were also elected: Vice-President, Mr. F. Harter; secre- tary, Mr. Rogers; treasurer, Mr. A. Sampson; committee, Messrs. W. Bate, J. Moss, jun., F. Monsdale, Robt. Lea, Harry Osborne, W. Green, J. McGuiness, W. Byfield, Jos. Deakin and R. Smith. It 'was reported that the sub- scriptions received sinoe the inauguration of the Association amounted to £9 55., and the expenses of inauguration, &amp;c., to .2.5 86. 9d.; leaving a balance in the 4ands of the treasurer of .423 16s. 3d., which vtas regarded as very satisfactory. In the course of a viva TOCe report on the working of the newly-formed Association, the President referred to the fact that they had already tackled the problem of wholesale trading in the Borough Market, with the result that he had reason to believe that the matter was receiving the earnest attention of the Council. This announcement was received with much satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Wyeliffians' Prayers for Soldier P.S..t,</head>
        <p>Wyeliffians' Prayers for Soldier P.S..t,</p>
        <p>Members.</p>
        <p>The Secretary (Mr. Ben Farrar) presided at Sunday's Pleasant Afternoon at the Wycliffe Congregational Church, and read letters from three soldier-members, one of them, Private Joseph Bond, is at present in the Ist Eastern General Hospital at Cambridge recovering from a wound received in a big battle on Stine Ifith. The other letters were from Corporal Hodson. and Private Yeates, an old and esteemed mem- ber of the P.S.A. Brotherhood, who is back at the Front fighting after having been once wounded. Mr. Farrar also expressed a hearty welcome to Private Spencer (who is on sick furlough, recovering from a wound) and Private Barlow.—Mr. Langton gave an address on the subject: "Am Imy brother's keeper?" He spoke of the evil accruing from enmity in the home, and urged the value and beauty of real brotherliness.—Mr. Yearsley also spoke.—Miss Jones sang, as the solo, "Songs my mother used to sing !" Mr. Payton presided at the organ.— Special prayers were offered for the President (the Rev. T. T. James) who is doing chaplain's work in France, and for all the members who are on active service and who are either at the Front or are making ready to go there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Place.</head>
        <p>Place.</p>
        <p>This afternoon the Riglit Rev. the Lord Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Chavasse) will conse- crate the new portion of the Warrington Ceme- tery which has been set aside for the interment of soldiers who succumb in the local war hos- pitals. The consecration service will com- mence at 3.45.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Bishop at St. Barnabas' Church.</head>
        <p>Bishop at St. Barnabas' Church.</p>
        <p>The- Lord Bishop of Liverpool the it. Her- Dr. Chavasse) paid a visit yesterday to St. Barnabas' parish, and in the evening preached to a large congregation in the church, touch- ing upon many phases of Church activity, and emphasising the fact that the one thing wanted to make the parish complete from the Church- man's point of view, was money to make the church complete. ,</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. Richard Bridge. A memorial service \ for the late Mr. Richard Bridge, whose demise we recorded in our last issue, was held in the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church on Sunday, when the preacher was the Rev. W. Cooper, of Leigh. There was a large congregation, and the organist (Mr. Banner) played "0, Rest in the Lord," whilst the choir sang the anthem "What. Are These?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Flower Services at Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Flower Services at Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>To-morrow special flower services are being held at the Golborne-street Baptist Church, the preacher for the day being the Rev. 11. Ogden (pastor). A children's floral service will take place in the afternoon at 2.30, when Mrs. Moores will preside, and the address will be given by Miss Bessie Dutton, of Moore. At the evening service Miss Bates will sing an appropriate solo. The flowers will be sent to the various hospitals, military and civil, in the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Picturedrome's New Manager.</head>
        <p>Picturedrome's New Manager.</p>
        <p>The public of Warrington need no introduc- tion to Mr. Arthur Turpin, and we are pleased to announce that he is once more with us. The management of the Picturedrome and the Picture House is now entirely in his hands- These two houses of high-class entertainment are to be congratulated on the acquisition of such a deservedly popular Manager. We wish him every success. A new scheme of decoration is, we understand, under consideration, which will convert the Picturedrome into a miniature fairyland. This, and the all-round excellence of the show, under the skilful handling of the genial "'Purpin," guarantee that the Picturedrome will be "some" picture house.</p>
        <p>Fatal Street Accident. An accident which occurred in Winwick-road at about tea-time on Thursday afternoon, had a fatal termination in the early hours of yester- day morning. The victim was a seven-year-old boy named Thompson, who resided with hie parents in Hill-street. It appears that he ran out suddenly from behind a vehicle which was proceeding down the road, and that the motor car, which was proceeding in the opposite direction, caught him and knocked him down before the driver could bring it to a standstill. ' When the lad was removed to the Infirmary he was found to be suffering from dire internal - injuries, as well as from various superficial wounds. He was unconscious, and remained 5° up to his death. Lady Crosfield Wins. The Ranelagh Club gave up an afternoon last week to competitions at golf, tennis and other outdoor games, the proceeds from the " gallery" being devoted entirely to the Red, CroSs Fund. There was a great gathering and a large sum was taken. The lawn tennis (doubles) competition organised by Lady Rock- savage, assisted by Lady Crosfield, attracted „‘) all, the finest lady tennis players in the king- - dom, the competitors including Mrs. Lambert Chambers, the lady champion. Among other competitors were Lady Victoria Primrose, Lad, Drogheda, Mrs. Winston Churchill, Miss de Trafford, Countess Zia Torby, Countess Nado Torby, and Lady Portarlington. Lady Crosfield and her sister, Miss carried off the first prize, a fine achievement, seeing that so many great artists at the gams were taking part.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Church AnniversarY</p>
        <p>The annual summer sermons in connection with the above church were held on Sun,dal• The Preacher for the day was the Rev. Job!) Francis Matthews, of Sheffield (former pastor). In the morning he preached from the words "Without shedding of blood there is no re' mission," and in the evening from the worde "Sir, we would see Jesus." Both discourse were very helpful to those who had the priri' leg,e of hearing them. Special anthems w-ert; rendered by the choir, under the leadership el Mr. F. W. Grounds. Mr. W. T. Wilkinson, jun., presided at the organ. In the afternoon at a musical service soloe, duets, &amp;c., were excellently rendered b-a the following artistes: Madame Sara Silcool" (soprano), Madame B. Eckersley (contralto), Mr. W. Dutton (baritone), and Mr. J. Sephton (tenor). Mr. A. E. Trow acted as accompanist. Mr. Matthews also gave an address at thie service. There were large attendances through' out the day, the congregation in the evening including the Mayor and Mayoress (Alden:PO and Mrs. P. Peacock). The collections for the day amounted to over £l5.</p>
        <p>Wood's College Successes: At the June examination of the National Shorthand Association the following students of Wood's College were successful in securiag their certificates : Theory.—With distinction: Helen Littler, Eva Pendlebury, Eva Wood, Eva. Muskett, Annie Chantler. Pass: Dora Epps, Catherias J. Little, Marjorie Barrington, Marion I_Vre' Ethel Bertles, Gladys Challoner, Hughes. Advanced Theory.—Pass: Rose Byrne, Mal Heath, Sissie Charnock. Speed (50 words).—With distinction: Ethfl Bertles. Pass: Sissie Charnock, May Ilea?: Marion Lowe, Dora Epps, Winifred Bennet' Gertrude Bradley, Marion Howard, Agaie Chantler. Speed (60 words).—Pass: Hilda Eva Baal: Speed (70 words).—Pass: Alice Cook (age—l'" Helen Wallington, Reginald Gallop. Speed (80 words): With distinction: V.. 0.1 Wallington, Florence Malkin (age 14), ;. beth F. Billson (age 14), Fred Shelley (ago Pass: Fanny Martin. Speed: words).—iVith distinction: Oil- bert Cook. Pass: James Milner. c ye During the last fortnight ten students LLEI taken up positions with local business houe°'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>FOR OUR FRENCH ALLIES'</head>
        <p>FOR OUR FRENCH ALLIES'</p>
        <p>Hippodrome Paiii6lls Buy Flags At Special Perlormaneegi At both both houses at the Hippodrome crr nesday evening, a large number of persuami;l young ladies sold miniature tricolour flag" Dir.. aid of the French Relief Fund; indeed ge*, suasive were they that their stock of the nationdl emblems was three-parts exhausteq. fore it was time for the second show, with tge result that many who attended that had„tf ot content with donating their coppers vriller getting the little memento in return. Altoget 1 ri the sum of ..211 8s: wail- realised, and it has.tciFeeent duly forwarded by the courteous manager, Mr. Will A. Jackson, to the quarter. Specially tb. Specially attraCtiVe progfaninves were, -d kindness of the management, sutinitted STo. houses, the efforts of the ,artistes appearing jug the week being supplemented by:that singer, Miss Marie Houghton, and bY the2",73, , of the 2nd South Lanes. Regiment, under ow master Cahill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>HONOURED DEAD.</head>
        <p>HONOURED DEAD.</p>
        <p>memorial Service :to Fallen S. Lancs.</p>
        <p>PHARSE ARM TIMMS</p>
        <p>For Lives Ungrudgingly Given and %York Done.</p>
        <p>The officers and Men of the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment attended the Memorial service held at the Ashford (Kent) Parish Church .on Wednesday evening, in honour of those of the South Lancashire Infantry Brigade who had fallen in action. The Brigadier, General Macfie, accompanied by 13r. 1' ajor Bellamy, D. 5.0., and Staff- Captain Southey were in attendance, and the officers of the 4th South Lancashires present ere: Col. J. D. Fairclough, Major H. G. Roberts, Captain Max Woods, Captain 0. L. 'Whittle, Captain W. McClure, Lieutenant H. Patch, Lieutenant A. E. Newton, Lieutenant R. John- ‘cm, and Lieutenant Rotherham.; The other officers included Colonel Bates (sth South Lan- .cashires), Major Clarke (9th King's) and Major Pairrie (10th Liverpool Scottish). . Many of the men in khaki had recently returned from .the Front, and there was also a number of wounded soldiers. The instrumental music was rendered with eaitiful effect by the combined band of the</p>
        <p>4th South Lancashires, under the conductor- hip of Bandmaster Sadler. . The service 6Dened with the Funeral March (Chopin), in Which the roll of the drums had a fine effect. he prayers of the Burial Service were read by the Bishop of Stafford (the Rev. L. Payne- Crawfurd), and the Lesson (Rveiation vit. 9 tO end) was read by Brigadier-General Macfie. Tho hymns rendered were "Now the Labour- er 's task is o'er," "For all the Saints," "On the Resurrection morning," concluding with " Rely Father, in Thy mercy," sung kneeling, for absent friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>GLORIOUS DEATH.</head>
        <p>A GLORIOUS DEATH.</p>
        <p>The Chaplain for the Division (the Rev. J. 9. Coop, of St. Catherine's, Abercrombie-square, Liverpool) gave a brief address from the 'text Jfeb i. 21: "The Lord gave and te Lord I..as taken away," observing that they had come to Church not to mourn, but to honour their com- rades who had given their lives for their country. There was not one single mention in the Prayer-Book, hymns, Or burial service *bout mourning the dead; but what was men- tioned was the praise and thanksgiving for the 4Plendid work done. It was not their duty to trudge the lives that had been given so un- 'grudgingly, and it was incorrect to say that their gallant heroes had died prem4turely. Xany a man had been cut off.in the prime of life- They had been ordained to die, and no Ittan could .die better than for his King and country. Their service had been honourably and gloriously rendered and it was for them to Pray in thanksgiving for the glorious hope of the Christian resurrection. The impressive service closed with the splen- did rendering of the Dead March in "Saul" by the Band of the 4th South Lancashires, followed by the "Last Post," sounded By four buglers standing in the gallery .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH MEN.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH MEN.</p>
        <p>'Service for the Fallen at St. Thomas' Church. At St. Thonias' Church, on Sunday, a Memorial service for those who have fallen on the field of battle was conducted by the Rev. E. W. Clarke:—A-sermon was delivered by the Rey. T. T. Lee-Jones (vicar of Daresbury), who dwelt on the heroism of the gallant fellows who have given up their lives in a cause near to their hearts, and on the sacrifice of the mothers who parted with their sons.. It was worse for the women left behind than for the men similarly situated, for the men had their businesses to attend to, but the women had to stay at home, and they missed the absent faces c;re than the menfolk, and had more time to u`nrink about their loss.—There was a big con- c'g.B-tion, including representatiyes of the artsh . branch uncil. and a detachment of the local of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>AU REVOIR !</head>
        <p>AU REVOIR !</p>
        <p>More Warrington "Terriers" Go Into</p>
        <p>War-Training. vo-S°lne of the more-recently recruited of the arri ogton Territorials left town on Wednes- 'Ay evening in ordei• to enter upon their war- ah‘ tr• - A.. -fang in the South, prior to embarking upon liaore serious and glorious adventure still iorther South. The detachment numbered 152 td they went to join their comrades of the (Territofial) South Lancashires, who 'Lave already been in training some little time.</p>
        <p>. A Rousing Send-Off. Tho men were given a rousing _send-off at Rank Quay Station, thousands assembling to „Tnequrage them with a parting cheer. The xhaki-clad lads—most of them were young, .with beardless chins—paraded at the Drill Ilan shortly after eight o'clock, and then, six- 'a -breast, marched down to the station by the nearest route, each carrying hi S white-canvas lit-bag. No band preceded them, but it Seeded no adventitious aid to arouse the en- 'thusiasm of the crowd, and deep and long 'were the cheers that awaited their ears as they lo'rung through the station portals. And touch- ing, too, were the personal an re,roirs that they Igaid—to wives, mothers, sisters, fathers, bro- 'there, and sweethearts, who, regardless of the '4lft-repeated "Stand. back, please!" of the Policemen, broke through the khaki ranka and tnnk affectionate farewells of their dear ones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Parting Scenes.</head>
        <p>Parting Scenes.</p>
        <p>• I Many were the little keepsakes exchanged, stid many were the tears shed. by the women- -k—after the brave lads had gone. Long "ger the last "Terrier" had passed through! Elks , Press and the station doors had been. ased. upon those who would fain have fol- on to the platform, the crowd lingered, 6Llld as they heard the waiting lads whiling 41.4°,a3- the time by singing the inevitable "It's ;7' long, long way to Tipperary" and the other 'ln•Pular ditties which "Tommy" has made Peeially his own, they joined in the choruses— fltil a chuff-chuff the engine drawing the tifl that was to bear the plucky lads away r-anaed into the station. Then for a few nutes all was silence, until a shrill whistle *3lgnalled the actual moment of departure, and ; .deep-throated cheer, that made the welkin ibs"ng,_ burst forth from the waiting crowd, 1,-16,ing cheers coming from beyond the grey -'14•18 of the station. And thus they went Zw:,B4Y, taking with them the prayers, the ~,,rati,tlae, ,tlae, and the heart-felt good wishes of thou- -11 of their fellow townsmen and towie- -°Daen. c,:r.lte men were -under the command of Lieut. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TWICE WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>TWICE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Private Leonard Woodward Safe At Home Once More. / After having been twice wounded. Private t.": 10 ,ilard Woodward, a young Warrington Tern- who went through the historic charge AZlcame out scathless, is now at his home in litleet, having been granted leave for i:alth reasons. Following the memorable fight toZbich the Territorials assisted in the cap- of three lines of the enemy's trenches, Ittivate 'Woodward was sent to hospital in m,2t_lnn and there he stayed until last week-end, 1.471 he was included among a draft bound for lie landed in England on Sunday, after .aituing no fewer than 25.1 hours aboard ship, 133.„0ugh lie and his comrades had only to ft the passage from Boulogne. The passe bY no means danger-free, an added spice of 11t being given when an unknown sub- -‘arine ,Da 'was sighted and again when the vessel ii,fad within a bare fifteen yards of the dere- ,a4—though live—torpedo, which was floating peut in the water. When the boat on which .._rvata Woodward sailed arrived at South- ZatAsll it was unable to get a berth at the fict.4g. and a motor boat had to put but from 110. and take the passengers off. „..:;an t Only did Private Woodward participate r, ever-memorable charge, but he also took i'vt. in the subsequent battle in which the 4rritoriab figured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>HEROES OF THE CHARGE.</head>
        <p>HEROES OF THE CHARGE.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL MOORE.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Constable Who Was</p>
        <p>Crushed.</p>
        <p>Corporal John Henry Moore, in, Fennel- street, formerly a police-constable, who did duty oil the Cheshire Lines Station at War- rington, and who has more recently worked in the same capacity at the Central, Manchester, was one of the many Warrington Territorials who were crushed by the falling in of the trench barricades under the force of the German bombardment. His back was badly damaged. He was sent to Boulogne and then to liphall, Edinburgh, where his mother has visited him this week and found him to be improving very well indeed. Corporal Moore, who is 32 years of age, is a native of Warrington, and as a boy attended the National School. He is a St. John Ambu- lance man and is well-known in Manchester</p>
        <p>CORPORAL J. 11. MOORE. Wounded.</p>
        <p>Married six years ago, and has three little children. He is on the Liberal roll of honour —as also are his two brothers, now serving with Scotch regiments in France and in the Dar- danelles respectively. A native of Warrington, Private Broadhurst is the son of Mr. and Mrs. Frank Broadhurst, of 31, Wellington-street— an employe of the St. Helens Cable Company, and previously a labourer for 17 years at the Dalla.m Forge. His father, continuing the fine political traditions of the family, has for 25 years been Liberal captain of Howley Ward. Five years ago Private Broadhurst joined the local "Terriers,' and up to the outbreak of the war was employed by Messrs. Crosfield's. As a lad he attended the National Bohoola.</p>
