      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>REPORTED KILLED.</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL J. HIGHAM.</p>
        <p>Chum's Letter Breaks Uncorroborated News To Wife.</p>
        <p>"I am very sorry to have to inform you of the death of your husband, Lance-Corporal J. Higham, who was hit by a Piece of shell and was killed instantly; he never spoke a word after being hit. We were chums for years, and as boys we used to sell papers togethei. We fought as men together, but now I am alone! All the,lads in his section were very sorry to lose him."</p>
        <p>Re-called To The Colours When War Broke Out.</p>
        <p>In these words Pte. W. Chadwick, of the Ist East Lancashire Regiment, writing under date July 11, broke to Mrs. Higham,. 13, Eldon-mtreet, the news of the death, in action, of her husband, Lance-Corporal John Higham, of the Ist East</p>
        <p>Reported Killed.</p>
        <p>Lancashire Regiment. No official intimation hus yet been received from the War Office. Lance-Corporal Highain, who was 27 years of age, was a reservist, and on the outbreak of war he was called up, he having previously on sick leave. He was before the war employed by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, and had pre- viously worked at Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Son's. He ,was educated. at the Heathside Council School. A brother Of ,his, Private Thomas Higham, is serving with the 3rd South Lancs. Regiment.</p>
        <p>Army and Navy, his brother-in-law, Sergeant Creamer, of the Warrington Territorials, hav- ing recently been wounded. His father 'was an old Volunteer. Acting-Corporal Brobbin formerly belonged to the merchant service and has travelled nearly all over the world.</p>
