        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL J. ITIGFIAM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Member of An Old Warrington Catholic Family.</head>
        <p>Member of An Old Warrington Catholic Family.</p>
        <p>He belonged to an old and respected War- rington Catholic. family and was only 32 years of age. He received his education at St.. Ben- edict's School, and many will remember him as an excellent athlete, being connected with the Warrington Catholic Cricket Club and other sporting organisations: Two brothers of this gallant citizen-soldier are also on active ser- vice—Vombardier Thomas Stirrup, R.G.A., who is in the Dardanelles, and Private William Stirrup, of the Manchester Regiment, who has been invadided from the Front and is in hos- pital in the South of England. The mother and family, who live at 46, Pinner's-broW, have been the recipients of much sympathy in their deep sorrow.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN CARTWRIGHT.</p>
        <p>Nineteen-Year-Old Hero Of More Than One Battle Hit In The Led.</p>
        <p>Private John Cartwright, of the 2nd South ancashires, whose home is with his mother, sirs. M. Cartwright, at 27, Dixon-street, has .)ean -wounded. Ile joined the Special Reserve year last January, and he was called up for Active service on August Bth, and, after a period of training, crossed to France in Janu- try of the present year. Educated at the Silver-street School, he for-</p>
        <p>merly worked for Messrs. Rylands Bros., and latterly .at the Cable Works. He will not be twenty years of age until next November. Popular with his workmates and with a wide ,ircle of friends, •Private Cartwright, in the piping times of peace, was an enthusiastic :.,yclist, whilst he was an habitue of the local rinks during the short:lived roller-skating 2raze and was 'considered an adept "rinker." lie had passed scathless through several fights before an enemy-bullet laid him hors de com- bat, including the big battle which was fought on May Day. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>No Cause for Uneasiness.</head>
        <p>No Cause for Uneasiness.</p>
        <p>News that Private Cartwright had been wounded was first conveyed to his mother by his Sergeant—E. H. Herbert—who, writing on the 10th of the present month, said: "Your son, Jack, has asked me .to let you know that he has been wounded in the leg . . . . but he is quite comfortable and you have no cause for uneasiness." A day or so prior to receiving</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>Wounded</p>
        <p>Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, had been wounded. Later, Private Clyne,s. himself wrote home, and said that a shell dropped in be- tween about twenty soldiers—he being among them—and wounded them all.</p>
