        <p>PRIVATE EDWARD CLYNES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>BANDSMEN D.C.M.'S.</head>
        <p>BANDSMEN D.C.M.'S.</p>
        <p>rdlerrters Of 2nd South Lancs. Band Honoured.</p>
        <p>Two members of the Band of the 2nd Batta- lion of the South Lancashire Regiment, which is at present quartered in Orford Barracks, and which is under the conductorship of Mr. J. e. Cahill, have been awarded the Distin- guished Conduct Medal. They are Lance- Corporal' Yorke and Lance-Corporal A. Cleve- land.</p>
        <p>Lance-:CorpOral Yorke gained the honour :for conspicuous gallantry on October 21st, when he returned, under heavy fire, to a. house to see if it contained a wounded- sergeant. He has since shown greAtt bravery on many occasions as a stretcher-bearer, notably at Ypres in November. Amongst the brave actions of Lance-Corporal Yorke is that told in the fol- lowing incident :—A number of wounded men were in a house in the firing-line. They were crying for Water: Two men set off to obtain some, but were soon shot down. Lance-Corpl. Yorke seized a. eouple of enamelled pails' and set off regardless of a terrific fire. He survived and brought the water back to the wounded men.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal. Yorke is 21 years of age. He has been at the Front from the first. Starting is fir,boy in the band, he has seen seven years' service.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal' A. Cleveland obtained his D.C.M. for conspicuous gallantry on many occasions • when acting as a stretcher-bearer, Particularly near Ypres in November, when he headed' hiS party several times, crossing the firing zone and bringing in the wounded. Lance-Corporal Cleveland is 27 years of age. He went out with the original Expeditionary Forte.- Almost the whole of the members, of the band are either men invalided from the Front or youths whose age makes them as yet ineligible 'for active service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>MISSING!</head>
        <p>MISSING!</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN BRIMELOW.</p>
        <p>;Last , SeenAinnning On Towards The</p>
        <p>Germans.</p>
        <p>Private John,Biimelow, son of Mr. and Mrs. Richard Brimelow, of 22, Pickmere-street, War- rington, is amongst the missing. After the issue of the official list relating to the casualties on June 16th, a message came fiona, a friend of Private BrimTlow's at the Front to say that though he had been wounded, he was now reported missing. The anxious parents were informed that they might be able to obtain some information as to the missing lad from a Corporal J. Lloyd. The latter, in response to a letter from Mr. and Mrs. Brimelow, wrote: "I'm sorry I can't give any definite information. The last I saw of your son was when we were rushing from the second line of trenches.. Then I got him. I saw him still running on in front. That was the last I saw of him."</p>
        <p>The writer of this letter advised the parents to write to the various hospitals in France for information, but though this course has been adopted, no information has been forthcoming so far.</p>
        <p>Corporal Lloyd cOncludes his letter as fol- lows: "Please accept my heartfelt sympathy in your great anxiety. We were good pals to- gether, and have done tialish jobs together." Private Brimelow's four years in the Terri- torials had nearly expired when the war broke out. He was employed by Messrs. Kenyon and Co., oil merchants. Twenty-two years of age, Private Arimelow belonged to Wigan, but at- tended at one time the Bank Quay Mission Sunday School. Another association he had with the Mission was as a member of the foot- ball club. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PRIVATE NORMAN WILSON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE NORMAN WILSON.</p>
        <p>Waved To A Cothrade And Was Seen</p>
        <p>No More.</p>
        <p>There is a good deal of doubt as to the fate that has overtaken Private Norman Wilson, of 6, Selby-street. Writing to his own par- ents, Private James Battersby hays :—" I have personally approached Adjutant-Captain Izod, and he was kind enough to look up Private Wilson's records. I am sorry to say he has found no trace of him. I think he must be one of the unfortunate five of whom we have no tidings. Captain Izod wishes you to con- vey the sad news to his mother as gently as possible. He was a good soldier and liked by all. Such men as he can ill be spared." Private Will Jones, a friend of Private Wil- son's, with whom he went out to the Front, writes:—</p>
        <p>"Dear Mrs. Wilson,—l received your letter and am sorry to say I know nothing at all about Norman. I saw him once, early in the morning, just as we got the order to advance. He seemed to be waiting for somebody, because while we were advancing he stood still at the top of the trench. He shouted to me, waved his hand and started laughing. I waved back and never saw or heard anything of him after- wards. . . I wrote to our platoon ser-</p>
        <p>geant, to see if he knows anything I am coming home in a few days and will call on you." Private Wilson worked, prior to the war, at Messrs. Croafield's Soap Works as a cooper. As a lad he attended St. Barnabas' -School. He was a member of a local junior football club. Ire has two brothers with the colours—Private Alfred, Wilson, of the 2/4th Battalion outh Lancashire Regiment, and Bombardier Fred Wilson, of the R.F.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ARTHUR NAYLOR.</head>
        <p>ARTHUR NAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Prominent Warrington Footballer Returns To The Front To-day. _ _</p>
        <p>Private Arthur Naylor, the popular: War- rington Rugby player, who has been at 'home this week at 18, Church-street, after a pqriod in hospital, leaves again for the trenches to-dlgy Sixteen years ago Private Naylor started playing for Warrington, and he has since be- come one of the favourites of the Wilderspool crowd. It is many years ago since he joined the regiment with which he went out to the Front shortly after the outbreak of war—the Grenadier Guards. Before receiving his wound, Private Naylor saw a lot of fighting, from Ypres to NeuVe Chapelle, in which engage- ment the Warrington Territorials were just be- hind his own regiment. Private Naylor was wounded at Neuve Chapelle, but only slightly, and it was not till some days later that he received the wound that eventually brought him home.</p>
        <p>Private Naylor is married, but has no family. He thinks that, judging from what he has seen, we are getting on all right. The Germans, he says, are not as keen as they were earlier, and show great pleasure at being captured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>GOOD NEWS FOR A</head>
        <p>GOOD NEWS FOR A</p>
        <p>MOTHER.</p>
        <p>Soldier Who Was Reported Dead Is</p>
        <p>Alive And VVell.</p>
        <p>One mother's heart is glad this week. Mrs. Sayers, of Manley-street, Warrington, has re- ceived news that compensates her for much suffering and anxiety. Some little while back her son—Private Sayers, of the Warrington Territorials—was reported killed, and prayers were said for the repose of the soul of the young soldier at the Catholic Church he had previously attended. On Tuesday morning Mrs. Sayers received a letter to say that her son was a prisoner of war in Germany.</p>
        <p>iris- or =or 1101111111-411111111</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>PRIVATE ARTHUR STIRRUP.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ARTHUR STIRRUP.</p>
        <p>Old 4Varrhigtonian Who Volunteered</p>
        <p>'‘'''‘iith Canadians.</p>
        <p>The valour of the Canadian contingent will jive for ever when the history of the war is written, and a son of Warringtoh, in the person of Private Arthur Stirrup, who was killed in action on May 22nd in Flanders, will for all time connect the old town with that glory. Private Stirrup was attached to the 16th Canadian Scottish, one of the Canadian units, which, by their indomitable pluck and gallantry; saved the British lines after the French were forced back by the barbarous uoie of asphyxiating gases \ in the last German desperate attempt to occupy Ypres, and which has been clearly chronicled ,in Sir John French's dispatch. Private Stirrup was an old Volunteer, being amongst those who oriinally constituted the Mounted Infantry Section, being present at several annual camps, notably that in the Isle of Man, where he was person- ally complimented for efficiency by Major Cros- field. Sacrificed All to Join the Canadians.</p>
        <p>lie left Warrington, where he had been engaged as a joiner by Messrs. Dolan, Fair- clough and others, in 1905 for the United States to seek a broader• scope for his energies and ambitions. For over nine years he successfully pursued his trade in Massachussets, Colorado and other States, and so well did he earn 'the confidence and respect of his colleagues that he was appointed President of the Confedera- tion of Labour at Denver. When war was declared in August last he sacrificed every- thing and hastened at once to Canada to join the Colonial Forces of the Motherland. After a course of training in Canada and a short</p>
        <p>-or</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Reservist Who Was Called Up.</head>
        <p>A Reservist Who Was Called Up.</p>
        <p>Private Clynes, who is 24 years .of age, was a reservist—having previously served six Years in the Army—and upon. the outbreak of war he was called Up. He was employed by the St. Helens Cable and /tubber .Company, and received his education at St_ Mary's Roman Catholic School, Buttermarket-street. He has five relatives serving with .the colours, one of whom is a prisoner of war in Germany. Two of th others—Private Joseph I.owe and Private Thomas Kiniblin—have recently been wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>PRIVATE W. S. BOARIiNIAN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. S. BOARIiNIAN.</p>
        <p>R.A.M.C. Man Contracts Severe. Illness And Is Sent Home.</p>
        <p>Private William Stanley Boardman, of the R.A.M.C., attached to the West Kent Regiment, served in the Army for eight years. . He was wounded recently and came over to England and son of Mr. J. Boardman, of 14, Norris-street, Warrington, is at present in hospital in London. Eight weeks ago he was admitted into the hos- pital at. Boulogne suffering from enteric fever. He is now, however, almost better again, and he writes home ,to say that he hopes to te in Warrington on furlough, in a few days' time. Whilst at the Front, Private Boardman wrote several interesting letters home, one of which ran as follows :—" I suppose you read of the desperate fighting around the Yser Canal. Well, my regiment were there, and we had a pretty rough time. We of the R.A.M.C. had plenty of wounded soldiers to deal with, but, happily, none were very seriously hurt. They included Frenchmen and blacks. The black soldiers are very funny people. I suppose you read about the famous charge my regiment</p>
        <p>,1111111111111111</p>
        <p>-anirins-wiirvirr-- -Noir —ix</p>
        <p>made at Hill 60. I think it was about tho worst fight I have been concerned in. Some Queer Sleeping Places.</p>
        <p>"The Germans threw over their poisonous shells. The summer is on now, and we will be able to live out in the open, which will be quite a change from the old barns in which we have been sleeping. I have slept in many queer places—f ones, pastures, barns, houses, churches, schools. dug-outs, carts, canvas huts, and many other places." Private Boardman, who is 19 years of age, I.as been in the Army about two years, and has been at the Front since the commencement of the war. He received his education at the Technical ,School in Rylands-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>STORIES FROM THE TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>STORIES FROM THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>STONE WALL BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>Germans Know That They Are Beaten.</p>
        <p>We have received the following letter from Rifleman C. Scruton; 2169, C Company, 4th Battalion Royal Rifle. Brigade, British E.F. • "Sir,—Being a regular reader of 'The Examiner,' I feel I would like to relate a few of my experiences out here in France. I get your most welcome paper every week in the trenches, and I can ten you we look forward to it. I am a native of Warrington, living at 192, Battersby-lane. Wounded at Ypres.</p>
        <p>"Well, Sir, I have now keen out five months, and I can tell you I have -seen a little bit of fighting since, including the battle of Ypres, also St. Eloi, now famously known as the 'Mound of Death.' Unfortunately I got wounded at Ypres on the 3rd May with shrap- nel, but I only got as far as Versailles, anti of course I am now back again in the firing line after being away for five weeks. Well, no one can realise the battle of Yyres, only those who witnessed it; and it will long live in history as one of the fiercest battles fought. For 38 continuous days. and nights this awful thing went on. First of all, as you all know, they started with their gas. Next thing they were shelling Ypres with their 17in. howitzers, which have been known to make a hole with one shell 261 t. deep and 32ft. wide. It was an inspiring sight to see old men, old women and children dragging at their heels on tramp to places of safety. Of course the people consisted only of old people and babies, because, all the youths were already in the Belgian Army. The bom- bardment which followed I shall never forget. They shelled Ypres for eight days without stop- ping, to prevent our transports and reinforce- ments coming up, and then they started their mission ,which the Mad Doi of Europe related .to them: 'Ypres at all costs.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Tricks That Fail.</head>
        <p>Tricks That Fail.</p>
        <p>1` They started advancing under cover of their gas shells; and. then, they bombarded us from first thing iii the rimming, daybreak, till dusk at night: few. 30 days; aliosdOses of shrapnel dur- ;ing the'nikht. When they advanced they were taking the uniform off dead British soldiers and advancing to try and deceive us, bitt the British soldier is not so slow,. because the Germans' dastardly-looking fares 4 give them away. Of course we let them get tb within 50 yards of our trenches, and oh, you should have seen them run. Well, to speak the truth, Jack Donaldson's 100 yards record was beaten</p>
        <p>times over that day. That game is played out now. 'We are the Coldstream Guards.' Any- way, Sir, our brigade, the 80th, was re-named after this as the 'Stone Wall Brigade' by Sir John French, and right proud of us the Generals were, as it was such an important issue at stake.</p>
        <p>"Well, it was the same with this brigade at St. Eloi in March. Old soldiers who were in the retirement from lions and also in the battles of Marne and Aisne say that Ypres puts them all in the shade, and so the Generals say that this brigade withstood the fiercest bom- bardment the war has,-yet known. "I can tell you we now have the best of the argument, and it's only a matter of time ere we are victorious, and those dirty, inhuman, good-for-nothing swine' know that they sire beaten, but they try to screen themselves and make out they are confident of victory. Well, 'wait and see.'_ Everything shall come right in</p>
        <p>the end. So,I must now draw to a close, Sir it is now Sunday morning and na WO am out of the trenches for a couple of days ve are going to church. So good-bye, wishing your paper every success. "Believe me to be, "Yours, "C. Scßirrow. 2169."</p>
