 . ....!-',,,,i.i. ...,:••• ::.:,:-::: •••• :-:,-;:i:.:.•, :.-:::q:?.:•.::m.i,:,(?.,...;w: It ..::::,:!::;:::!.:g.,::E... ..,:',-..,,,, :,,,,,,x..:•-, .., ~,, , ....„.••• •:•,..„•• ••:.,.. 4,,..:::0•A:::•::::.:::, ••••••,...xs,:x•-•,•,...,14,-.,•„.,,,,ti.• -,:i::.• ,*.:,::i.....,:: , , .k.,... :,,,i:iatliii:imi,ve.:::::::ii.ii-z?,ii..-A ..............,...:.:„,:„•,......,.,..;,.,:t.?:].•.,.:,,1',7,:i.,§•:,...': ,-,?:i, .:,...•,,, . •Iliiiii...w:N...l N* :**:*.::i.., ::•:::::;.....'.::::,,,'::',.:s .:::.,-: ': ~... ~",-.1•1:,.. \-:.•,.,.. • :::::::... -.4::. ::::... ....... , -.:':i.:•::?*.i:::.':**.:.::-' ..:, :.:-•:: tr,",:....'X.' -..r.i,..'*:•:•.,.::....•?,:*. '".:.:::: 'ii,',i:: . f..::K::. ''''. § .....:'•'ft.::::"A::.:"'":'' ..,::•::::.:•...:':1:4::::%:'..-. L VA' ' •..i.:ksf. l &lt;,1ft.„....,................ f --,.%'......' •.:::::.E.::::i:.:?50, -..., -::::... . . .. .</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN SCRITTON. Wounded at Ypres.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Enlisted Soon After the Outbreak of War.</head>
        <p>Enlisted Soon After the Outbreak of War.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Scruton, who is 18 years of age, enlisted soon after the outbreak of war, and while at the Front, be has been wounded, and has had his eyes temporarily damaged by poisonous gases. He was formerly employed by Afessrs. Armitage and Rigby as a piecer. lie was educated at the Hamilton-street School, and worshipped at St. George's Mission Church. His father, who is now dead, was a member of the King's Liverpool Regiment, sand waa statilned at Orford Barracks. He has two step-brothers and two brothers-in-law with the Colours, one or whom is serving at the Front with the War- rington.Territorials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>"PLUCK AND COURAGE."</head>
        <p>"PLUCK AND COURAGE."</p>
        <p>Orford St. John% Player Visits Enem3</p>
        <p>Trenches.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal F. Smith, 21, Notris-street,</p>
        <p>serving with the Royal Munsters, writes to his brother, Private John Smith, Ammunition Depot, Birmingham, and says:— "I have received all your papers and letters, but I was in the trenches at the time, so could not answer before now. A Friend Indeed.</p>
        <p>"While we were going to the trenches I met Wm. Coulson, of Warrington, who is in the A.S.C., and he knew me quite well. I can tell you he was a good friend to me. He lives in Gorsey-lane. We were just finishing a march of about 12 miles in the blazing sun and the heat of the afternoon, and we stopped in a big lane to have our tea, and that is where I met him. I was feeling a little downhearted and ,cold with the pressure and perspiration. All my underclothing was soaked, so he gave me a complete change of underclothing and a jolly good wash, which was better to me than .21,000 under the circumstances. Not only that, he had just received a parcel from home, and, of course, you know the rest of the doings. So we continued our journey to the trenches, and I had a fine experience. "As soon as I arrived there, that would be about midnight, I was taken for listening post, but we were only about 50 yards in front, and it was very quiet, so when morning caxne I thought I would go scouting a bit on may own. I got over the barricade and began to crawl towards the German lines, but not before I had asked God's permission and guidance. So I didn't care where I went, for I knew I was quite safe. Obtained Valuable Information.</p>
        <p>"Anyway, I got out about 500 yards (the enemy's trench at this point is about 800 yards) and watched for about an hour. Then I came back with a few souvenirs, such as a German bayonet, scabbard and a couple of German belts, and a German officer's shoulder-strap, which you will find enclosed, and several other filings. The officer got to know about me going out and said he had no objection, so I went out again in the afternoon and was out for about two hours and I came back with a sketch of the enemy's trench. The officer asked me a few questions about them which I could not answer, so he asked me if I would go out again</p>
        <p>and find out a little more and he would report it to the commanding officer, so I did. "I came back and drew a fine map with al, the details, which he sent with a report to the commanding officer, which afterwards went to the General, and he said he was very pleased indeed. Anyway, an officers' patrol was to go out reconnoitring with me, so we had a .fine time, going within fifty yards of their trench. I got some very valuable information, finding out the enemy's machine gun embasement and listening post. The officer said he would see what he could do as far as recommending me for my pluck and courage."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>"IN CONTACT WITH A SHELL."</head>
        <p>"IN CONTACT WITH A SHELL."</p>
        <p>" Tommy's " Laconic Description Of A Narrow Escape.</p>
        <p>"Private john WikOck, whose home is at 13. Arnold-street, and who is serving at the Front with the 2n.d Smith Lancashires, writing to Mr. A. Prescott (the new landlord of the Mersey Hotel, in succession to the late Mr. Burke), tenders his thanks for a gift of tobacco for- warded him,. and adds that at the time it was sent lie was in the hands of -the Field Ambu- lance Brigade, having come into contact with a 'shell the enemy had sent over. "Still," he goes on, "although it did damage me, I was on the whole very lucky indeed; for the same shell killed three of my chums outright. It gave me a bad shaking, but lam all right now. . . There will be Many families in mourning in Warring- ton owing to the 'Terriers" losing so heavily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>"All Credit to the Brave Lads!"</head>
        <p>"All Credit to the Brave Lads!"</p>
        <p>"All credit to the bravo lads. It was a grand charge and the memory of it will always Jive. I have seen the lot of the survivors since then, and they all look 'in the pink.' No doubt you will have seen a few of them, as they have been and are coming on leave."</p>
        <p>_ Private Wilcock joined It itchener's Army early on in the war, and prior to doing so was employed in, the Electricity Department ef the Corporation. He is a married man. In a postscript to his letter to Mr. Prescott. the writer mentions that he has been able to follow the doings of many local lads tlbough the columns of "The Examiner," which is regularly sent to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>BRAVE RIFLEMAN.</head>
        <p>BRAVE RIFLEMAN.</p>
        <p>Betvsey Forgeman Carries Messages</p>
        <p>Under Shell-Fire.</p>
        <p>Rifleman David Smith, of the Ist Rifle Bri- gade, 30, Leonard-street, Warrington, a furnaceman employed by the Pearson and Knowles' Coal and Iron Company, who joined the Army last September and went to Fiance in January, has written home to say that he has gained the good opinion of his captain by successfully carrying out some dangerous duty. Rifleman Smith, who is 32 years of age, is a Forester and a member of the Conservative Club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Officer's Praise.</head>
        <p>Officer's Praise.</p>
        <p>Writing under date July 12th, to his wife, Le _says:— -</p>
        <p>"I expect you will see a little bit about us in the newspapers this week. I was well into it, and I am proud to tell you that I am get- ting recommended by our captain. I don't know what it will be, but our ,captain called me the other day and said: 'Smith, I have found' you a job as orderly, and I shall recom- mend you well for what you did the other day.' I ran across the field with messages twice while the shells were bursting over my head ,and bullets whizzing around me. Two men with me were wounded, but I got through twice without a scratch. I am proud of myself for what I have done—it is a great honour to be recoiiimended</p>
        <p>"When I went to see him he tapped me on the shoulder and said: 'Well done, Smith! I am very 'proud of you.' He got hold of my hand and very near shook my arm off.</p>
        <p>"We have come out of the trenches for a bit of a rest. I am in the pink of c6ndition, and I am glad to hear you are going on all right at home. "I might get recommended for furlough as well, which I should like; but we will have to wait and see."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>"ATE SAND WITH EVERY MEAL."</head>
        <p>"ATE SAND WITH EVERY MEAL."</p>
        <p>Ambulance Man's Experiences With</p>
        <p>The Mediterranean Force.</p>
        <p>Writing to a friend in. Warrington, Private Tom Handles, Son of Mr. and Mrs. Handles, of Percy-street, and who is a bearer serving with the Ist Field Ambulance attached to the R.N. Division of the Mediterranean Expeditionary Force, now engaged against the Turks, says : "We had it rough coming, through the Bay of Bis- cay, and I can tell you, we did look nice squatted about all over the deck moaning and groaning. Still ... . I soon got over my sea-sickness. . . We made a , stop at Malta, putting in at St. Paul's Bay, where they have a statue of the Saint from whom the bay takes its name. It is there that the Saint is supposed to have been shipwrecked. From the bay we passed into the Grande Harbour and were permitted one night ashore. . . . Some few days later, having reached the scene of the Dardanelles operibOns, we went into action for the grst time. . . . We had but few casualties. We next went tarci-- and then had a route march. We remained in this town two days, and from there sailed to , where we spent a fortnight under can- vas, our camp being on the sands. .. . Only Two Things to Grumble At.</p>
        <p>"There were only two things to grumble about—the flies and the sand. We atcbaand with every meal, and our eyes got filled up with it. The natives hereabouts neither trouble to keep themselves or their town clean. . . . Re.. embarking, after several days, we went to the help of a tnair-o'-war that had been torpedoed by the enemy, and then we made for the Galli- poli peninsula, where we landed. Since then we, have had some hard and exciting work to perform andl have seen some awful sights. The Field Ambulance to which I am attached has suffered a few casualties at the hands of snipers and from shrapnel."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WYCLIFFE BOY</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE BOY</p>
        <p>Promptly Answers Master's Call For,</p>
        <p>Names.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday Mr. S. W. Jeffery inserted RR advertisement in "The Examiner" asking for the names of old scholars of Wycliffe- Day School (of which he is the headmaster) wile are serving with the colours, the object belay to draw up a roll of honour of old boys. Yesterday .he received the first reply—in lees than a week after publication—showing at once that "The Examiner" reaches the trenches quickly, and that the postal organisa- tion each way is well organised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>THROUGH THE CHARGE WITHOUT A – SCRATCH.</head>
        <p>THROUGH THE CHARGE WITHOUT A - SCRATCH.</p>
        <p>The letter runs:—</p>
        <p>"Thursday, July 15th. "Dear Sir,—l have read in this last week- end's Examiner' about you asking for names of old scholars of your school that are serving their King and 'country. ;I believe the Idea is to make a roll of honour of the names, and being an old day school and also Sunday school scholar, I thought I would write to you just to ask you if you would put my name on the list. I am out in Flanders, serving with eur local Territorials, and have been out with them since they left England. I took part in the great charge, and I can thank God that I was one of the lucky ones to escape without a scratch. I am servant to one of the best officers we have—Captain Ridgway—who has been out here with us all the time the battalion haw been abroad.</p>
        <p>"I am hoping to be over in Warrington shortly on leave, and if I am spared I shall try and pay you a visit at the old school. If you have any spare time to write to me I should be very pleased to hear from you. My address is: Private R. Mitchell (No. 1,929), D ' Company, 4th South Lancashire Regiment, France.—Hoping you are keeping in the best of health, as this leaves me in the best of spirits, I repain, yours sincerely, Itowun Dirrcuzuft"</p>
        <p>'</p>
        <p>';•:;1</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>t I '</p>
        <p>'</p>
      </div>
