      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>I_4Y MM</head>
        <p>I_4Y MM</p>
        <p>Colonel Groves' Visit.—Colonel 'Groves, of :the sth Cheshires, paid a brief and unexpected visit home to Dean's Green this week, leaving for the Front again a day or two later.</p>
        <p>More Recruits.—Four More Lymm men—all employes of the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany—have this week enlisted in the Regular Army for duration of the war. Of three others who have joined the Cheshire Yeomanry, two belong to the Wednesday Football Club. The morning after recrditing the four Ship Canal men were notified by pest that they would- have. to get from the company permis- e3ion to enlist. This was not granted, but they have been provided with war-badges .to show that they cannot be spared.</p>
        <p>Territorial Visitors Depart.—The hundred- and-four Northwich and district 3/sth Cheshire Territorials; who 'spent a fortnight billeted in Lymm under the command of, tieutenants Milne and Armitage, left early on saturday morning for Chester. They have been exceedingly "well done to" by everybody in Lymm, and several letters of appreciation have been received from them by the people with whom they were billeted. The Churches also have been heartily thanked.</p>
        <p>Volunteers Busy As Ever.—This week two further honours have been gained in musketry in the Lyrnm detachment of the Cheshire Vol- unteer Regiment. J. Jelley has qualified as a marksman with 99 deliberate and 96 rapid; whilst J. T. Milnes—one of the oldest 'members —has become a first-class shot with 97 deliber- ate and 89 rapid.—On Tuesday night the 'usual drills took place in the Drill Hall, and on Thursday evening some smart attacking and manceuvring was gone through on the Golf Links. This afternoon the men are to fall in at two o'clock and march to Altrincham. where they will meet the battalion in the Market Square, afterwards proceeding to Cheadle for extended order drill. The battalion band will be in attendance, and the Lynam men will be commanded by Captain George Williamson and Sergeants S. D. Moston and J. Lister- Farrar.</p>
        <p>FIIIE AT •OLD TANNERY.</p>
        <p>Roof-Ottibreak Promptly Quelled R. - Brigade.</p>
        <p>An outbreak of fire o-;..curred early on Sunday afternoon at the Old Tannery House, Cherry- lane—the residence of Mr. Johnson. The fire- bell was promptly,- rung, and the brigade turned out in charge of the Superintendent, Mr. William Kirkpatrick. On arrival they found the roof at the east gable-end of the house on fire; the overhanging gable-roof is of lath and plaster, with barge-board. Happily, a very good supply of water was available from the Old Tannery reservoir, whence it was pumped through four hundred yards of hose. The ladders were quickly run up, and within two hours the brigade was able to return home. having effectively extinguished the flame and allayed the danger of any further outbreak. The cause of the conflagration is not known, although it has been said that a bird's nest near the chimney of a bedroom stove caught fire and was responsible for it. • This is, of course, only a ..urmise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>YOUTHFUL THIEVES.</head>
        <p>YOUTHFUL THIEVES.</p>
        <p>Changed Note For Money, Shared And</p>
        <p>Hid It.</p>
        <p>At the• Council Offices on Wednesday morning, a Juvenile Court was held, Messrs. T. J. Ridg- way and'S. W. Gould being on the Bench. Two boys, each aged thirteen years, were charged with stealinc, on Tuesday, the 6th,. a .4.'5 note— the property of Jane Brazendale, of South View, Booth's Hill. They admitted the theft. The Entrusted Luggage.</p>
        <p>From evidence given by Miss Brazendale, Porter Horace KnoWles and Acting-Sergeant Griffiths, it appeared that on the night in ques- tion Miss Brazendale arrived at Lymm Station at 7.45 and handed over her luggage to Porter Knowles to be taken to her residence. Knowles in turn handed it over to two boys to take- for him—as they said they were going past the place and offered to take it for him. In the meantime she made a call, and on arriving home examined her bag, and found that one of two ,t 5 notes was missing. The follow- ing day she reported the loss- to the police, and Acting-Sergeant Griffiths found after inquiry that the boys had had the note changed into silver at a local bank. The boys at first denied all knowledge of the note, but when confronted with each other they confessed to 'having taken the note and divided the money with two other boys.</p>
        <p>The boys were ordered respectively to receive twelve and eight strokes of the birch.rod. Chairman's Reason for Leniency. '</p>
        <p>The other two boys, were at the same time, charged with receiving atl. 6s. sd. and £1 Is. .9d. respectively, but got off with a severe caution. They said they were told by the other two that the money had been found—they having seen a lady drop it out of her satchel. At first they advised the two to report the matter to the police, but the reply was: No;" what's the use of telling the police? Maybe we should only get about .21 reward, and we've got X 5 now !" They shared the money at noon the following day. They hid the money in the plantation off Eagle Brow. Mr. Ridgway said his reason for taking a lenient view in the . ease of the two who received the money was that their first wish had been to do the right thing and report the matter to the police.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>LYMM AND WAR LOAN.</head>
        <p>LYMM AND WAR LOAN.</p>
        <p>Friendly Societies Patriotically Invest Large Sums. In addition to many investments in the new war loan on the part of private Lymm indi- viduals, some of the local Friendly Societies and, other bodies have taken an interest in the scheme.</p>
        <p>Recently the Lymm Lodge members of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds held a. special meeting at the Lymm Temperance Hotel on Saturday evening to consider the financial position of the Lodge and the War Loan. On the motion of P.M.. Joseph Brazen- dale, seconded by P.M. Wm. Ratcliffe. sen.., it was resolved .that the sum '.of X3OfY be invested: in the loan. This has been. done , by. the Treasurer, Mr. Arthur 'Sutton. The atrionnt'i4 particularly creditable, as the Lrmin only has a membership of fifty—and the sum invested by no means represents the total of their resources.</p>
        <p>Last week the local district of the Ancient Order of Foresters—of .which Mr. Arthur W. Holt is secretary—held a meeting at Altrinc- ham, and we understand that as ,a result the district intend to invest a sum in the loan. The Lymm and • Altrincham districts, of course, are. joihed together—so the sum should be a large one. .. , _</p>
        <p>The' Warburton Lodge of the .Ancient .Order of Druids—unlike • the, Warrington Equalised District of the A.O.D.—are not investing any money in the loan. They hare, 'however, only a small membership and form part of the Warrington District. Mr. Derbyshire is their secretary, and Mr. Walter Seymour their treasurer.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Whitlow, treasurer of the Lymm Lodge of Oddfellows, M.U., L0.0.F., informs us that although his society are not taking any action on their own, the Manchester Unity— to which they belong—has invested £200,000. We are informed that a Lymm farmer has taken out £2,000 worth of shares in the loan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>LYMM TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>LYMM TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>LEAVE ONE SET OF TRENCHES TO ENTER OTHERS.</p>
        <p>The Lymm "Terriers" at the Front left the trenches on Saturday, after a fortnight in them; but are expecting to enter some others not far away.</p>
        <p>Private Percy Clare': writing on the 6th to Miss Bessie Cross, of Bank Cottage, Bridge- water-street, .said:. `.The boys are in the pink. Well, I can fancy what Lymm was like when our Captain and Harold arrived home; but you see the holiday is all over . now and we are expecting them back with us again . to- night. Some more left the trenches yesterday to go on leave. Over here it is too hot to walk about?'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Marvellous Trench-Plans.</head>
        <p>Marvellous Trench-Plans.</p>
        <p>The next day word from the Private ran "We are still up at the trenches. Captain Hartley and Harold landed . here last night; thy were both ready for a good sleep. It was hard lines coming back to the trenches, but the trip was worth going for! Roll on the time when it is our turn! I think it will come, with a bit of luck! You see, in twelve hours we can • leave the trenches and be in England. We can get in and out of our trenches any time in the day. It is really marvellous. We have trenches running from the fire-trench, so that we can walk in and not be .seen. In fact, it is worse at night- time than in the clay; ,there is hardly •a shot in the daytime—only shell-fire; and we chance that! But at night it is like walking round a bee's' nest; the bullets came all ways. . . We have just been for a swim in the river, and without a word of a lie it is not two minutes' walk from the trenches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>As Much Fun As In Lymm Canal</head>
        <p>As Much Fun As In Lymm Canal</p>
        <p>"There ;is a big lock-how and a big wall ..ait the top of the river, and we get in behind this; and have as much fun as though we were in the canal at Lymm. But if wf just go on the top of the bank the A.llemans' (as the Bel- gians say) can see us; Sowe keep down. . . I think we are going into some fresh trenches this week-end, but in the same place—not far from ihere. They say . . we are going to see W. Madden and J. Thomason; we shall all he very -glad. to see them. . . . Men are what we want here, though it is not a nice job to come to; but we must all 'buck up,' as it is for a good and noble cause. . . I wrote to George Appleton the other day, and had an answer yesterday. He is going on all right. Poor George! I wish lie was with us; it would be better for him, and I should like bo see him. I hare a letter from W. Jackson; lie is doing fine in Surrey." Further news, dated the 10th, was received on Tuesday. "We arrived safe and sound out of the trenches again, and We are in the fields. We are haying some grand weather now. . . We were glad when our Captain got back safely to us again, as he is a Captain we are all proud of. We have got two more Lymm lads with us—W. Hadden and J. Tho- ihason. They came to us when we came out of the trenches."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>An Expected Bombardment.</head>
        <p>An Expected Bombardment.</p>
        <p>Private John Thomason, in a letter written on the 7th to his wife at New-road; says:— " I am quite well and safe for the present. We are expecting to go to you will very likely get some news of a bom- bardment. Jimmy Leah is coming home this week-end for a few days. The first man I saw here was Tommy Kirk—making a cross for an officer who was killed last week. Tommy does not look very well. 'Fags' are fourpence, a packet here, and we cannot go for them -unless there are four men and a corporal; because the; Germans are shelling the place where they sell them. . We can hear the guns going off about every two minutes; they are only three miles away from where we are now, but we shall,be a bit nearer towards the week-end."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>"Way Back In Dug-outs."</head>
        <p>"Way Back In Dug-outs."</p>
        <p>Private Ernest lladden, in a communication to his mother at Pepper-street, says: "I hear you have got soldiers with you. Don't forget how well I was treated 4wherever I went. I hope they are comfortable. . . Sergeant Clare is back again, but is with the Lymm lads a little way back in 'dug-outs' only. J. Read and I are in the trench as servants. Things are very quiet at present, and we have not had many casualties; we lost two officers the first two days—one killed and one wounded. . I am lost for news. . . ."</p>
        <p>Captain G. W. C. Hartley has evidently al- ready resumed his former duties, for this letter has been " passed " by him. Lymm Man as Cook.</p>
        <p>Corporal Jack 11. Moston writes: "I am keeping well and going on all right. I have been put on brigade-guard; so I am away from the Lymm lads at present. We are staying at a big farm just behind the firing line. There are fifteen of us in all—a sergeant, myself a lance-corporal and twelve men. We have Joe Thomason as our cook, and I can tell you we are all right. Great Things to Happen in Last Bid For</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Calais.</head>
        <p>Calais.</p>
        <p>"I thought I should have stood a chance of coming over to see oyou, but they .have stopped all. N.C.O.'s for the present, as they are expect- ing great things to come off here in a day or two. I think the Kaiser is going to make his last bid for Calais, as our aininen _have s_e_.n a great many troops behind the German lines. Won't he have 'a suck in'! They will get 'knocked down just like leaves dropping off a tree on /a very windy day! We all know his time has gone taw; it's 110 use his trying, as we have too many guns, but the sooner he tries the sooner the war will finish. So let him start to- morrow!</p>
        <p>"I shall be seeing something of the boys to- night, as they pass -here when they go up to. the trenches at night on fatigue duty.' -I don't like being away from them, but things can't be .helped." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Going on Well.</head>
        <p>Going on Well.</p>
        <p>Private Joseph Clare wrote from the Front on Tuesday to his mother at Booth's Hill: "Things are going on very well here. We have had three, days' fatigue, and Chinney and Jack Thomason have been with us. Rare and well they look; but they will soon find oat here is .not as Cbsy as being'in .England. But it is their duty, and we were pleased to see them. We shall be able, to 'show them the graves of H. Barber and J. Guest if we go in the same trenches .again. , We asked J. Thomason what he had, come out. here for, and he said 'to go 'back v,lth us.' Well. I hope it comes true! And if we can pull through another couple of months I think it will be all *right. Every day we were shelled. No one got hit, but I tell you it got wind up them. It's only what you can expect for the first time. -but they will get 'broke , in; no one likes them dropping near them. Thins will be lively in Lymm now you have a band turning out and playing for you every day. We don't often hear one out here. Well, keep on.smiling !. . . We are still in dug-outs behind the trenches. Georg 2 .is the only Casualty. from .Lymnr." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>"Like Blackmen."</head>
        <p>"Like Blackmen."</p>
        <p>Writing to his mother on Monday from the Front, Private Percy/ Clare said: "We are still in the fields. We came out of the trenches sooner that we expected this time; we thought we were going in for a long spell, but we were only in for a week. I wrote to Geo. Appleton [of Crass Brow, Statham] just before we went up to the trenches, and had a card from him while up there. He says he is going on all right and in the best of health. I shall keep a look-out for him; I don't think he is far from us. Everything is going on well with us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>MOTHER 9 BANISHES .„., – ! . . . SYRUP</head>
        <p>MOTHER 9 BANISHES .„., - ! . . . SYRUP</p>
        <p>fmnimm.mh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <p>• 7."</p>
        <p>_ll;`,'=" M==:=</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
