      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>"I don't know what sort of weather you are having, but here it is too hot to walk about. We shall be like blackm.en if it keeps as hot as it is now. . . .</p>
        <p>"We have done well this turn in the trenches. We only had about half a dozen killed and wounded. It was very kind of Mr, Morris to mend those watches. . . "</p>
        <p>Private William Jackson, who was wounded, writes from the Military Hospital, -Banstead, Surrey, to his parents at 3, Moston-street : "My leg is doing champion, and I am eating ik a horse, so you had better prepare for me when I come home—which will be a fortnight yet or perhaps more. I can't walk yet, and reckon I shall be about another week- beiore t.lwy let me .have a try. The wound is in the way, and when I put my foot down it opens. So you see it will have to be thoroughly bet4r before I can walk. I went for another motor- ride on. Monday and it was all right. We went past Epsom racecourse—where the Derby is run."</p>
        <p>extensions and improvements—including much- needed decoration and increased numbers of wounded patients. Indeed, the general .ex- penses for the half-year—incredible as it may seem—totalled no less than ct 1,000, despite the economy exercised. An appeal is therefore made—and the com- mittee confidently anticipate a generous response—for donations- as liberal as po'Ssible. Funds are urgently needed.</p>
        <p>Well, I should not go that far! I take it that if the deficiency is serious then—from cases you have quoted—it is not a sufhcient defence. It is a question of quality. Mr. Steel: If you come to the conclusion that the deficiency is sci great that the milk could not have been sold as it came from the cow, then you do not accept the evidence for the defence. I think it rather a pity that a sample was not taken from the cow, because it would have been the strongest possible evidence that it was sold as stated. Continuing, Mr. Steel submitted that the deficiency was not a great one. In the months May and June the milk generally was poorer. Mr. Moss's cows were in the very best of condition; they had been fed, housed and looked after in a very good manner. But the weather in which they had been turned out to grass was cold, and their field was ex- posed to the east wind. In any case the fault was not Mr. Holland's, as it was not his cow's milk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>LIEUT. 'H. F. DAVIES.</head>
        <p>LIEUT. 'H. F. DAVIES.</p>
        <p>Killed At Front After Mention In Dispatches.</p>
        <p>Another link between the 1/sth Cheshire Territorials and Lymm has been snapped by the death of Lieutenant 11. F. Davies, who went out to see if the wire entanglements were all right and was shot by several %bullets. - The Lieutenant did not belong to Lyzmn, but to Chester. Ile has, however, become known to several Lymm men, and his name is very familiar as that of one of the most fre- quent letter-censors. Only three weeks ago he. was mentioned in dispatches by, Sir John French for conspicuous bravery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>PRIVATE GEORGE TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE GEORGE TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Bad Accident, But He Will Soon</p>
        <p>Recover.</p>
        <p>Private George Taylor, of Rush Green, is now lying in hospital in France—having been very seriously wounded by slipping on to a bayonet, which passed through his back and stomach. Writing on he 10th to his mother, Mrs. G. Oldham,. Private Taylor said :—" I am recov- ering nidely and I am hoping to be down at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>tz*</p>
        <p>?</p>
