      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>PRIVATE GEORGE TAYLOR,</head>
        <p>PRIVATE GEORGE TAYLOR,</p>
        <p>the base before very long. When we get there we go. to hospitals in England. Don't trouble, niotiter; as I have no doubt I am doing Al so far, and am over the worst now. I have been living on water for a few days, but I am now on milk-diet, and start haVlng food in a day or two. We have the best of stuff—whatever you like; so we are not doing any harm. My bayonet wound is in the stomach, towards the side.. I fell back in the trench at nightime, and did not see the rifle with the bayonet on; that is how it happened. . . Cheer up! .1 will soon he with you now."</p>
        <p>'Private P. Clare writes: "On Sundiy night, 24th inst., George Taylor,, from Rush Green, fell on a bayonet, and it Went in his side. It was a bad wound, but he will soon be all right again." Rather A Big Gash.</p>
        <p>Private J. Clare writes :—" We • were on fatigue difcy carrying things to the trenches, and going over one of- the, trenches there hap- pened to be a bayonet fixed on a rifle. As he was jumping over, he slipped and fell on it. It made rather a big gash, but H. Brookes was soon at .his assistance. He bandaged him Up; then we took him on a stretcher to the dres- sing station, after which he as bungled off to the hospital. It is .not serious. He will get over it all right and land in England shortli."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHN W. JONES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN W. JONES.</p>
        <p>Arrives In London Anti Improves Ver.), Gradually.</p>
        <p>Private John William Jones, of Reddish- view, Rush Green—who, as we stated last week, has had a nervous breakdown following the death of his mate, Private Jack Guest, arrived</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>On the Bench.—At the Daresbury Petty Sessions, held on Tuesday morning at Stockton Heath, there were present on the Bench: Messrs. F. A .Frost, H. Roberts, W. Baxter, J. Dalcin, W. Fletcher, J. Hepherd, C. Jackson Holmes, Roger C. Parr and J. A. Ransome.</p>
        <p>Temporary Authority.—At Tuesday's Petty Sessions there was granted to John William Starkey temporary authority to sell exciseable articles at the Cat and Lion, Stretton;</p>
        <p>Tradesman's War ,Difficulty.—Charles Henry Hardy appeared at Tuesday's Petty Sessions for not having had control over a horse drawing his lorry in Fairfield-road, on June 11th.—It was stated that defendant was fifty yards behind the horse.—Hardy said he had had to go round to the back of a customer's houX, and was not away a minute and a half. His son, whom he usually left in charge, had enlisted.—The fine was is.</p>
        <p>Ambulatory Horse.—Arthur Greenhalgh, of Church Farm, Stockton Heath, appeared at Tuesday's Petty Sessions for having allowed his horse to stray in London-road, on June 20th.— He explained that he turned the horse out to graze on the Saturday afternoon and it must have strayed the following morning.—The Superintendent pointed out that the field was at Stretton—a good distance away from Stockton Heath.—Greenhalgh was dismissed with a caution.</p>
        <p>Officer Contradicted.—At the Petty Sessions on Tuesday morning, William Struthers, of Grappenhall, was summoned for driving a heavy locomotive along Tarporley-road, Stretton, at a speed exceeding four miles an hour, on the afternoon of July lst.—He pleaded not guilty.— Acting-Sergeant Worth spoke to findin\s, the vehicle to be going at six miles an hour.—De- fendant contradicted the distance over which he was timed, and said his speed Was below four miles an hour. Two other men who were also on the locomotive bore out this statement, and the case being dismissed, it beir,g also understood that the motor was on His Majesty's service.</p>
        <p>The Coat and the Plate.—Henry Critchley. bricklayer, of 15. West-street, Widnes, appeared at Tuesday's Petty Sessions for having at Preston Brook on Sunday, June 6th, driven his motor-cycle with the number plate covered.— Acting-Sergeant McKenna said defendant's brother was on the back of the cycle and his coat was hanging over the plate at the back. Defendant, when stopped, said sorry."— Critchley said he had passed several policemen. and had even spoken to one, and none had mentioned the matter to him.—He was ordered to pay the costs of the summons.</p>
        <p>"Stand Up Properly."—At Tuesday's Petty SesSions. James Clifford Edgerley, of Newton- by-Daresbury,- was summoned -for having at 10.5 on June 15th, in Whitley-road i Runcorn, driven a trap without a light.—A censtable said that When the d6fect was pointed out to defendant he replied: "I didn't take a lamp because I ex- pected to be home sooner."—When about to speak to the Court. defendant lounged over the front of the box, whereupon the Clerk (Mr. H. Greenall) said: "If you are going to address the magistrates hold yourself up properly !"—De- fendant did so, and then declared that be was home by five minutes past ten ",by any buzzer blown in England.''—The fine was 3s. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>EXCEEDED SPEED-LIMIT.</head>
        <p>EXCEEDED SPEED-LIMIT.</p>
        <p>‘Vliv Brewers' Motor Lurries Have No</p>
        <p>Speedometers.</p>
        <p>Frank Webster, of Mitchell-street, was sum- moned at Tuesday's Petty Sessions for driving a heavy motor car in .Tarporley-road, Stretton, on July sth, at a speed exceeding eight miles an hour.</p>
        <p>Acting-Sergeant Worth said the vehicle was timed over a measured distance by himself and another officer, and was found to be travelling at 12 miles an hour. When stopped, Webster said: "I don't think I have come very fast." P.C. Wellings corroborated.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Newton Pratt, of Manchester, de- fended. He did not deny the accuracy of the evidence, he said, but would point out that de- fendant did not know that he was exceeding the speed limit, and was doing his best to keep within it. He was employed by Messrs. Green- all; Whitley and Co., Ltd., and had an abso- lutely clean record. On this occasion there was a certain defect in the mechanism of the vehicle, and defendant did not think he could travel more than eight miles an hour. All the drivers employed by the firm were under strict injunctions not to exceed the limit. Defendant's work was exceedingly hard in these exceptional times, as 20 per cent. of the wagons and horses belonging to the company had been com- mandeered by the Government. The man had no speedometer, and had to judge entirely by -him- 'self as to what the speed was. Up to now it had not been possible to find a satisfactory speedometer to fit to these vehicles. The case was dismissed on payment of ss. costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>IMPORTANT MILK CASE.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT MILK CASE.</p>
        <p>Reasons Which Might Account For</p>
        <p>Fat-Deficiency.</p>
        <p>A milk, case of considerable importance was brought before the Daresbury . Petty Sessional magistrates at ,Stockton Heath on Tuesday, when :Joseph ,Holland, of Birch Tree Farm; Appleton, was summoned for selling on May 14th, milk which was deficient in fat to the extent of 10 per cent.. Mr. William James }Lollard (county inspector under the Food and Drugs Act), prosecuted, whilst Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, of Warrington, conducted the defence—entering a plea of not guilty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Milk-Standard Varies.</head>
        <p>Milk-Standard Varies.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallard told how he *stopped defendant's son—who was in charge of a milk float in Walton-road, Stockton Heath—and bought a pint of .milk from him, divided it in the usual way, and told him it would be analysed. The public, analyst's 'certificate showed that there was only 2.7 per cent. of milk fat, whereas the minimum standard was 3 per cent., which meant a deficiency of 10 per cent. on the amount of fat that should have been in the milk. When asked later for an explanation, defendant said he could not give one; the milk was not his own, but was bought from Mr. William Moss. of Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath, and if there was anything wrong with it it must have been wrong when the milk was supplied. Cross-examined by .Mr.; Steel as to why he..did not go to Mr. Moss's farm for a further sample, Mr. Hallard replied, that there was .no -obliga- tion on him tp dose. He adraitted that;milk from a cow varied greatly, and that in May and June the amount of fat was lower than at any other time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Effect of Calving.</head>
        <p>Effect of Calving.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: Mr. Moss has only two cows, and these had calves about two or three months be- fore the Sample was taken. Might not that make a difference?</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallard: No, not at that length of time. It might two or three weeks after. Mr. Steel: I understand that the quality is often poorest three months after calving? Mr. Ha'lard: Yes; but I took five other samples that morning, and that was the only one wrong.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel, after emphasising the fact that the milk was not from Mr. Holland's own cows, said that he had been selling milk for 29 years from his. own cows, but when he ran short he took a supply from Mr. Moss. It was a significant fact that although the Board of Agriculture issued a circular stating the standard of fat required, after ten years of experience they admitted that there were reasons for the milk sometimes falling below that standard. It was therefore clear that if the Bench were satisfied from the evidence for the defence that the milk was sold as it came from the cow, there ought not to be a conviction. "A Question of Quality."</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Roberts .(a member of the 13ermth):</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>Dismissed With Costs.</head>
        <p>Dismissed With Costs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Moss and Albert Holland (son of defend- ant) having borne out Mr. Steel's statement, the case was dismissed on payment of costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>REGAL ROSES.</p>
        <p>Fine Display Of Blooms By Local</p>
        <p>Society.</p>
        <p>SALE FOR SOLDIERS AND SAILORS</p>
        <p>Roses, roses everywhere; but not a flower to pick. This floral edition of the moan of the marooned Mariner ran through the mind of "The Examiner" man as he toured the Marquee at the rose and sweet pea show at Penketh on Saturday. But any slight mortification that the scribe may have experienced in having his passion for, posy-picking fanned only to be denied fuel receded into its proper perspective when the. information was. imparted by: Mr. Dendy, the keen and courteous secretary of the society, that as many flowers as purchasers could be found for would be sold at the end of the show, for tho benefit Of the soldiers and Sailors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL BLOOMS.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL BLOOMS.</p>
        <p>. The seventh annual show of the Penketh, Sankey and District Rose and Sweet Pea Society was a. very admirable exhibition.. Many beautiful blooms, roses, 'sweet peas and other flowers made a gorgeous • display. The roses were, perhaps, the first of all, but it was an excellent all-round exhibition, and one of which the members may be justifiably proud. A large. number ,of the flowers were soM (by the unanimous consent of the exhibitors) at the conclusion of the show, and the proceeds are being devoted to the local sewing fund for soldiers and sailors. Mr. C. E. Parker, put in a splendid exhibit of herbaceous plants, not. for competition, the arrangement of the collec- tion proclaiming Mr. J. E. Hughes an artist. Mrs. Dodds, of Penketh House, also contributed to the beauty of the show with an enviable selection of roses and sweet peas (arranged by Mr. Peter Davis), and a third solely ornamen- tal exhibit, in the shape of some first-rate specimens of the two flowers from which the society derives its name, was supplied by Mr. J. D. Arthur. Mr. P. W. Boardman was re- sponsible for the. arrangement in this case. The special prizes for the best roses in the show were won by Messrs. J. Moores and A. Appleton with exquisite blooms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>DANCING TILL DUSK.</head>
        <p>DANCING TILL DUSK.</p>
        <p>The show, which was held in Mr. Kelsall's charming croft, Bank 1101.160 Farm, was well attended. It was opened at three o'clock by the President (Mrs. C. E. Parker); From seven till dusk, dancing was indulged in, to the strains of Mr. • James Watts' orchestra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>OPENING.</head>
        <p>THE OPENING.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Woods (chairman of the committee), in formally introducing Mrs. Parker to the audience assembled for the opening teremony, alluded to the valued . support of the lady President, and said that some people had thought the show should not be held this year, owing to the war, but the committee felt that no good purpose would be served by taking a step calculated to lessen interest in the culti- vation of the beautiful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>PRESIDENTIAL CONGRATULATIONS.</head>
        <p>PRESIDENTIAL CONGRATULATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Parker warmly congratulated the mem- bers upon the splendid exhibition. A good deal of talking, she said, was now going on about compulsion. It seemed to be in the air at Penketh, because they had made her open their show for the second 'successive year. (Laughter.) At any rate, it was a compulsion that was by no means unpleasant. In fact, it was a task she was only too delighted and honoured to perform. (Applause.) The prize list was as follows : R CkS ES. Open Classes.</p>
        <p>Display of roses not to exceed four feet in length' (this class includes gentlemen's gar- deners).-1, Mrs. Dodds.</p>
        <p>Basket of roses arranged for display (any foliage).-1, Mrs. P. Davies; 2, Mrs. Dodds. Twelve blooms, any variety.-1, Mr. C. E. Parker; 2, Mr. J. Moores; 3, Mr. J. rdgerley. Vase of blooms arranged for display (any foliage).-1, Mr. C. E. Parker; 2, Mrs. P. Davies; 3, Mr. I. llampson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>Members' Classes.</head>
        <p>Members' Classes.</p>
        <p>Six blooms (other than tea roses), distinct and named.-1, Mr. J. Moores; 2, Mr. Walter Pye; 3, Mr. D. Fowles.</p>
        <p>Six blooms, tea or noisette, any variety.-1, Mr. J. Moores; 2, Mr. D. Fowles; 3, Mr. J. Ray. Six correctly named blooms, any variety.-1, Mr. J. Moores; 2, Mr. G. E. Carman; 3, Mr. D. Fowles; 4, Mr. J. Appleton. Vase of blooms, any variety, arranged for display.—!, Mr. I. HampSon; 2, Mr. J. Davies.</p>
        <p>_ Three blooms, H.P., distinct varieties named. —l, Mr. R. Rigby. Three blooms,.H.T., distinct varieties named. —l, Mr. Jos. Carman; 2, Mr. R. Rigby. One bloom, made up as a buttonhole, any foliage.-1, ._Mr. R. Rigby; 2, Mr. D. Fowles; 3, Mr. P. Davies.</p>
        <p>One spray for lady, any foliage (open to members' wives and daughters).-1, Mrs. P. Davies; 2, Mrs. G. E. Carman; 3, Mrs. 4. Ray. Three single 4-ases of ramblers or cluster roses, any variety (rose foliage only).-1, Mr. I. Hampson.</p>
        <p>One bowl of roses, any varieties arranged for display, any foliage (open to members' wives am! daughters).-1, Mrs. G. E. Carman; 2, Mrs. Walter Pye; 3, Mrs. P. Dendy..</p>
        <p>Three blooms, anyc variety named, open to novices only, a novice to be, one who has never gained a prize for roses.-1, Mr. R. Rigby; 2, Mr. J. Berry .; '3; One "Frau Karl Druschki" rose. 1, Mr. J. Davies; 2, Mr. W. Dwerryhouse; 3, Mr. F. Oxley. One bloom "Juliet."-1; Mr. J. Moores; 2, Mr. J. 1)9-vies; 3, Mr. W. Dwerryhouse. One .bloom of any red rose.-1, Mr. G. E. Carman; 2, Mr. J. Davies; 3, Mr. J. Berry. 'One bloom of any 'pink 'rose.—l, ;Mr. W. 'Dwerryhouse; 2, Mr. G. E. Carman; 3, Mr. L Hampson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>SWEET PEAS.</head>
        <p>SWEET PEAS.</p>
        <p>One vase of sweet peas to consist of 21 spikes, four distinct colours, not more, to be used in the vase; any suitable light foliage, grasses or gypsophila may be used in association with the sweet peas.-1, Mr. P. Davies; 2, Mr. W. iewerryhouse; 3; Mr. W. Lightfoot. Six single vases of sweet peas, six varieties, six sprays in a vase, own foliage.-1, Mr. P. Davies.</p>
        <p>Three vases of sweet peas in three varieties, nine sprays in a vase, own foliage.-1, Mr. P. Davies; 2, Mr. W. Lightfoot. One vase of sweet peas, thirteen sprays, any varieties, for novices only, a novice to be one who has never won a prize for sweet peas; own foliage.-1, Mr. S. Jones; 2, Mr. William" Pye; 3, Mr. J. Bay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>MISCELLANEOUS.</head>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS.</p>
        <p>Three single vases of cut flowers in three distinct kinds, outside grown (excluding rose and sweet peas). 1, Mr. I. Ilampson. Twelve violas in four varieties, three of each. Mr. W. Dwerryhouse; 2, Mr. D. Fowles; 3, Mr. S. Jones.</p>
        <p>Three violas and three pansies, any variety.- 1, Mr. Jos. Carman. A special prize of 12 choice H.T. rose trees offered for a bowl of outside grown roses.-1, Mr. P. Davies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="5" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>PRIVATE J. W. JONES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. W. JONES.</p>
        <p>in London eight days ago and proceeded to King George's Military Hospital, S.E. On Monday evening his 'mother received .from the nurse a card to say he had beeti safely in- stalled; and the next evening Private Jones himself wrote in a letter that he had been very ill, but thought he had now got over the worst. He - was not allowed to write more. He is progressing as well as .could ,be hoped— very slowly. Private Jones, whose age is 21, was, prior to the outbreak of war, employed as a fireman at the Partington Paper Mill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>RED CROSS SUBSCRIPTION'S.</head>
        <p>RED CROSS SUBSCRIPTION'S.</p>
        <p>Good Amount, But Not Sufficient To</p>
        <p>Maintain Hospital.</p>
        <p>The Working Men's Committee of "Brook- , ' field," the Lymm Red Cross Hospital, have adopted the half-yearly balance sheet, Novem- ber 17th to April 27th—drawn up by the Hon. Treasuret (Mr. A. H. Hudson) and audited by Mr.- John Thomason. This shows that the income, from weekly subscriptions collected by the, committee and from sale of tickets and programmes for concert, totalled £335 6s. 11d. The average monthly income from weekly sub- scriptions amounted to £4B 17s. This is, of course, a good amount; but it is not nearly sufficient to keep the hospital going. Recent expenses have been heavy, in consequence of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>. . : ..,•-•,- --;-,- , -; , . l:.'"..et_ _.:.,`• . . -40.044. . -i"" . – •, .: . ..._ ...,....,...„..,....... –</head>
        <p>. . : ..,•-•,- --;-,- , -; , . l:.'"..et_ _.:.,`• . . -40.044. . -i"" . - •, .: . ..._ ...,....,...„..,....... -</p>
        <p>TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, SUIT CASES, &amp;a.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>J. S. GARLAND,</head>
        <p>J. S. GARLAND,</p>
        <p>51, 53 &amp; 55, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>FRIENDS' SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>FRIENDS' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Patriotic Part Detailed At Annual General Meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</head>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>Very little happens in Penketh to bring home to us the horrors or even the trials of the war. The effect of the war upon school life has been small. But all appeals made to the children on behalf of the sufferers have met with a ready response.</p>
        <p>So spoke the Headmaster of the Friends' School, Penketh, on Thursday. The occasion was the. annual general meeting—which was marked by a somewhat larger concourse of visitors than usual, and which passed off very successfully. In the morning the arrangement oi , lessons was explained to the parents, and a committee appointed to examine the house. This they proceeded to do, and after, at the end of the morning, announced with much pleasure that everything had been found highly satisfactory. In the meantime the remainder of the visitors watched the teaching o'f. the various customary subjects, languages, trades and household duties; and also examined exhibitions of drawing, writing, notebooks, photography, carpentry, Nature-study, needle- work, and so on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>BUSINESS MEETING.</head>
        <p>BUSINESS MEETING.</p>
        <p>After dinner, the business meeting was pre- Sided over in the Lecture Hall by Mr. John Collinson, of Manchester. • There were also on the platform Messrs. Arnold Hodgkinson (sec- retary), C. Jackson Holmes . (treasurer), Llewelyn Jones (of Ashworth, a member of the Committee of Management), and W. E. Brown .(headmaster).</p>
        <p>Mr. Hodgkinson read the report of the Com- mittee of Management, which ran : "The school household has shown practical sympathy. with sufferers in this terrible time. Money that is usually spent on Fifth of Nov- ember celebrations was contributed to the Friend' War Victims' Fund; boys made splints for wounded limbs, and the girls' needlework took the form of providing clothing for sol- diers' children; they also sttbseribed a sover- eign in weekly pennies to a Clogyund. Other signs of 'out-of-school' activity worth men- tioning are the starting of the Petiketh School Magazine' and the division of the boys into Houses,' known as Frys, Penns and Foxes, whose friendly rivalry is promoted- by an elaborate system of marking, in which class- places, games, sports, work in societies, and even freedom from impositions all count. "Anticipations of the value of the recent additions and alterations to the 'school build- ings have been abundantly realised, the only exception being a fortunate one, viz., that the nursing ward's have -hardly been used at all. This year several other minor , but valuable improvements have been made—a first-class hard tennis court has been laid down, a new plot of gardens provided, and a new cricket pitch for the girls; one class-room has been re- furnished with modern hygienic desks—a reno- vation which it is intended to adopt in other rooms as soon as funds allow.</p>
        <p>"Owing to there'being so many young pupils and consequent congestion in the lower forms, a new class has been made in the middle of the school and a fourth-master added to the staff. The upper forms continue to acquit themselves creditably in the College of Pre- ceptors' Examination: the 'principal features in the results this year are that the total number of successes-40—is greater than hither- to—so are the numbers of seniors—five—and the number of distinctions-23; it may also be remarked that one of the boys gained the 2nd place in Scripture. Feeling that the examina- tion results are not the only test of efficiency, it was decided to have the school formally in- spected, and this work was carried out by Mr. Charles J. R. Tipper, B.Sc., director of educa- tion for the county of Westmorland. He paid special attention to the subjects of English, geography, arithmetic, and science. His ,report points out certain. weaknesses in methods and in attainment, but on the whole it is encour- aging. Two quotations may be made from it : " I was very favourably impressed with the tone and discipline of the school.. There is a large measure of freedom, but no sign of laxity, and the relations between the staff and the children seem to be perfectly happy.' " ' The school is giving a sound education on good lines, an excellent moral training. and is providing for the healthy, physical development of its pupils. The Headmaster and his staff are to be congratulated." HEADMASTER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>This was adopted, whereupon the Headmaster read his report. • The number of children in the school, he stated, continued to increase; and now stood at 56 boys and 43 girls. As there had not been much use for the new nursing wards, one of them was now regularly established as a girls' dormitory, and the other one had so far pro- vided ample accommodation for the amount of nursing required. In leisure hours the school's life. had proceeded evenly, without much varia- tion from previous years, though the increase in the number of girls had naturally led to their having more older girls in higher and more responsible positions—a circumstance which had resulted in a- fuller realisation of the school's corporate life. During the present term the swimming bath had been in daily use and gardening had also been a favourite hobby; healthy rivalry and interest having been quick- ened by three periodical inspections and award of marks by a member of the staff. A fair number of the Natural History Society were now collecting plants, with a view to com- mencing botanical study. The most impor- tant development outside the class-room was the production of the Penketh School Maga- zine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>Portugal and _Belgium.</head>
        <p>Portugal and _Belgium.</p>
        <p>"The boys and- girls in the school," con- tinued Mr. Brown, "must indeed see little to remind them of events abroad. One pupil, whose home is in Portugal, has been compelled to remain at home during the past twelve months, but, on the other hand, we have been glad to welcome as day boarders two girls who belong to the company of Belgian refugees which has been established in Penketh.</p>
        <p>"As far as we know at present, thirty-one Penketh- old scholars have been assisting at the Front in one form of service or another. Of these, four have joined the Friends' Ambu- lance Unit; two have worked under the Friends' War Victims' Committee; one belongs to the ambulance department of the British Red Cross Hospital at Rouen; one girl is a Red Cross nurse; and the remaining twenty-four enlisted in the iordinary way. It was with the deepest regret that we learnt that one of these —Harry 11. Leaver—had been killed during the fighting at Ypres." This report also was adopted, and the offi- cials and committee re-elected en bloc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>PROPERTY VESTED.</head>
        <p>THE PROPERTY VESTED.</p>
        <p>It was decided to vest the property in the. hands of the trustee of charitable lands. - At the conclusion of the business a meeting for worship was held. Tea was served later.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</head>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>The prize distribution took place in the evening, and the Lecture Hall was, of course, full to overflowing. The Headmaster pre- sided. A large number of prizes were handed out to the successful students and athletes by Mr. Collinson, each boy and girl being heartily applauded.</p>
        <p>In the course of a short and very appropriate address, Mr. Collinson dwelt upon the pleasure he had been afforded by visiting the rooms and inspecting them; by watching the lessons being taught, and noting the progress which was being made in all subjects and branches. This led him to say—as Lord Bacon had said—that all learning was useful; thus they could not make their learning too v.aried or too exten- sive. But when it came to the study of the arts and sciences and so on, if they could not excel in all they should beset their attention upon one branch, and study in one direction. Richard Cosway proposed, and Edith Esc°lme</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>Itttas to the Odor</head>
        <p>Itttas to the Odor</p>
        <p>THE NATIONAL DRINK BILL</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I trespass on your valuable space to correct a serious mis-statement reported in your issue of the 26th inst.? At an open-air temperance meeting on Friday night the Rev. W. Harris (of Ashton-in-Makerfield) stated that our annual drink bill was £500,000,000. The United Kingdom Alliance put forward every year an estimate prepared by their secretary, Mr. G. B. Wilson. His figures for the last three years are as follows:</p>
        <p>1912, £161,553,330. 1913, £166,681,000. 1914, £164,463,000.</p>
        <p>The estimates include special taxation and local rates, which totalled .246,000,000 in 1912. and of course a lot more since, and which should rightly be deducted. They represent the extreme teetotallist estimate, the maximum figures which anyone has dared to pot forward up to now. I fancy the Rev. W. Harris has made what is vulgarly termed a "bloomer,"_ and trust he will acknowledge his error, which does not inspire much confidence in teetotallist facts and figures. , Liverpool, June 28th, 1915</p>
        <p>,T. E. SIMPSON.</p>
        <p>seconded, a very hearty vote of thanks to Mr- Collinson for coming and distributing the prizes. This was carried with acclamation; and, in its acknowledgment, the recipient de- clared that there could not be added to the joy and pleasure he had had in the whole of the proceedings—and not least in handing out those handsome prizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>MUSIC AND SPEECH</head>
        <p>MUSIC AND SPEECH</p>
        <p>The capital entertainment which followed opened with a piano ,duet by Edith Escolme and Hannah Jolleys—who later gave in spirited style Carse's "Danse Grotesque." Miss Wait,es also earned much applause by her piano solos, and the Girls' Choir with "The Angel" (Rubinstein) and "Viking Song" (Coleridge- Taylor). An amusing recitation—" Ma Mere m'envoie au Marche "—was given by Hewson Hartley ii., Lily Lowndes, Rosalie Jenks, -Ursula Thorburn, Taylor, Eileen Winstanley. Mildred Albright and Jean Ransome; . and the entertainment was brought to a, close by a good rendering of the trial scene from "The Merchant of Venice" by Edith M. Helliwell, Ellis, Cosway, price, Nuttall and Bellas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>American Tea.—Owing to the kindness of Dr. and Mrs. Bennett an American tea, organised by the Young Leaguers' Union, was held in the grounds at Grange Mount, on Saturday after- noon. The proceedings were entirely in the hands of the children, Master Roy Dakin being in the chair. The opening ceremony was very ably performed by Miss Eileen Morton, and votes of thanks were moved by Miss C. Tunstalf and Master B. Stables. Tea was served in. the dining-room, after which a sale of articles, which had been given by members Of the Union and friends, took place. Games were also in- dulged in and proved very interesting and enjoyable. The proceeds, which, were in aid of the National Children's Home and Orphanage, realised £B. •</p>
        <p>- Choir Picnic.—The members of the Padgafe Wesleyan Choir had a delightful outing to . Chester on Saturday afternoon last, on -the occasion of their annual picnic. During the afternoon many of the party took advantage of the interval before tea to spend a pleasant hour or so on the river. After tea, a launch was chartered and the members had a delightful trip up the river as far as Eccleston Ferry, for Eaton Hall. Although gloomy at the out- set, the weather on the whole, -waS all that could be desired, and a very enjoyable time was spent by all present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>GREAT SAIN KEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SAIN KEY.</p>
        <p>ELECTRICITY ?</p>
        <p>Lighting Affairs Discussed At Parish Council Meeting. At last week's meeting of the Parish Council, Mr. W. Furness presiding, some dis- cussion took place on lighting matters. There were present, in addition to the Chairman, Messrs. J. G. Pownall, S. Fair, Lowe and J. Smith (clerk).</p>
        <p>• . The Clerk read a notice from the Warrington Corporation of their intention to terminate their street lighting contracts with the War- rington Rural District Council in respect of certain parishes, including Great Sankey. March 31st, next year, is the date fixed for the ending of the contract. Mr. Po*nail wanted to know whether the Council had been allowed anything for lamps which were not lit. The Clerk said they could make a claim. Mr. Pownall said that, with regard to street lighting in the future, he was inclined to pro- pose that the Council do their utmost to save all the gas possible. They were asked to use the greatest care in spending money at the Tiresent time, and he thought that during the war they might do without lights altogether. In con- sequence of the reduced lighting order, there had been only about half a dozen lamps lit. The Chairman said he thought it would he better to leave the matter over until the con- tract expired. Mr. Lowe suggested that perhaps it would be more advantageous to have electric lights in- stead of gas lamps, in view of the Corporation's proposals. The Chairman said that the Rural District Council had told the Corporation that they would only support the proposed Order if the Corporation supplied the current at the same rates as they supplied it in the boroogn. If the Corporation were granted the 0:41m-, theY would be anxious to supply as many consumer; as they possibly could—business firms ena Private houses. No doubt they would offer facilities sufficiently cheap to induce Sankey 10 take a supply. The Council decided to defer the matter for the present THE CANAL BRIDGE. The Chairman, in reply to Mr. Lowe, wil° asked When the new bridge over the canal would be ready for use, said that he heti requested the Rural District Council Surveyor (Mr. B. S. Newcombe) to communicate with the County Bridge Master on the matter, and anticipated that his reply would be forthcoming at the next meeting of the Rural District Conn' cil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER RACING MOTORIST. Running Against Time As His Wife</p>
        <p>Was 111. At the Warrington Police Court on Thursd_ral' before Mr. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), r. A. J. Jolley and Dr. Naden, Stanley Gibbs, of Rose Dell, Grappenhi was summoned for driving a motor cycle. side-car, at a rate dangerous to the public, il• Liverpool-road.---Defendant pleaded guilty 4 P.C. Skinner stated that defendant's sPee,;,, at the time he saw him was from 24 to miles an hour. Witness held up his hand. 1)11 11 defendant took no notice. There were of children about at the time and there also a good deal of traffic. Defendant said that he used the road 7, number of times weekly and did not n reniewbed e• ever to have travelled at a excessive erhis He drove 25,000 miles year and had bei° -at licence for nine years, and had never beon stopped before. It was just possible thel.ot the day in question he was running agal ,time, as his wife was ill. as • The Chairman said the Bench would be lenient as they could. Defendant would fined. DS. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
