      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>People's Park. The closing of Bank Park to the general Public last Saturday reminded me of certain incidents in my experience of other towns. In one of these in West Yorkshire, some twelve acres of enclosed space were put into a condition of floral loveliness and something like artistic perfection by a highly successful business man of those parts, and, signifi- cantly entitled "The People's Park," dedi- cated to the use of the public for ever, the Stipulation being that it must on no account be closed against them for a single day. •As time went on, a •bandstand came to be pro- vided and military bands to be engaged to Perform there, but of course it was impos- \Bible under the terms of the gift to make any charge for admission; all the committee of management could do was to place sheets for voluntary contributions and seats in* the vicinity of the bandstand, for which a small fee was charged. It was a heavy handicap for the promoters of the concerts, but</p>
        <p>A Dif forence. A reference to another case that likewise came under my own observation will enable the reader to complete the foregoing sentence. In a Lancashire town in which I spent some of the intervening years was a larg4 park than the one above mentioned, which in the course of each year was often closed for a day for the purposes of some show or fete, or band peFformance. A section of the rate- payers strongly resented this at first, claiming on principle a right of access to their own park under all circumstances. They im- pressed many with the reasonableness of their Contention, and the- agitation on their part Progressed satisfactorily enough up to a certain point. When they sought to con- summate it, they were confronhed with a clause legally reserving to the Corporation the right to close the park twelve days in the year I</p>
        <p>Egg Farmers. The item in the poultry column, " Soldiers aa egg farmers," brought to my mind the ease of a young mechanic whom I met half-a- dozen years ago in the Eye Hospital, Oxford- road, Manchester. He had suffered serious injury to one eye at his work, and the acci- dent had resulted in a sympathetic affection, sO that for some time he was threatened with total blindness. I have kept in touch with him by means of a periodical exchange of letters, and he tells me that, having vainly hoped to qualify as a piano-tuner, he finally decided to invest his compensation money in a poultry farm, Once I asked him if he Managed last season to solve the problem of Winter egg production at a profit, and he replied to the effect that, in spite of the sub- stantial increase in the cost of maintenance and certain unfavourable local conditions, he had done well enough to determine to per- severe in the business. Assuredly there is something in what the correspondent quoted in last week's " Examiner" had to say on the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>_ Dangerous Driving.</head>
        <p>_ Dangerous Driving.</p>
        <p>I am glad to note that motorists are being summoned at the instance of the local police for driving at excessive speeds. A few days ago I had a startling adventure Which plainly showed me how easily unsuspecting pedes- trians may be knocked down by reckless motorists—and incidentally emphases the plea for the same rule of the road for both drivers and foot-passengers. I was about to call on a tradesman, and, keeping to my right, was walking along the extreme edge of the path, when -a light-running car of the latest type rushed close past. The chauffeur was literally and very precisely observing the rule in that one particular, but he had not sounded his hooter or given any kind of notice of his approach, and if at that moment I had stepped off the footpath (and he could not possibly igness at my intentions as to that) what would ) .fl pretty generally regarded as an abreident " would have been quite unavoid- k2. Of course it is for the pedestrian to n'P a sharp look-out in both directions °ltadays. The motorist need not greatly tiTneern himself about the matter; even should bue, Worst occur he can as a rule count on °nig "exonerated from all blame" by a soft- 'loaned jury. Anyhow, prevention is over so much better, and the activity of the police Mttinst offenderg is highly commendable. It j 8 to be hoped that the needful encourage- ..sibent will be duly forthcoming from the</p>
        <p>The Bell-ringing Nuisance. No, the reference is not to church bells at 'all. I have in mind the case of a week ago in which certain local boys were fined for 'Wantonly ringing a door-bell." It recalls a story told by one of our bishops against him- self. Walking•along the street, he came upon an urchin trying to reach the handle of a door- ; I suppose most of us have passed the youngster, suiting the action to the word, Uttered the warning cry " Now run." Well, Well, I suppose most of us have passed through that and the bird-nesting stage, and We must not be too severe with those who have just reached it. All the same I did not at all like having to remove the electric bell from the front of my house; but alas, it had an irresistible fascination for the lads of the neighbourhood, and they yielded to the temptation so frequently and in such numbers that there was no alternative. An Old Warrington Clock. On Saturday I had a day off, and, visiting a favourite haunt of my youth, had the good fortune to fall in with a party of about .30 lnembers of an Antiquarian Society, from a town in which I worked as a young man. One of them—whom I had failed to recognise, and small wonder—claimed acquaintance, although we had not met for forty years. If that were all, I need not have troubled the reader with this item ; however, in an old house Which the company proceeded to inspect be- cause of its interest to the antiquary, I noticed a gran.dfither's clock bearing the Maker's name, "Edward Harrison, Warring- ton." I questioned a professed expert in such Matters and things, and he admitted that he had not previously met with the name in such bannection; but he made a note of it for further inquiry. This was between 40 and 60 Miles from here; doubtless there are many Varringtoniahs to whom the name of the old clock-maker is familiar enough. False Alarm. - -</p>
        <p>The local paragraph in the last issue, " False alarm of fire—and why," sent me hack in thought to an ambitious little muni- cipality in which I lived for a while that set 1113 an elaborate system of electric fire alarms, adlti for months Thereafter suffered many ,Itiugs on account thereof. The brigade at Luo central fire station received ,mysterious t"llg almost daily, and, as they were bound t 9 turn out promptly in response, the situa- Lill was not only puzzling but exasperating, ;76'1_ use of the chaff they had to endure as 11:037 returned after one of these false alarms. 7;',oreover, considerable expense was involved: ultimately the 'authorities overcame tho diffi- OilltY by appointing to the superintendency °lle who was a skilful electrician as well as a enPable and enthusiastic fire-fighter. That B?lved the problem; there were no more false alarms of fire in that district. Croviners Quest. • The Deputy-Coroner for West Lancashire I'vkpiald fain during the war erisiadispense with ne services of male jurors or have women 'lnPanelled. Putting the latter out of the tiiluestion, let me suggest as a matter of per- :nal observation and experience that the isYerage coroner does not habitually exercise 218 undoubtedly wide discretion so far as dis- Pecnsing with the services of juries is con- t,sr_ll, cd• There are many simple cases of un- "tified death which, by informal preliminary</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
        <p>"The Social Problem: A Constructive Analysis. By Charles A. Ellwood, Ph.D. London: Macmillan and Co. Price Se. 6d. net.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>*THE SOCIAL PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>*THE SOCIAL PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Nineteenth Century Selfishness Blamed For Present Chaos.</p>
        <p>WIDER OUTLOOK NEEDED.</p>
        <p>This book is peculiarly interesting to English readers at the present time. It is the work of an American University Professor, who lias visited this country subsequent to the outbreak of the war. The volume contains many refer- ences to the war and to its underlying causes. An introductory chapter of nearly 50 pages is based on an itddress delivered by the author to the Sociological Society of London on " The Social Problem and the Present War;" and it contains a most scathing indictment of modern civilisation. Rotten Foundation of Civilisation.</p>
        <p>" The present war," says Professor Ellwood, "has come as a .shock to those who have not studied intimately the foundations of nineteenth century culture; but, in the writer's opinion, it is not an accident of any sort, in diplomacy or otherwise. Rather, the war has merely exposed the' rottenness of some of those foundations of Western civilisation. We have supposed that we could rear a secure social structure upon the basia of an egoistic and materialistic social philosophy. ,We have permitted a re-barbarisa- tion of the individual's moral standards without imagining that these would- actually express themselves in the life of nations. We have thought that somehow, out of a programme of self-interest, material satisfactions; and brute force, followed by men and nations, a settled and harmonious order would result Even now there are those who fail to see that the egoistic socially-negative doctrines, which got such a hold of Western civilisation in the nineteenth century, both in theory and in practice, are the sources of present disordet. They look for some more ultimate sources in biologic or economic necessities. But those who see clearly must per- ceive that while biologic and economic condi- tions may act .as stimuli, the real roots of civilisation are always in the mental attitudes and conscious' values of individuals. They will also see that some of the mental attitudes and values approved as sound by the nineteenth century have proved unsound in practice: and that the present war of nations calls for a re- construction of our social philosophy—a rebuild- ing orit—on a different basis from that approved by the nineteenth century." Professor Ellwood shows conclusively, in our 'opinion, that the social problem is not to be .solved by approach along any one particular line, but only by a comprehansive view of the whole matter. 'lt is not a problem that is peculiar to any one nation, but is characteristic of all nations. Ile has fairly reached the core of the question when he says that" it is the con- flict between inharmonious traditions and ideals in our culture, on the one hand, and the lack of adaptation of our ideas and ideals to the present conditions of life, on the other, which have pro- duced the modern social problem. . . . Men are in inharmonious relations, classes and nations are in conflict, civilisation is rent asunder in the modern world, because men have been trying to live by socially inadequate, con- flicting, one-sided ideas and values." Humanity Involved. -</p>
        <p>The social problem is the problem of human living together, and it cannot be confined to the people of any one country; it affects the whole world. As soon as one commences to consider the question deeply, it is apparent thatAhe unit of thought in this connection is not the State, but humanity itself. Here it is that'llermany has gone astray, and we are' not surprised to find a neutral writer like Professor Ellwood de- claring in regard to von Bernhardi, that "when an author claiming to speak for any one nation asserts that action in ' favour of collective humanity outside of the limits of the State or nationality is impossible,' and that its own mis- sion is to impose its superior civilisation upon as large a part of humanity as possible, the rest of the civilised world stands aghast at this frank avowal of group egoism as a basis for practical living." No solution of the problem is possible while nations refuse to think in terms other than those bf the State.</p>
        <p>We are, at the present time, as Professor Ellwood points out, in a state of transition. We have inherited from .the nineteenth century a legacy of materialistic individualism which has largely overwhelmed the socialising tendencies making for social cohesion. While nations are trying by collective efforts to remove glaring evils and abuses, people are still making the endeavour to pursue a strictly individualistic course. Even where parties advocate measures of an. apparently collective nature, in reality they are inspired by class consciousness and with a view to class aggrandisement. The old religious sanctions have lost their hold. The people Seem to have no centre, no point of view, from which to adjust themselves to life, unless it be that of self-interest. "Self-realisation," "the development of individuality" have been preached .in such a manner that their real meaning is lost in the pursuit of material wealth at the expense of any who stand in the way. Apparently we have no social ideals or values that will act as a constraining influence on the disruptive tendencies in human nature, and the result, if no arrest of this process ensues, will plainly be chaos. Diverse Factors.</p>
        <p>The great need ,of the present time, a need greater than ever with such a war as the pres- ent in full swing, is what Professor Ellwood calls "the synthetic view of life," that is a view which takes into account all the diverse factors which are involved in the social life. of man. People Will have to realise more fully and deeply that they cannot live to themselves alone, but that we are "all members of one body," if life is to be made tolerable on this planet. And bp this end religion of some sort is a vital necee- sity. But it must not be a religion which in- vests the individual with a supreme value, apart from his relation with his fellows. It must be a religion which "exalts the service of humanity over and above the service of any in- dividual class, nation, or even race, as the high- est end and value."</p>
        <p>Though taking a.Tery serious view indeed of present conditions, Professor Ellwood does not permit himself to drift into pessimism with re- gard to the future. Far from the present state leading to a counsel of despair, he thinks it should be an inspiration to effort. For, ae he points out, human society has entered upon the self-conscious phase of its own evolution, and through consciousness of its own condition, it can, if it. will, within certain limits control its own destiny:</p>
        <p>We cannot speak too highly of this little book. Were it only for its references to the war it would be deeply interesting to readers in this country. But the author's largeness of view, his grasp of the question, and his hopeful out- look, render this a book Of great value in the severe trial through which the nation is at present passing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.-</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.-</p>
        <p>Continued.</p>
        <p>inquiry of the doctor in the case and perhaps one other witness, might be satisfactorily disposed of without the trouble and expense involved in summoning twelve men to inter- vene as jurors. Under the prevailing system, the latter in the majority of cases merely come together to. append their signatures to a verdict frankly based entirely on the medical testimimy. In my humble judgment the " crowner's quest" before a jury is a once indispensable function that has outgrown its usefulness, and might safely and beneficially be superseded in accordance with the former part of Mr. F. A. Jones' suggestion as reproduced in a local paragraph in last week's paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="6" unit="words">
