      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>WARBURTON.</p>
        <p>Adult School " Ramble " To A Notable Church.</p>
        <p>RECTOR'S HISTORY.</p>
        <p>The third ramble of the WarringOn Adult Schools' season took place on Saturday. The destination for the day was Warburton, which was reached by train and cycle. The interests of the day were of an antiquarian nature, and the party of forty-five which assembled at the old church were met by the Rector, the Rev. G. Egerton-Warburt n, who acted as leader for the afternoon. Fortunately the wind kiTt the threatening rain at a distance, and the old-world restful and peaceful _setting of. the venerable building was appreciated under ideal conditions for rambling. Church History.</p>
        <p>The Rector told us in his description of the building that Warburton is mentioned in Doomsday Book, and that there may have been a Saxon Church here, but it is known for certain that Norbertine White Canons settled at Warburton in the reign of Henry IL anti. the date of the timber work in the church has been placed by some in the 12th century, which would make it coeval_ with their arrival. By some other good authorities the present build- ing is said to be two centuries later in date. However that may be, a church of mudi the same description has stood upon the same spot for 700 or 800 years at least. The whole build- ing we may take for granted was originally of timber, as is the north side now, but the south wall and the west end were rebuilt in Lymm sandstone in 1615. The nave-and the chancel have evidently been built quite independently of one *another. The chancel has at some time been extended in brickwork, very likely about the year 1711, when the brick tower or "steeple," as it is always called, was built. The "Little Chancel" on the south of the chancel, partly of stone and partly of brick, is an addition, as is also the "Park Pew" of Lymm sandstone on the north side. There is a date-1595—0n the font-cover, and the one bell is dated 1575. The stone coffin which is. now placed in the Park Pew was found buried in the churchyard about fifty years ago. It is said to be of the 12th century and may well have been made for a prior of the Norbertine Canons. The low-pitched • roof of the nave is of a wide span and is supOorted by strong pillars of oak. On one pillar there is a plain iron hour-glass stand, and on others there are stage-horn hat-pegs.</p>
        <p>- D 'eats on the south side and they were removed in 1895;, at the same time the painted plaster was taken off the two main pillars, and a thick coat of paint from the font. The pulpit and the oak com- munion table are said to ,date from the time of Charles I.</p>
        <p>Mr. Warburton very kindly exhibited the old oak, iron-bound, church chest; in which are deposited. the MS. treasures of the building. The registers were examined by the members of the party. The earliest date in the regis- ters is 1611, but entries were not regularly made until 1634.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>Ideal Rectory Garden.</head>
        <p>Ideal Rectory Garden.</p>
        <p>The party passed into the :Rectory garden, about which the owner has written a delightful book of Nature notes. The wet, stormy wea- ther of the previous week had destroyed much of the floral beauty of the extensive garden, but there were still abundance of roses and blossoms of all colours and hues to admire. The spot is an ideal situation for horticulture, well-sheltered, with plenty of trees surrounding for moisture. Scattered here and there amongst the beds were many plants not planted by the hand of man, plants which in various ways (some perhaps transported in the form of seeds attached to the earth' on the feet of wandering birds) have found the ground suited to their growth, and have taken up their per- manent residence. The Rector takes care, that they are not disturbed, for he is a botanist and a Nature lover as well as gardener. An excellent tea, which was much enjoyed, was provided at the School House at the War- burton New Church, the walk in the cool, crisp air having given everyone keen appetites. After tea, a real jolly Adult School "free and easy" was held in the same building, many of the musically gifted members giving •.I.eir services for the enjoyment of the company. Mrs. Nurse and Mr. Harry Wright led off with recitations, Miss C. A. Mathews sang "My Bonnie," and 'The ,Marseillaise" in French; Miss Ivy Webb sang "My old father was Irish"; Messrs. John Smith and John Elwell</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <p>obliged with a duet; but Jim tit-bit of the evening was the unexpected talent shown by Mr. W. J. lowland as -a conductor of a band</p>
        <p>of bagpipes.' At the close a vote of appreciation to the Rector and the lady who provided the tea was carried with acclamation.</p>
        <p>Altogether the Warburton ramble proved a most enjoyable one in every sense, combining an instructive afternoon with a jolly evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>MARIE CORELLI'S WORKS.</head>
        <p>MARIE CORELLI'S WORKS.</p>
        <p>Lad Lecturer Gives Shorthand riters A Candid Opinion.</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of the Warrington Shorthand Writers', Association, a lecture on ."Miss Marie Corelli end her Books" was delivered by Miss L. Fleming, who said that Miss Marie Corelli was perhaps the most widely read of any Ming novelist, but it did not necessarily follow that her books were superior in literary style or charm to those of such writers as Hider Haggard and Sir A. Conan Doyle. Her popularity was due to a certain extent to her remarkable and almost extraordinary personality, which was reflected in her books. She was distinctly feminine in her style, and no one would suggest for a moment that a man had written her books; in fact, the lecturer said, she had never known or beard of a man taking a woman's name as a literary non-de-plume. Women had often re- sorted to the opposite course,, and Miss Marie Corelli !.'as. in several of her books, at great pains to explain why it was so. She told .them, for instance, that it was because men were jealous and would not give a woman any credit. Miss Corelli was almost consumed with that idea; so much so, that she spoilt some of her best work with malice against a certain class of persons, mostly men, whom she seemed to consider ought not to be allowed to live. In the first book she wrote, "A Romanee of Two Worlds," the theme was' everlasting life. Every one knew that they had to die, ,but happily Divinity had so constituted them that the fact was hardly ever in their minds. But what a tempting bait it was to be able to read a book that taught them how to stave off the inevitable, bar accidents, indefinitely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>Gifted—But Deluded.</head>
        <p>Gifted—But Deluded.</p>
        <p>But the lecturer - ventured to 'think tbat neither Edison nor Marconi, 'ever received any inspiration from Miss Corelli's, theories be- cause her ideas did not connect the great forces of Nature with the human agencies which brought them into captivity, and made them of practical use to mankind. Her candid opinion of Miss Corelli was that was, undoubtedly, one of the most, gifted 'writers of the -present day, but one of the most deluded, if she really believed her theories and thought all the bard things she said about certain people. On the other hand, if she wrote her books as pure fiction, she was an exceedingly clever woman. Her books did nob compare with those of Dickens, because they were not pictures of life and character. They did not approach the variety of style displayed in those of Rider Haggard, neither was there the high purpose and aim underlying the works of Harold Begbie and Arnold Bennett.</p>
        <p>. A discussion followed, after 'Which a vote of thanks was Proposed to Miss Fleming and carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <p>•:• 40:* • 0 0 •:* i:p •:* •:p 0 4. * .I: . -TIIE AVABRINGTON : • 4. 4. -A.B.C. 4. , • 4 • . 4. RAILWAY. GUIDE. • • 4. . 41 4 . 4•. The Ilailway Services for the •:* A • • Summer Season came into . • .0 • operation last Monday. + + + + + + The Warrington A.B.C. Guide • • • for July was published on 4:* + • that day There are many • • • alterations. • • - • • - • • PRICE ONE PENNY. 0 .:. .4.</p>
