      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Protest Allainst Suspension Of Union</p>
        <p>Activities.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, held on Tuesday evening it was decided to endorse a motion forwarded by the Gas Workers' and General Labourers' -Union "entering an emphatic pro- test against the autocratic policy" adopted by 'the officials of the Trades Unions in having agreed to Waive the use of the strike weapon and consented to compulsory •arbitration, and having sanctioned the employment of female labour to ,supplant male labour during the period of the war. Uniens Should Have Been Consulted.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Dukes contended that the matter was one upon which the various Trades tnion branches should have been -consulted before the so-called delegates agreed, at the behest of the Chancellor of the Exchequer, to a snspen- sion of Trades "Union rules—an agreement which became binding on millions of men and women who had had no voice in the making of the compact. it was a most undemocratic procedure. " The Times," commenting on the matter, had said that tile Labour movement, apart from its officials, was not to be trusted, and he (Mr. Dukes) said emphatically that that was 6Q, because, whilst it was suggested that wages should remain a fixed quantity, no guarantee was given that prices were also going to remain at a fixed level. Fixed Prices As Well As Fixed Wages.</p>
        <p>The very least the Trades Unions might have asked for before consenting to the abrogation of some of thefr activities was. that if the maximum • amount of wages was to he fixed, a maximum price should also be fixed for the ordin- ary domestic requirements. The statement that the new arrangement with regard to Trades Union activities was only to last till the end of hostilities, was, the speaker con- tended, a nebulous one, and conveyed no feel- ing of certainty that, after the signing of peace terms, the workers would get back all their rights. The strike weapon, of which they were to be deprived during the war, would be of no use to them for years after the war, for then the Labour market would be glutted and there would be so many unemployed wait- ing for jobs, that a man who had got work dare not even look black at his "boss," never mind how much he might be put 'upon. What guarantee had Trades • Unionists • that the status quo was going to be restored after the war was over?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>" Ludicrous—lf Not So Pathetic !"</head>
        <p>" Ludicrous—lf Not So Pathetic !"</p>
        <p>A delegate opined that if the ,surrender of Trades Union rights perpetrated by the so- called leaders were .not ludicPous, it would be pathetic. At the end of the war the worker would be left stranded, just as were the flot- sam and jetsam after the tide went out. Another delegate said that in their desire to keep back the Kaiser's hordes the workers seemed to have been willing to create a horde of Ctesars. If the situation were so critical as Mr. Lloyd George had represented, why did not the Government take control of all the industries and thus have put themselves in a position to produce the required munitions without having to pay the capitalist profits on them, and whilst, at the same. time, paying the workers who .produced those munitions a fair wage?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL .%ND WAR</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL .%ND WAR</p>
        <p>ORK.</p>
        <p>Complaint was made at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council held on Tuesday evening, that the Council had not been consulted in the matter of the nomin- ation of workers'. representatives on the Man- chester and District Armaments Output Com- mittee, whose sphere of operations included the borough.—The Chairman (Mr. Berry) thought that the Trades Council had been slighted. Seeing that they represented the workers of Warrington he thought they should have been appealed to to nominate represen- tatives.—Mr. C. Dukes the secretary) said that Messrs. Sutton (of the Ironfounders! UniOn) and Oxley. (of the Amalgamated Society of Engineers) had been appointed on the com- mittee, but the Trades Council had had no say in nominating them.-3.1r. C. l?linston thought it was a pity to waste time on such a matter, and moved that .the Council should not recog- nise thcB committee "in any way, shape or form."—This was seconded • and carried.—Mr. Dukes added that the Chairman and himself had both filled in forms stating their willing- ness to work.</p>
      </div>
