      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>HOLIDAY OF HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>HOLIDAY OF HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>(Queries and correspondence should be addressed to "The Hub," c/o The Editor. Replies can- not be sent by post and all queries will be answered in our columns.)</p>
        <p>At the moment when nearly everyone is find- ing it essential to exercise the most rigid economy in order to make both ends meet,' it might appear unfortunate that the railway companies have found it necessary to withdraw all their cheap travelling facilities. As a mat- ter of fact, however, it may prove a blessing in disguise, for it is bound to have the effect of increasing greatly the number of those who take their. holiday awheel. And this is really thq holiday of holidays. There is no other way of seeing the beauties of the country so well ; there is no other exercise which is so health- giving; and there is no other way of spending so cheap a holiday. There is no difficulty what- ever in finding excellent accommodation in cottage, farmhouse, or inn. And the prices 9harged are astonishing to those who. have only had experience of the ordinary seaside lodgings. A clean and comfortable bed and a wholesome breakfast may be had for 2s. Gd.; anothei 2s. Gd. for dinner and tea is ample. This works out at less than £2 a week. A good bicyck must be procured and a careful examinatior before making a start ensures easy and smooth running. Too Much must not be attempted One doesn't want to exhaust oneself; for ir this case no real benefit will be obtained Thirty, forty, fifty miles a day, according the individual is ample, and enables one tc secure the full benefit of the holiday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>SUSSEX TRIP.</head>
        <p>A SUSSEX TRIP.</p>
        <p>To those who wish to see the best of thc South I recommend the charms of Sussex, which are wore apparent now than at any other period aT the whole year. Sussex is first and foremost a "summer. county." An attrac- tive trip, taking in nearly all the beauty spots. is from • London to Eastbourne, thence tc Chichester and back to London by Midhurst and Guildford. The best way out of London h• via Kennington, Streatham and Croydon tt Purloy,- then through Claterham and Godstom to East Grinstead, a delightful old-world tciwr in the midst of typical Sussex scenery. Tin ride across Ashdown Forest soon after leavint East Grinstead is a. lovely one, while the roac surface is in excellent condition. The ascent to Wytch Cross is severe, but the views amply repay one for the climb. By a series of easv descents, through Nutley and Mansfield, field is reached. Care is necessary in descend. ing into the town, especially as a level-crossint. has to be -negotiated in the main street. Frei', uckfield the road passes through East Ileathly Ilailshatu, and Wellington. From Eastbourm to Seaford the road over Beachy Head shouh be taken, for although it is extremely hilly ane often rough, it is more interesting than tin main road through Polegate and Alfriston. Tlli road winds easily from Seaford to Newhaven. and then follows a fine run along the top oi the cliffs to Brighton. The Channel is in full view all the way to Shoreham, after hcl comes a glorious wooded run till the Duke el Norfolk's mighty castle at Arundel comes it sight. Passing under its lee, keep on tt Chichester, with its graceful • cathedral spire. and then there are two good routes to Guild- ford, but the prettier and the one at the Present moment with the better surface is via Single- ton, Cocking, Midhurst, Haslenttere, Milford and Godalmin,g. This leads through a real Poet's country, where wood and heath blend in gorgeous colouring. From Guildford the route lies through Ripley, Esher, and Kingston. The distance of the round trip is 171 miles, and those visiting the south will find that this route will give them ndvel and beautiful scenes that will \ remain sweet memories for many a day to come. A TIP FROM MOTORISTS.</p>
        <p>Cycli4s as a body are progressive in spirit, and they are always ready to pick up a wrinkle from those who enjoy other pastimes. Many motorists keep their lamps encased in a canvas bag wlien'not in use. Their object is not only to keep the metal bright, but the cover pre- vents dust and dirt from penetrating into the lamp. The large•numbc.ir of cyclists Who have trouble with their lamps might well learn a lesson from this. With the average rider the number of miles he _travels with the lamp alight is only a very small proportion of the distance he .coves with it out. All the time the lamp is collecting dust and dirt, and this accumulates to such an extent that when the lamp is wanted it sometimes requires a con- siderable amount of coaxing to get a good light. Bags can be purchased for a few coppers. The result is well worth the outlay.</p>
        <p>MOTOR CYCLES IN THE ITALIAN ARMY. There are very few private motor cycles in Italy to-day. The Italian Government has requisitioned practically all of them for the use of the army. Not only have machines been specially ordered from the Italian firms, but nearly all the British made motor cycles have been taken up for war work. A number of side- cars are in use, these being principally employed for taking artillery officers from one battery to another. The Italian Army has also a number of machine gun sidecar outfits, these having been built by a native firm. For con- veying the wounded the combination machine</p>
        <p>A patient once asked her physician why his moustache was black and his hair was grey. "Why," he replied, "my moustache is 20 years younger than the hair on my head."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>At the Circus.</head>
        <p>At the Circus.</p>
        <p>First Freak: ,Did you know that the strong lady and the contortionist were going to be married? Second Freak: Oh, are they? I suppose she'll be able to twist him around her finger now.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>ENGINE COMPRESSION AS A BRAKE.</head>
        <p>ENGINE COMPRESSION AS A BRAKE.</p>
        <p>A considerable number of expert riders make very little use of the brakes attached to their motor cycles. They trust to the compression of the engine to act in this way. The effect of raising the valves is to turn the engine into an air pump and the braking is more effective. On the down stroke of the piston no return, of energy is passed to the flywheel, and, more- over, energy is used sip in pumping the air in and out of the cylinders. In addition to this the friction of the engine acts as a brake. The magneto cut-out on a racing machine is a good means of slowing for rounding corners end it saves the necessary effort required for lifting the valves. On a two-speed machine, dropping into low gear, and keeping the clutch at such a point that it clips and does not turn the engine will produce a considerable braking influence. NEW LIGHTING RESTRICTION.</p>
        <p>In these days the law is everything. Motorists will therefore, be wise to remember a new Order in Council made under the Defence of the Realm Act:—" No lamp shall without law- ful authority be carried on any vehicle (other than a locomotive or carriage on a railway) which displays, any coloured light except such coloured light as may be required by any law or regulation for the time being in force. No lamp shall without lawful authority be carried on any such vehicle unless it is so constructed, fitted and attached.</p>
        <p>(a). -,As n&amp; to be capable of movement in- dependent of the movement of the vehicle, and (b). As not to throw light in any direction other than than which the vehicle in proceeding or is intended to proceed, ex- cept in the case of lamps required by any law or regulation for the time being in force to throw its light in some other direction.</p>
        <p>Where nuy lamp is carried, in any vehicle in rontravention of this regulation, the person in rharge or having control of the vehicle will be held responsible. Nothing, however, in the regulation affects the red and green side panels with which lamps on the vehicles are usually fitted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>ALCOHOL AS FUEL.</head>
        <p>ALCOHOL AS FUEL.</p>
        <p>A great amount of interest is being taken in the new fuel, namely, "Natalite." A motoring ontemporary has been carrying .out some ex- periments with regard to; its value, and the results aDpear to be very satisfactory. It .has been the general opinion that a speedily con- itructed engine was necessary in the case of Alcohol, but from what I hear the new spirit ~ftri be used in the ordinary petrol engine with- 3ut alteration, though the results are somewhat better if the supply of air be reduced. In con- lection with the removal of the duty of alcohol it is interesting to note that a mixture of ben- -7.01e and natalite gives good results. The mixture would be undrinkable, and could therefore be sold duty free. The two tulvan- tages of benzole—namely, carbon deposit and the difficulty in starting, are not apparent in fhe,mixture. The natalite gives off ether and is stated to be a carbon remover.</p>
        <p>TIIE RUB.</p>
      </div>
