      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>Mr. Shaw Takes Up The Cudgels On Behalf Of The Old Folk.</head>
        <p>Mr. Shaw Takes Up The Cudgels On Behalf Of The Old Folk.</p>
        <p>A "DAUNTLESS THREE" DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>A division was taken at the fortnightly meet- ing of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday, in the matter of a proposition made by.Mr.'Shaw that the tea given to the inmates ' of the House be of the same quality as that pro- Tided for the staff. Mr. Shaw was supported - in his worthy effort by Mrs. Hall and the Rev. A. M. Mitchell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>WORD FOR THE INMATES.</head>
        <p>A WORD FOR THE INMATES.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Shaw, in moving his proposition, said that there were in the House many old people who. were there from no fault of their own, but were the victims of circumstances. He felt that, in the matter of tea, no line of demarca- tion should be dittwn between the staff and the inmates. It was a question of only a penny or twopence, and he contended that it would be money nobly spent. In any case, the lowest figure submitted when contracts were invited did not always represent the best value. It was not always wise to accept the lowest tender for goods, as the, Guardians almost invariably did. Personally, he was. sure that if the Guardians showed a little more confidence in their own local tradesmen they would be hotter served than at present. -It would he fairer to. the ratepayers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>REV. A. M. MITCHELL SAYS: " A GOOD CUP."</head>
        <p>THE REV. A. M. MITCHELL SAYS: " A GOOD CUP."</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. 31. Mitchell As a tea-drinker, I second what Mr. Shaw has proposed, because I do think that the old people—and especially the old women—ought, if they have tea at all, to have a good et). Mrs. Hall: Hear, hear.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell went on to inquire how long the supplies of tea lasted. He noticed that a hundred pounds had been ordered for the officers, and a thousand pounds for the inmates. How long dikl such quantities last? The Chairman: The end of September. The Rev. A. 4. Mitchell: That's not much more than two months. It seems rather a lot for that time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather: Hear, hear. I'm glad to hear Mr. Mitchell criticise tea drinking. (Laugh- ter). The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: If we had a better class of tea, we could put less in the teapot. (Laughter.) I would warn everybody—and I'm sure Mr: Mather will support me—not to over- drink tea. (Renewed laughter.) THE OTHER SIDE. •</p>
        <p>The Chairman: For a long 7 period', nothing but good tea has been supplied- to the inmates, and the suggestion that the old people haven't had good tea is unwarrantable. The Guardians raised the price some time back and have taken care ever since- that nothing but .good tea should be served. That there should be some differentiation between what was supplied to the officers and What was provided for the in- mates has always been an understood thing. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell pointed out that he didn't mean to suggest that the tea supplied to the inmates was bad tea, but he thought, in that particular item, the usual differentiation might be dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>"EATABLES, DRINKABLES AND WEARABLES."</head>
        <p>"EATABLES, DRINKABLES AND WEARABLES."</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather, alluding to the remark of the Chairman as to the suggestion that the inmates were supplied with poor tea, said that it wasn't only the tea that was good; the Chairman's remark as to the quality of that article applied to everything else provided by the Onardians— " eatables, drinkables and wearables." On a division being taken there voted for r. Shaw's proposition : Mrs. Hall, the Rev. A. M. Mitchell and Mr. Shaw. Ten voted against, and one member did not vote.</p>
