      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>GEORGE V. SHIELDS.</head>
        <p>GEORGE V. SHIELDS.</p>
        <p>Presented To Boys' And Girls' Schools For Athletic Prowess.</p>
        <p>N 1.1% OR 0 V U•( .kTION.</p>
        <p>A pleasing little ceremony took place at the National School on Monday afternoon, when the Mayor presented the George V. Boys' Athletic Shield to the boys of that institution—who are to bold it for six months. Besides his Worship there were also present: Messrs. A. T. Hallaway (Deputy Town Clerk); and J. Moore Murray (Director of Education), Councillor J. T. Locker (school manager), Rev. T.. Vickers, and Mr. G. W. A. Brown (headmaster).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>MR. BROWN AND MAYOR'S VISITS.</head>
        <p>MR. BROWN AND MAYOR'S VISITS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brown, in introducing the Mayor to the boys, said it was not often that they had the pleasure Of having a Mayor at the school. The rast time was at the Coronation, and the pre- vious occasion was' in 1909, when the school was extended, and Alderman Forshaw, then Mayor, opened the new section. Now Alderman Pea- cock had come to present the athletic shield which was offered for competition amongst the elementary schools of the toll-Li—the one for boys, of which they had also been the first winners two years before. This year they had gained it together with the boys of the Bolton Council School,' and would hold it for six . months. Those who had gained it for them were: Albert Brookes, six - points; Harry Wyatt, 6; Alfred Livesey, 6; .Edward Birchall, 4; Roland Openshaw, 2; Fred Holden, 2; John Graham, 2; Herbert Isherwood, t; Joseph Riley, 1; Frank Daintith, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>DO HIGH SCHOOLS FALL SHORT?</head>
        <p>DO HIGH SCHOOLS FALL SHORT?</p>
        <p>The Mayor said that was a duty which gave him Very great pleasure. He was sorry that the Mayoress could not come, but it was one of her days at the Lord Derby War Hospital. He was sure that if they would go on with their school 'work in the same manner as they. did on that sports day which he enjoyed amongst them, they would some day make names for themselves which would cause' them to look back with pleasure upon the days when they received their education. He did not think that the best education was obtained at what they called the high schools. He was never, ashamed to confess • that the only school he 'went to—or could afford to go' to—was an elementary school. But to-day, thanks to the generosity of the municipality and the Government, boys and girls were enabled to go to the high schools if they wished. They had also better ideas of teaching. Yet sometimes he did not think the community got the best results for all the money which they spent upon the scholars; Let them, however, take all the advantages they could and every penny of the money they could earn; and then perhaps they would some day attain high positions. .It was a very easy matter to be mayor of a town; but it was a very difficult one to attain higher positions, and education would be of great benefit to them in this life. Let them take all their opportunities! lie congratulated the school very heartily. ( A pplause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>PATRIOTIC SONGS.</head>
        <p>PATRIOTIC SONGS.</p>
        <p>Three cheers for the school were then called for by the Headmaster, and were given in fine style. Afterwards the scholars sang "Excelsior," "God Bless the Prince of Wales," and the French and English National Anthems, Mr. Fred Daintith presiding at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>BEAMONT GIRLS IIECEIVE THE</head>
        <p>BEAMONT GIRLS IIECEIVE THE</p>
        <p>SHIELD.</p>
        <p>Boy And Girl Champions From Ihe</p>
        <p>'Same Council Schools.</p>
        <p>The other George the Fifth Challenge Shield was formally presented to the Beamont Council School by Mrs. J. Moore Murray, the wife of the Director of Education, who, in hand- ing over the trophy—which will be held by the school; for a twelvemonth—warmly congratu- lated the girls upon their prowess on the green sward. When she was a schoolgirl, she said, it was thought that sports were not the things for girls, and that those who dared indulge in them were tom-boys. But. that stupid notion had quite (lied away. Mrs. Moore Murray also ex- pressed the hope that that would not be the last occasion on which herself, or, somebody else, would have the pleasure of presenting the girls' shiela to Beamont School. Having handed the shield over to the custody of the representatives of the scholars, Mrs. Moore Murray proceeded to present a gold pendant, the gift of the committee of the local branch of the N.U.T., to Lily Goodwin, who in return, presented to Mrs. Moore Murray,. on behalf of the top two classes, a beautiful bouquet of roses.</p>
        <p>NINETEEN PRIZES WON! BY BEAMONT</p>
        <p>GIRLS.</p>
        <p>Prior to the ,presentation ceremony, Miss Taylor, the headmistress, mwitioned that 238 girls of Beamont School entered their narrieS for the sports, and between. them they won 19 'prizes, They obtained a total of 52 points, and became holders of the girls' shield by a margin of three points, after, last year, just failing to win it by one point.• Before and after the presentation ceremony, the scholars submitted a musical programme, which included Kipling's "Recessional," Elgar's "Land of Hope and Glory," and "Rule Britannia," whilst a number of the girls per- formed the sailors' hornpipe, and Scotch, Irish and Welsh dances..</p>
        <p>It may recalled that one of the Beamont girls distinguished herself at the recent sports by tying with M. Gleave, of Holy Trinity, for the girls' individual championship. One of the Beamont boys, Jas. Emery, won the boys' championship outright and, after she had finished in the girls' department, Mrs. Moore Murray proceeded to the boys' school in order to present Emery with a silver medal, given by Mr. Hallett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>LABOUR MARKET.</head>
        <p>THE LABOUR MARKET.</p>
        <p>In the trades compulsorily insured against unemployment the perEentage of unemployment at July :2 was 0.96, as compared with 0.92 a month ago and 3.53 a year ago.{ It ovill be seen that the rate of unemployment in these trades remains less than a thjrd the rate of a year ago. As regards the uninsured trades, the number on the registers of the Labour Exchanges shows an increase on the figure of a month ago, being 63,693, as compared with 58,262.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE CONSTABULAR Y.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>Appointment Of .lelim9 Under-Chief</head>
        <p>Appointment Of .lelim9 Under-Chief</p>
        <p>Constable.</p>
        <p>Superintendent George Ennion, head of the Northwich Police Force, has been appointed Acting-Deputy Chief- Constable of Cheshire, under Acting Chief Constable Leah. • . Superintendent Ennion has seen over thirty- four years' service in the Cheshire constabu- lary, and was, before going to Northwich, the superintendent at Runcorn,for twelve years. The ' Cheshire Chief Constable, Major Mal- colm ,is serving in the Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
