      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>•:• •:• + •:• +•:•s:•+•:••:•+•: • s :• . :• • :. • :• •:. •:. •:• •:• •:• •:• •:• •41. sl'• +++ + 4. •:• + •:. •:is + •:. •:• •:• •:• + •:• •:• •:. •:• •:• + •:• +++++++ + + + + + + •:. + •:. + + •:. •:• + •:. •:. •:. + + •:. •:. + •:. + + + •:. •:. •:. •:is + + •:. •:.• + •:• •:. •:s. + •:. •:. •:. + 4.:4 + 1:• + •:• + 4:. •:• •:• •:. •:• •:• •:. + •:• + + 44. + 4 0:. + 4:* + + + •:0 + + + 0) + + +</p>
        <p>•:. •:. •:. 4- 4. •:. •:. 4. •:. •:. •:. •:. •:- •:. •:. .I.</p>
        <p>1 ,"' • ", 44; , d „ ; • MI WOR LD•</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Women and War Service. The war service movement for women is very much to the fore, aid women generally hope that the National Register will help them to find ways and means to share in the work of the nation. There is the difficulty experienced by most women in saying in what rapacity they can best utilise their energies and talents, and there is the objection which ninny entertain in 'regard to disclosing their age, and other details, but everywhere there is the desire to treat the National Register seriously, and make any use it affords for enabling women to under- take war work. It is the claim of women to help in this emergency, that inspired the great demonstration and war service procession of women in London this week, organised by the Women's Social. and Political Uu ion. Mrs. Pankhurst believes that we should have been more energetic and successful in conducting the war, if women had had, an effective voice in the nation's 'affairs, but from the first she has patriotically abandoned politics and ili- tancy in the desire to aid the country in its present grave situation.</p>
        <p>French Women at the Bar and on the .Land. Women barristers have, since the outbreak of war, scored many successes in France, where they have been allowed to plead in the • onsei:s de Guerre, and have been compliznent.A on their eloquence by the most .learned of the judges. On the land, the. women of France are doing the most wonderful work, more especially in the department of the . Aisne, which was over-run and utterly ruined by the Germans. The destruction of the once pros- perous and flourishing towns and villages, and the devastation wrought on the farms, must be seen to be fully realised. With the aid of the Society of Friends, huts have been built near the ruined and pillaged farMhouses, and some of the land is bearing crops, which are looking well. The women are in many in- stances widows, and most have lost relatives, but they are all the time making a brave effort to rebuild their shattered fortunes, even though :there is still the haunting fear that the work of regeneration may be undone by a return of the ruthless invaders.</p>
        <p>The Jews in the Armies of Belligerents. The Jews have, contrary to ancient custom, joined in the world war. Thousands of Jews are fighting in Russia, Austria and Germany, and not a corps, regiment or division in the British Army is without its contingent of Jews. In these, every type and every class are d represente; even the almudic Schools con- tribute a considerable number to the' new armies. For some. time changes have been going on in the education and outlook of the Jew. Compelled to lead a sedentyry business life, the Jew in former limes was not a fighting man. Of late, however, the young Jew has taken to .athletics, and entered the Army and Navy, as well as various professions, and thousands have lately volunteered for active service. The Colonial contingents have ninny Jews among them,, and in- our base hospitals there are Many Jewish doctors, nurses and orderlies. No More Williams. Wars have invariably furnished a variety of baptismal names for children. The names of famous battles, towns and generals have been turned to account in this way, but so far there has. been no distinguishing episode in the war to catch the popular fancy in this way. That will no doubt come when a decisive event favourable to our arms is achieved. The war, however, is responsible for the general ostracis- ing of the name of William. Until last year it was the Most common of masculine Christian names. An, examination of " front names" showed that William was easily first, then next in order came John, thomas, George, James, Charles and Henry. William is now avoided, or at any rate given second or third place. People do -not wish to name their offspring after William the Horrible, the Butcher, of Potsdam, whose wife occupies her time in teaching little children the motto "Gott Strafe England." The. name William has become almost as unpopular as the German measles. German Influence on Paris Fashions.</p>
        <p>Of late years French fashions have not main- tained their former high standard. Before the war they became extravagant and freakish, and were often in the worst possible taste. This deterioration was said to be due to the influence which the Germans managed to acquire, even in the Realm of Fashion. The fact that this influence has for the present vanished, may account for the simple yet graceful, and withal practical styles that now prevail. The changes that the war has -brought about are indeed</p>
        <p>drastic, but in the matter of comfort as well as good taste, each and all are for the better. That the slouch silhouette has gone, no one will regret, for it threatened to become in some instances almost a disfigurement, or malforma- tion.- Now a Ratural, straight, upright, supple back is demanded by the short skirt and little military coat that so often accompanies it, and also d smaller waist, but " pinching in " is not likely to become fashionable.</p>
        <p>Inexpensive Accessories. Buckles are having a vogue just now, and a new substance which has the merit of being in- expensive, as Well as very effective, is the latest thing in these decorative accessories: The material is said to be made of milk, but it has the appearance of ivory, or pebble, highly polished. It can be very brilliantly eoloured, is unbreakable, and very durable. The buckles made of it are in curious, shapes, and sometimes take the. form of large insects, such as butterflies or moths. Some kind of orna- mental, but inexpensive fastening, for keeping a feather boa in place has limg been needed. Quite an ingenious one has been designed from this new material that cannot fail to find a welcome. Italy is providing the most popular parasol of this season. These are also very cheap, being made of gay-coloured cotton finished- with striped borders, alSo in bright colours. These parasols are carried by Italian fieasants as a protection from the hot sun, and are almost universally adopted for seaside and country wear by women of all classes. Picturesque Hats. Black aerophane is very distinctive when used with white lace. A charming July model has a rather high crown of the latter, and a beautifully carved brim of black aerophane. The base_ of the crown was encircled by a narrow black satin ribbon, whiel4, with couple of beautiful roses completed the trim- ming. Some of the most successful summer hats are trimmed with berries., and fruits of all descriptions. Clusters -of rich purple grapes, placed at intervals round the crow,n of a French sailor in grey tagel, made a very effective and handsome trimming. Two flat clusters of cherries also formed a pleasing decoration for a small white tagel hat. Very pretty chapeaux are to be had in muslin and ,coloured linens. Some are embroidered, whilst others are orna- mented with tall straight Mounts of various flowers, in several hues, placed at the top of, the crown, where the band of ribbon is also given an unaccustomed .place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Sleep Before Midnight.</p>
        <p>It is widely known that atuemic girls may long be treated with want of success until they</p>
        <p>- - are compelled to cultivate the .habit of going early to bed., And, though strong and vigor- ous young men may with apparent impunity continue. to hear the chimes at midnight, middle age announce itself by some dulness of the faculties on the days following such ex- periences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>Oatcakes.</head>
        <p>Oatcakes.</p>
        <p>There are various ways of making oatcakes, according as they are desired • thick or thin, rich or "water." For an ordinary (not too thick) oatcake, put two or three handfuls of oatmeal into a bowl, add pinches of salt and baking soda, and then stir in one dessertspoon- ful of dielted dripping or lard, and moisten with just enough boiling water to form a firm dough. Knead the cake very firmly, strewing the baking-board well with meal. Roll out quickly to about a quarter of an inch in thick- ness, cut and fire one side of the cake on a hot girdle, and toast the upper side before the fire. Failing a girdle, the oatcakes can be baked in the oven; they should: take about fifteen min- utes in a fairly quick oven.</p>
        <p>The Cooking of Fish. Fish needs more careful cooking than any other kind of food, and we so often find that it'is spoilt in the cooking, and what might have been an appetising and nourishing dish has been made the reverse by careless cooking. STEAMED FISIL—AII you need is a steamer that will fit on your fish kettle. See that the water is quite boiling in the kettle, then wrap the fish in well-greased paper, put it in the steam, and steam it just a little longer than if you were boiling. One cannot lay down hard-and- fast rules with regard to time, as it depends so much upon the thickness of the fish. Remem- ber that underdone fish is very unwholesome, especially salmon; therefore it is better to err' on the other side. Dish it up 'carefully on to a lace paper, and improve its appearance with some garnish—cucumber, lemon, parsley, or beetroot, cut in .fancy shapes. Serve with</p>
        <p>BAKED Ffsn.—The simplest way to cook fish is to bake it, and it is one of the nicest ways also. Wash the fish, and 'while it is still quite wet put it in a fireproof dish that has a lid. Add a sprinkling of pepper, loz. of butter cut in small piecgs; put on the lid, and bake until cooked through. A medium-sized sole will only need about ten minutes in a good oven. Dish up the fish, add to the liquor a glass of Madeira, a few drops of anchovreSsence, and a few picked shrimps; Maki it hot, pour it over the fish, and serve.</p>
        <p>FRIED Fisn.—Fish fried in egg and bread- crumbs is most delicious. See that the fish is clean. If too large to fry easily, cut it into portions of a convenient size. Then wipe them as dry as possible with in clean cloth. Rub them over with flour to make sure that there is no moisture remaining, dip them into beaten</p>
        <p>egg, and then roll them in fine breadcrumbs. Put sufficient clarified dripping, lard, or any good fat to cover the fish in a deep frying-pan or fish kettle. Wait until it has ceased bub-. Ming and smoke is rising, -then put your pre- pared fish in immediately and fry till a delicate golden brown. Drain them carefully from any superfluous fat, and serve very hot.—" Girls' Realm."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>0.0.4.00...0 .4...+0 ...... • . 4. : Children' 8 Cornero. •:. • 0 . ,:. . 0 Conducted by UNCLE NED. 4. * 4.</head>
        <p>0.0.4.00...0 .4...+0 ...... • . 4. : Children' 8 Cornero. •:. • 0 . ,:. . 0 Conducted by UNCLE NED. 4. * 4.</p>
        <p>Csittnew Or ALL Aote are cordially invited to join this Coiner if they read it regularly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to " "(Isms NED, ' Examiner ' Office, Bewsey- street, Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competitions held every week, to the winners of which useful book-prizes are sent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>HOW DO YOU WANT TO SPEND YOUR</head>
        <p>HOW DO YOU WANT TO SPEND YOUR</p>
        <p>HOLIDAYS?</p>
        <p>AIY DEAR BOYD AND GIRLS,-</p>
        <p>I. suppose you are all looking forward very eagerly indeed to your holidays, are you not? Well, I know that -Lam, and I have just been making arrangements to go away for. a week or two to a beautiful place by the sea- side. "Bow do you want to spend your holidays?" That is the subject Of this week's essay competition. Do not send more, than a few lines, please; and let me have them before Tuesday. I am sure you will, every one, have plenty of ideas for spending a happy holiday —even in time of war. Because you must remember that one of the reasons our sailors and soldiers are fighting so bravely is to let you have your holiday as usual; one of the very last things they would wish for would be for you to-give up this year your annual bit of pleasure. So don't forget! A few lines only, and Monday the closing day! -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>NIECE WHO READS A LOT.</head>
        <p>A NIECE WHO READS A LOT.</p>
        <p>I am always delighted to receive a letter ihe this</p>
        <p>" 52, Chester-road, Warrington, 6/7/15. "Dear Uncle Ned,—l was so delighted when I read the Children's Column and found that I had won the prize for the com- petition; and to-clay it has arrived and I thank you so much for it. I • have quite a lot of books, but not one of them is written by the same author. I shall take great care of it and value it very much. I hope all the other boys apil girls will eater ;for the</p>
        <p>competitions, because if they could only see my book they would be delighted and think it a lovely prize.—With love, from your</p>
        <p>loving. niece, JENNY DUNLOP." lam pleased, you are, Jennie. ''Your lettqr was beautifully written; I am sure you did it to please mg. Thank you very much! NIECE MARIAN BARTON writes: "I thank you very much for having lily letter printed in " The Examiner.' Glad to say you pleased me very much."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>The Natural Conclusion.</p>
        <p>"I didn't know your mother was dangerously ill," said the observant neighbour. "Why, she isn't," replied the .dainty daugh- ter. "'What made you think that?" " I saw you washing the dishes this morn-</p>
        <p>ing." And is it any wonder that they never speak as they pas by ?</p>
        <p>Praise for Mothers.</p>
        <p>All that I am or hope to be," said Lincoln, after he had become Presideht, " I owe to my angel mother." "My mother was the making of me," said Thomas Edison recently. "She was so true, so sure of pie; and I felt that I had someone to live for; someone I must not disappoint." , "All that I have ever accomplished ih life," declared Dwight L. Moody, the great evangelist, "I owe to my mother."</p>
        <p>"To the man who has had a mother, all women are sacred for her sake," said Jean Paul Richter. •</p>
        <p>The testimony of great men in acknowledg- ment of the boundless debt they owe to their mothers would make a record stretching from tlte dawn of history to to-day. -Few men, in- deed, become`great who do not owe. their great- ness to a mother's love and inspiration. How often we hear People in every Walk of life say, " I never could have done this thing but for my mother. She believed in me, encouraged me, when others saw nothinin me."</p>
        <p>" A-kiss from my mother,maile me a painter," said Benjamin Wes 1, A distinguished man of to-day says: " never could have reached my present position had I not known that my mother expected me to reach it. From a child she made .me feel that this was the position she expected, me to fill; and her faith spurred, me on and gave Me the power to attain it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>MORE MEMBERSHIP.</head>
        <p>MORE MEMBERSHIP.</p>
        <p>And hello! Here is another letter to cheer my old heart! " 123, Orford-lane, Warrington, July 6th,</p>
        <p>• 1915. • , "Dear Sir,—l wish to become a member of your club, asking you to let .me know what is. my number. My name is Tot WILLIAMS, and I live at the address given above: My brother also Wishes to become a member of your club, as you will .see. We both go to Hamilton-street School—my . brother • being aged nine years and in Standard 11., ..and myself aged 11- years in Standard V. We Itopeo be able to win a prize and prosper in our work. Afy brother's game is-GeoauE WILLIAMS, living at the same..address.—l re- main, your loving nephew,. Tom WILLIAMS. "P.s.—My brother ;wishes to • know his number." Very good, Toni! Your number is -40, and George's is 463. I wish you all good luck in our competitions. LOse no time in starting!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>WARRINGTON STREETS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON STREETS.</p>
        <p>And vOw For our latest competition.. We have not had quite so good an entry as usual —which is very 'surprising when one thinks how very easy. it is to write an essay about any street on earth ! Why, even a country lane without a person or house in it is really entrancing, full of Nature's wonders and peopled . with all sorts of industrious little creatures. How much more interesting, then, are human beings, and the things they make and do—all to be ,seen in the streets! Perhaps you have never stayed to think what a • lot there is to be seen in a street. People, their clothes and habits; shops and houses, their contents, keepers, structure, aspect and condition; carts, their •contents and occupants; motor cars; trams.; 'buses; barrel organs and their music; animals; hoardings teinhi a wonderful little art gallerx); statues; paving; side-streets and their direction—there is no end to the interesting things about a street. Charles Lamb, who was perhaps our finest essayist, said that the countryside was almost as good</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Child Labour Dangers. The doctor severely condemns child labour. "A few more generations of it," he says, 'and we will become a race,of degenerates and weak- lings. ,A child's place is in the school and playground. It should have fresh air, sunshine and joyous companionship instead of being forced into a mine, mill or factory. A nation which permits child labour to exist cannot call itself civilised. It must at once abolish child labour to give up the pretence of being pro- gressive."</p>
        <p>Poppy for Gnat Bites. correspondent writes Perhaps your gardening readers will be interested to learn that Bocconia cordata, a,free-growing perennial, sontains a juice which is 'a remedy , for gnat bites. The juice is in the stalk of the leaf, and if squeezed out and applied to the spot bitten within a few minutes of the bite being inflieted, immediate relief is procured."</p>
        <p>Tired Body and Mind. • "Investigations have shown," Says Dr. Morris Korshet, of Passaic, New Jersey, " that when the work capacity of the body is taxed, fatigue Products or toxines are elaborated, and that these toxines circulating in the blood and act- ing upon the brain and muscles are responsible for the tired feeling. When the blood of a fatigued animal is injected into a healthy one the latter soon beconies fatigued. " -Unless fatigue is checked by rest or abstin- ence from hard, intense work, there ensues a condition which may be called chronic 'fatigue. The worker is continually tired. If he is . a Mental worker he finds that he has lost interest in his ,work, lie feels flabby and dispirited and cannot concentrate his mind upon any particu- lar subject. If a manual worker, he becomes enervated, his muscles lose their customary skill, resulting in botched work or accidents. "Chronic fatigue creates a craving for stimu- lants, alcohol, tobacco, tea, coffee, heavy dinners and trashy amusements: in short, the pace that kills is not the result of sinfulness and depravity, but of a tired body and mind. These things are taken in the hope of instilling energy into a -rebellious body, just as a horse IS doped for a race.-</p>
        <p>"Chronic fatigue is responsible in a large measure for degeneracy. Exhausting work breeds the opposite extreme of abhorrence of work. Men become tramps and in turn become criminals they commit crime for the mere excitement of it, just as a child will set fire to a house to see the engines run."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <p>as a street; and you may be sure that he knew what'. he was talking about. Personally I do not agree -a bit with what he meant; still, that does_ not say he was wrong, does it?</p>
        <p>I award the prize to 444 NIECE MARIAN BARTON,</p>
        <p>for the following nice letter : "42, Wakefield-street, Warrington, July 12th, 1915.</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Aled,—l ail' going to tell you about Bridge-street It-is very long and very wide. There are shops on each side of the street. At one end of Bridge-street 'stands a policeman, because that part ,of /the street is very dangerous.. Warrington Bridge is situated at the other end. Opposite the bridge stands the statue of Oliver Cromwell. He looks a very stern man.—l will now close my letter because I must remember you cannot bear long ones.—l remain, your affec- tionate niece, 441 MARIAN BARTON."</p>
        <p>Another good letter comes from NEPHEW SAM BACKHOUSE-: „</p>
        <p>".G, Milner-street, Warrington, 11th July, 1915. ,</p>
        <p>" Deitr.Vticle Ned,—You asked me to write about ,any street in Warrington, so I chose 'Bridge-street. This thoroughfare is the chief street in my residential town. Bridge- street derives its name from the bridge at the end of it. This bridge was at one time the only bridge spanning the River Mersey. Improvements are being made at the Market Gate end of the above-mentioned, thorough- fare. In Bridge-street is the terminus of 'the tramways. Near the new bridge stands the monument of Oliver Cromwell, who fought a battle at the bottom of Bridge- street. The chief Warrington branch of Boots Cash Chemists stands in the 'Street which is the subject of my essay.—From your loving nephew, Si BACKHOUSE."</p>
        <p>I am very glad indeed to see that 434 NIECE HILDA HASSALL has sent a capital essay. She has done what I want you all to strive for— covered a good many points and still kept the essay ' short by leaving out unnecessary matter :</p>
        <p>"36, Factory-lane, Warrington, July 12th,</p>
        <p>1915. • "Dear Uncle Ned,—l have decided to write about Bridge-street. This .street is a very busy one. and contains numerous shops and big buildings. It is rather a dangerous - 'street, because of the different kinds of traf- fic, which come so quickly that people have hardly time to turn round, as it were. It is a, large strvet, and all kinds cif people, rich</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>(Cljurrij</head>
        <p>(Cljurrij</p>
        <p>AA ESLEYAN CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Election Of Dr. Moss :as President.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan Conference held its opening session on Wednesday evening, at the Central Hall, Birmingham, the Rev. Dinsdale T. Young (the retiring president) in the chair. The Secretary of the ' Conference reported that a young Wesleyan minister, the Rev. Wm.' Dimmock, had been killed in action in East Africa. His • home was in the Birmingham district, and he had been trained at Richmond College. The Conference expressed its deep sympathy with the relatives. This is the first casualty in the ranks of the Wesleyan ministry. The Rev. James Hanby invited the Confer- ence of 1916 to Hull, and the invitation was accepted.•• • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>Tho New President</head>
        <p>Tho New President</p>
        <p>An interesting scene occurred when the Legal Hundred cast every yote for the Rev. Dr. R. Waddy Moss as president. The Rev. Simpson Joimson was unanimously re-elected secretary _of Om Conference for an- other year. Revised. First Draft of, Stations.</p>
        <p>The changes included the following joseph Benson vice Leonard. EVallS ; Leeds (Brunswick), George Frazer vice. John Edwards and George W. Olien vice, J. Oswald Warbur- ton ; Leeds (Wood house Moor), H. G. Price vice' Charles Daw ; Wakefield, John Edwards vice George Frazer; Pateley Bridge, Stanley Hoare Vice Joseph Parson ; Sheffield (Park-street), F. J. Brewer vice J. W. Acres; Liverpool (Wool- ton), ( htarles Daw vice Bay Scott; Manchester (Radnor-street); J. W. Stormouth vice Philip Thompson; Manchester (Chee.tham Bill), Wal- ter Vevor vice Bright Jehu; Leigh, Bright Jelin vice Walter. Trevor; Farnworth, A. F. Chaplin vice George W. Oliver; Colne, J. Leonard Webber vice Herbert Daplin; Wigan, Philip Thompson; Bebden Bridge, Stanley C. Jones 'vice Francis Lloggarth ; Bradford (Ship- ley), F. C. Hoggarth vice John Ward; Keighley, J. 0. Warburton vice Percy Pegler; Lough- borough, Frederick W. Gostich vice J. S. Hicks; Belper, Arthur B. Cannon vice Ernest T. Nut- tall ; Bridlington, • G. J. Chamberlain vice Douglas Jackson; Scunthorpe, W. Watson (C.) vice Joseph G. Benson; Gateshead (Benshaw: road), Thomas B. Kirkness vice East; Durham, Enoch Green vice James Jenkin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>UNITED METHODISTS IN CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>UNITED METHODISTS IN CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Ne‘% President.</p>
        <p>The eighth annual conference of the 'United ethodist Church • counnouccd its session in _Exeter ,on Tuesday evening. The retiring President (the Rev. George Parker) occupied the chair. There were 360 delegates ,f)rcent when the roll was called.</p>
        <p>In accordance with the designation of last conference, the Rev. • .1. B. Stedeford, Hull, was unanimously elected president. Councillor J. A. Lorain, Exeter, was appointed general secretary; the Rey. J. 11. "Burkitt, Liverpool, minute secretary; the Rev. Charles Pye, Car- diff, journal secretary; and the IteN% C. A. Ashelford, 'Huddersfield, corresponding 'secre- tary. • The Connexional secretary, the Rev. Di.. Packer, Leedi, was re-elected.</p>
        <p>The history of the movement which had cul- minated successfull3l in enabling the three United Methodist Churches to have their own chaplains with the forces was explained by the Rev. "'env Smith, the Connexional editor. The movement was started by the .Portsmouth district in 1912. Baptists, Congregationalists, and Primitive Methodists co-operated with the united Methodist Churches and the members of the bar denominations now with the .colours numbered 100,900.</p>
        <p>• It was decided to send a message tO every member who was in the Army and Navy, and a resolution of congratulation to Lance-Corpl. Leonard Keyworth, Lincoln, the first membet .of the United Methodist Church to be decor- atkd with the Victoria Cross.</p>
        <p>In reference to the effort bn behalf of foreign Missions, it was 'announced that towards the £l,OOO it was hoped to raise, I:586 had been already promised. . Appointments.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. Pike was elected governor of Shelibear College, and the Rev. R. S. Hall was designated governor of Edgehl tollege. Four ministers were allowed JO remain in the sta- tions without pastoral charge. The Rev. James Proudfoot, West Africa, was accepted for ser- vice in_England.</p>
        <p>The Rev: T. J. Cox, Bristol, was elected Pre- sident-designate.</p>
        <p>MISS ASQUITH'S Ai EDDING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>Postponed Thrh An Attack Of Typhoid Fever.</head>
        <p>Postponed Thrh An Attack Of Typhoid Fever.</p>
        <p>Miss Violet Asquith, whose engagement to Mr. :Bonham Carter, the Prime Minister's private secretary, was recently announced, the wedding being fixed for July 27th, has con- tracted typhoid fever and the wedding has been indefinitely postponed. Miss Asquith has just returned from a visit to her wounded brother in Egypt</p>
        <p>Mr:darter and his fiancee have received many messages of sincere Sympathy and expressions of hope for the complete and speedy- recovery of the patient.</p>
        <p>A. castle in the air should always have a wing or two.</p>
        <p>Pride is an indefinable something that pre- vents a woman from looking her age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>VOLUNTEER CORPS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER CORPS.</p>
        <p>County Meeting Suggests A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>Lancashire Regiment.</head>
        <p>Lancashire Regiment.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the Lord Lieutenant of Lancashire (Lord 'Shuttleworth) a meeting of the lord mayors, the mayors, and other in- terested persons in the bounty was held on Mon' day at Preston to consider the question of the organisation of the Volunteer Training Corps.- Lord Shuttleworth, Lord Pesborough, the Earl of Derby, • and General Sir Henry McKinnon were among the speakers. A committee was formed to consider the .advisability of organis- ing the volunteer corps of the county in a regiment. ,</p>
        <p>No Official Instructions. Lord Shuttleworth said the recruiting in the county had been splendid, and made special reference to what Lord Derby _and the Lord Mayor of Manchester had done. With regard to the organisation of the Volunteer Training Corps, he- asked the meeting, to accept bis assurance that there were convincing reasons why a conference could not have been held earlier with real advantage, and he said .that even now they would not-be able to get further than, the formation of a representative com- mittee to work out a scheme of organisation.. The War Office had adopted no scheme .Of which he could tell the • meeting. It should be understood that for the purpose el the defence of this country in Case of invasion the counties of England were divided into three categories.. First, there were the counties en the, east and south-east coasts,' and in those., counties special instructions had been issued hY the Government to each lord lieutenant. In the counties adjoining those coast counties, in- structions,. -which were limited but very, encouraging to volunteer corps, had recently been issued by the Home Office after considera- tion by the Committee of. Imperial Defence. But the Committee of Imperial Defence de- liberately decided not to issue those instructions . to other counties, and Lancashire was one of : a considerable number of areas in which the Lord Lieutenant had received no instructions, whatever. - Lord Deshorough said that the Government: recogniSed the • corps if affiliated, and woulcl, allow them to bear arms and fight. lie su gested to the Prime Minister and the War Offi that men who volunteered for guarding prison and other duties should be allowed to make form of attestation, putting them wider tory discipline for the time they wefe perforirl•-H ing their duties. Lord Derby Dots Not Wait for thc War Office.</p>
        <p>Lord Derby said that the new. County Coin' mittee it was proposed to form should try t° get everybody to work on the same lines, lie frankly coatfessed that at first he was ver!' much opposed to these voluntary corps over the country. Most of them were nothing but shirkers' homes; flut after 'Lord Desborough's letter, and noting the conditions laid down, ho saw nothing but good in them. The com- mittee's first duty ought to be to lay down general conditions, in addition to, but not con- tra,vening in any way, the conditions of. Lord Desborough's scheme. He agreed that an wanted to belong- to a county regiment, but at the same time he believed that companies or even half-companies were the proper formation, and that these should be amalgamated accord- ing to the areas in which they were raised- The general system adopted in regard to national reserve—namely, raising in companies —should be accepted, but extended, and as companies were formed they should be grouped in battalions. Lord Shuttleworth had said he had received no instructions from the War Office. "I never would wait for them," Lord Derby observed, amid laughter, and he sugges- ted they should follow the general lines of tho instructions giyen, to those counties which had received instructions. He was certain that if the county would adopt a scheme, instead ef waiting for instructions from the authorities, the authorities would signify approval. Officers Would Welcome the Corps. Lord Richard Cavendish suggested that men .Who had been discharged after serving at the Front, and desirous of continuing to do some- thing for their country, could be employed in training and organising new units. He als!: suggested that Territorial depot officers ha° sometimes not much to do, and they might also assist in the organisation. General Mackinnon said that so far as officers and himself were concerned they wnuld warmly welcome the voluntary corps, and do everything they could to help them. There wB5 a good deal to do before they were fit for al'lns; In enumerating some of the duties they coil" discharge, he pointed out how they could re- lieve troops who were guarding , Germalk prisoners, and added that not a day passed Lancashire without a train going through it German prisoners. • • As a result of the conference, ft represetutn- tive committee was appointed to consider how best the Volunteer Corps can be utilised.,</p>
        <p>a</p>
      </div>
