      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Close Finishes In Second Round Of " Finlayson " Cup Contest.</p>
        <p>DRAW FOR THE THIRD ROUND.</p>
        <p>The second round of the Finlayson Cup Com- petition, promoted by the Warrington Works' Amateur Bowling Association, was continued on Saturday, and eight clubs have so far drawn for the third round, which is to commence on the Horse and Jockey green, in Winwick-road, this afternoon. Although the exigencies of war work have frequently necessitated the absence of crack players from various of the teams, the bowling in the first two rounds has, on the whole, been of a decently high order, whilst, all the while it has been interesting, and some exceedingly keen finishes have been witnessed. That the teams 'competing on Saturday were pretty evenly.matched is attested by the fact that in one case a single point separated winners and losers, whilst in two other cases victory was wrested by the narrow margin of five points, and, in yet another, by only seven. Detailed scores of the matches, which were refereed by Mr. T. Humphries, are given below :</p>
        <p>WHITECROSS WIREDRAWERS v. CHESHIRE LINES No. 2.</p>
        <p>The Railwaymen pulled off this match by the I narrowest margin possible; viz., a single point,' and that despite the fact that seven of the twelve sets fell to .the wiredrawers. Three of the- railwaymen, only lost by one each and two others were only beaten by three, the White- crossians being chiefly let down by two of their men,. one of whom only put up 6 out of the 15, whilst another managed but 8. Scores : Whitecross Wiredrawers. Cheshire Lines No. 2. H. Houghton (7) l3 J. Craddock (6) .... 15 W. Ball (3) P. Gleave (2) H. Bennion (4) G. Jones (4) S. Leigh (4) D. Clayton (5) J. Waring (6) J. Norris (6) A. Foster (5) W. Waring (6) T. Sutcliffe (5)</p>
        <p>PEARSON AND KNOWLES' (BEWSEY) v. ARMITAGE AND% RIGBY'S.</p>
        <p>Pearson and Knowles' Bewsey Forge team administered the biggest licking of the after- noon, Armitage and Rigby's team 'being the sufferers, their deficit being the substantial one of 34 points. Nine of the forgemen obtained the aggregate number of points, and five of the cotton workers failed to achieve double figures. Details :</p>
        <p>P. and K. (Bewsey). Armitage and Rigby's J. Coyne (6) ' l5 P. Lawless (4) W. Bowering (3) .... 15 C. North (4) J. Spencer (4) .... 7 J. Bennett (7) W. Meakin (6) W. Hewitt (5) • l3 J. Hardman (7) P. Cowell (3) W. Littlefair (6) W. Blackledge (3) .. 10 H. Holbrook (2) J. Bradshaw (3) .... 15 S. Firth (4) R. Stevens (5) J. Probert (5) D. Armitage (5) .... 15 J. Smith (7)</p>
        <p>165 131 VULCAN ENGINEERS v. WALKER'S No. 1.</p>
        <p>The Brewerymen suffered defeat at the hands of the Vulcan Engineers from Earlestown, though the game was an exceedingly close and well-contested one, only five points separating the two totals at the end. The Engineers were best in seven out of the twelve ends, but there was not a man among the losers but failed to get into double figures, whilst one was only beaten by a single point, another by but two and four by only three. Individual scores-:</p>
        <p>Walker's No. 1. Vulcan Engineers. J. Milner, jun. (6) .. 10 J. Roberts (4) l5 J. Dagnall (4) .... 15 E. Clarke (4) J. Singleton (7) .... 13 A. Harrison (2) A. Glover (7) J. Roberts (6) J. Nevins (6) ... J. Lawton (5) Jas. Litherland (6) 15 F. Jones (2) J. Hill (5) J. Titchard (5) T. Boardman (6) John Smith (7)</p>
        <p>160 165 BELATED FIRST ROUND GAMES.</p>
        <p>PEARSON AND KNOWLES' ENGINEERS v. RYLANDS'S No. 1. These teams met in a belated first round fix- ture, the Engineers proving successful by seven points, although victory in the majority of the individual sets rested with Rylands's men. The Wiremen were unfortunate in having to make a last minute alteration in their team, and this fact largely cbst them the game, for the substitute who understudied Jas. Massey, a good sound player, was able to contribute but six points. The individual scores were:— P. and K. Engineers. Rylands's No. 1. W. Bouchier (6) l2 J. Gleave (scr.) .... 15 W. Leigh (5) H. Bennett (scr.) .... 12 S. Massey (1) J. Pemberton (4) .... 15 G. Monks (2) A. Hackett (5) H. Higham (1) J. Boardman (4) A. Price (4) Jas. Hewitt (2) F. Brandwood (6) .... 15 A. Jolley (3) A. Bennett (6) Jack Hewitt (5) .... 11 G. Shaw (1)</p>
        <p>158 151 LONGFORD No. 1 v. R. A. NAYLOR'S.</p>
        <p>Naylor's put up a good fight, in the first round, against Longford No. 1, but were worsted by five points. Scores</p>
        <p>Longford No. 1. R. A. Naylor's. P. Fearnley (6) —.. 9 E. Lunt (6) R. Ratcliffe (5) J. Johnson (6) P. Whittaker (6) .. J•. Simister (4) D. Meachin (5) . . 15 J. Barnard (6) R. Tickle (5) W. Bampton (6) .... 15 F. Morris (7) John Clare (6) A. Holloway (6) .... 15 C. Ward (4) N. Dale (5) W. Lythgoe (6)</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S CUP-TIES. Four Games Down for Settlement.</p>
        <p>The following teams are drawn to meet to-day in the third round of the Cup Competition, at the times stated :- 2:O.—P. and K. Engineers v. Fletcher, Rus- sell's: 2.4s.—Whitecross Netting v. Crosfield's V.B. 3.3o.—Cheshire Lines No. 2 v. Bewsey Wire Ropery. 4.ls.—Firth Mattress v. Assurance Agents No. 1. Mr. J. Dagnall will be the referee.</p>
        <p>RATES ON FOOTBALL GROUNDS</p>
        <p>Official Advises Appeals To Assessment Committees.</p>
        <p>" A Prominent Yorkshire official of. the Northern Union," says a correspondent in "The Athletic News," "is recommending clubs, and is taking action so far as his own club is con- cerned, to consider the question of rent and assessment during the present crisis. With grounds lyii)g idle thefe is an obvious oppor- tunity to claim on void property or at least to effect a reduction in the assessment. The com- mittee of the club in question are going one better by approaching their landlord and endeavouring to persuade him to accept a rent equivalent to the agricultural land value of the ground. "Clubs with both cricket and football pitches are less favourably situated, but have, of course, a claim to a reduced assessment, par- ticularly in the cases of the various clubs whose cricket is not a paying proposition. It is worthy of note, too, that the modified pro- gramme suggested for the coming winter is also a ground for reduced assessment."</p>
