        <p>HOW THE BOWLERS BOWLED.</p>
        <p>One of the best performances during ihe day was that of P. Gleave, W. (2) who beat T. Hackett, B.H. (scr.) in the second round 15-13, A. Regan, B.H. (scr.) in the preliminary roand 15-13, J. Swanton, B.H. (scr.) in the third round, and wound up the day by beating R. Gregson, P.T. (5) 15-10. C. Smith, K. and Q. (1) was also prominent, his best win being over A. Broady, S.C. (1) 15-5. Geo. Shaw, No. 1, B.H. (scr.) looked, like going strong until Le went under to a prominent novice, F. Hambie- ton, K. and Q. (5) 15-11. H. Hughes, S.C. (1) had a walk over in the second round, but bis third and fourth rounds were stamped with the hall mark of perfection. E. Brookes, B.H. 13) did well, his best win being over J. Isherwood, B.H. (scr.) 15-12. J. Roberts, B.H. (2) also. OKI well, and T. Hockenhull, B.H. (1) found his clubmhte "hot stuff" in the fourth ro 15-11. R. Marsh. S.C. (3) made some txcA- lent returns, and G. Warburton, S.C. (2) found a fellow member too good for him, 15-11. S. Garnett, K. and Q. (4) sailed along nerrily ail "put paid" to H. Whittle, S.C. (4) in the fourth round, 15--8. A. Duckworth, R. (4) 'vas another prominent novice, and sent in s!:•*ne good cards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>FINAL STAGES.</head>
        <p>THE FINAL STAGES.</p>
        <p>As it was unable to finish the main on Satur- day, competition was brought down to the last eight, and these met on Wednesday evening, before an excellent "gate," Who were rewarded with some exceptionally fine bowling. In the fifth round H. Hughes soon had A., DuelcWorth guessing, the result being 15-10. E. Brookes beat S. Garnett, after being in arrears, 15-14. C. Smith and P. Gleave had a "battle royal," Gleave failed to reproduce his Saturday's form, and was beaten 15-13. J. Roberts soon ran away from R. Marsh, and won 15-7. In the semi-finals. H. Hughes again bowled with rare judgment, and E. Brookes was soon in arrears, the result being 15-8. C. Smith also played well and always had the game well in hand, the final score reading 15-6. The final looked like being a rare struggle. but H. Hughes kept up his great form and C. Smith was soon behind, the card handed in reading 15-7. At the conclusion Mrs. Freeman presented the prizes to the winning competitors, and thus</p>
        <p>wit; brought to an end one of the most interest-</p>
        <p>ing handicaps promoted by the Association. Set out in detail the scores in the closing stages were as follows :</p>
      </div>
