      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>LIBERALS' AFTERNOON* OUT.</head>
        <p>LIBERALS' AFTERNOON* OUT.</p>
        <p>Home-and-Home Matches With The</p>
        <p>Stag End In Their Favour.</p>
        <p>The Liberal Bowling Club played off their return match with the Stag Bowling Club on Saturday,- on the green of the Stag Hotel, Lower Walton. The green was in perfect con- dition, and the Liberals, who, on the aggre- gate scores in the home and home matches, won both the singles and doubles, expressed them- selves as delighted with it, although on Satur- day they by no means repeated the performance they gave on their own green a week or two back. The officials were Messrs. F. Hatton (for the Stag) and Geo. Marshall (for the Liberals), and both discharged the duties falling to their lot in an extremely- satisfactory manner. The Stag men did specially well in the singles, in which no less than twelve of the twenty-two Liberal players failed to attain double figures, whilst several: cut a, very sorry, show. In the doubles, however, the-politicians gave a some- what improved display, but the Walton men were still too good for them and ran out win- ners:by 19 points. When the teams met on the green of the Liberal Club the aggregate scores were : Liberals: doubles, 123; singles, 266; Stag: 64; 172. On Saturday the aggregates were :—Stag, 134, 250; Liberals, 115, 183. On home and home matches the totals, therefore, read: Liberals, 238, 429; Stag, 198; 422. Thus the Liberals on the two encounters were 40 to the good on the doubles and seven on the singles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</head>
        <p>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</p>
        <p>The individual scores made on Saturday were as follows:</p>
        <p>Walton.</p>
        <p>Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>s. d. s. d. A. Price S. Wray -13 E. A. Sanders .. 13— 4 W. Fraser H. Arnold J. Buchanan .... 8 J. Winder N. J. Clark .... 11-13 L Graham H. Twiss T. Dobson .....13-13 H. McMahon .... 9-10 F. Arnold B. Smith G. Millington J. Vernon ...... 10-13 F. Walker I. Wilkinson .. 13 F. Sterlingfleet I. Scattergood .. 3-13 H. Bennion 13-12 T. Dooley L. Bogg F. Shaw • 11. Hindes ...• .. 13— 5 G. Marshall -13 W. Swann T. Beswick .... 13-12 A. Timmins -13 R. Wbitfield .... 13 H. Johnson J. Broadhurst, jun 7-13 J. Broadhurst, sen 13-11</p>
        <p>250-13.1</p>
        <p>183-115</p>
        <p>TEA AND THANKS.</p>
        <p>During an interval the players sat. down to- gether to an excellent tea, under the chairman- ship of Mr. Herbert Johnson, Mr. and Mrs, Hatton doing the catering in a manner which won for them a. hearty vote of thanks from those who partook of it. At the conclusion of the games the Liberals accorded a warm expres- sion of thanks to the members of the Stag Club for their hospitality.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>COUNTY INDIVIDUAL CHAMPION-</head>
        <p>COUNTY INDIVIDUAL CHAMPION-</p>
        <p>SHIP.</p>
        <p>Last Year's Champion Beaten In The</p>
        <p>Filial.</p>
        <p>One of the most important events of the bowl- ing season was finished on Saturday at Old Trafford, when the finals of the Lancashire County merit competition were played. The finalists were J. T. Hilton, Wigan Subscription Club, and G. W. Corner, Flixton, who won the championship in 1912. The bowling 'through- out the afternoon was good, and Hilton beat his opponent 21-16. The two players named are eligible to compete in the British Crows. Green championship at Nantwich on August 2, along with J. Hart (Wigan Subscription), J. Charnock (Rainhill), W. 0. Johnson (Pen- dleton Belmont). S. Steele (Leigh Railway), G, Hollis (Flixton Roebuck), and R. Baines (Stanley).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>UNREGISTERED PLAYERS.</head>
        <p>UNREGISTERED PLAYERS.</p>
        <p>Bowling Green Club Disqualified From Championship.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Council of the Warrington and District Amateur Bowling Association was held on Tuesday evening at the Ainior Con- servative Club. The protest lodged by the King and Queen Club against the Bowling Green Club playing Messrs. A. and J. Gleave as un- registered players in the replayed tie in the second round of the championship last Satur- day was sustained, and the Bowling Green Club was disqualified from the competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>Draw for the Semi-Final.</head>
        <p>Draw for the Semi-Final.</p>
        <p>The draw for the semi-final of the champion- ship, which takes place on the 24th and 31st July, was as follows : King and Queen v. Rylands's No. I.—Referee, Mr. C. Bowers. _</p>
        <p>Senior Conservatives v. Butt's Head.—Referee, Mr. T. Garnett.</p>
        <p>The draw for the semi-final of 'the Consola- tion Handicap, which will take place on the 7th and 14th August, was as follows:— Walton v. Rope and Anchor. Penketh Tannery or Walker's v. Junior Con- servatives.</p>
        <p>A RE-PLAYED TIE. The Losms Disqualified. The King and Queen and Bowling Greer</p>
        <p>Clubs .-met on Saturday to replay their tie in the second round of the Club Championship. The King and Queen players won by 34 points —222 to 188—the individual scores being as follows:</p>
        <p>King. and Queen.--H. Higham (captain) 21, C. Smith 13, A. Jolley 21, J. Barnard 21, T. Timperley 21, S. Garnett 16, W. Balf 20, J. War- burton 21, D. Bromley 18, S. Green 21, H. Hol- brook 14, H. Longhorn 15; total 222. - Bowling Green.—J. Appleton 17, R. Jefferson 21, T. Tweedle 7, J. Gleave 8, J. E. Thomasson 6, T. Litherland 21, A. Gleave No. 2) 21, W. Randells 6, T. J. Rigby 21, H. Jones 18, Albert Gleave 21, J. Hutchinson 21; total 183. As the Bowling Green Club played two -un- registered men it was, as reported elsewhere, subsequently decided by the Council of the Association that they be disqualified.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <p>SOMEWHERE IN GERMANY.</p>
