      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>DRUIDS' BOIA LING MAIN.</head>
        <p>DRUIDS' BOIA LING MAIN.</p>
        <p>The Order of Druids Friendly Society held their eighth annual bowling main on Saturday at the Bowling Green Hotel, Bank Quay, when the silver cup kindly given by Sir Gilbert Greensll, Bart., and other prizes, were bowled for. , Mr. Campbell, the genial host, had the green in splendid conditioh, and a pleasant afternoon's sport was thoroughly enjoyed. The winner of the cup and first prize was J. •Clarke, "Pride of Latchford" Lodge, s who beat Jos. Lloyd, the "Penketh Pride" Lodge, 13-7. Other prize-winners were :-3, J. Hill; 4, W. Roughley; 5, ,1. Andrews; 6, W. Gill; 7, Jos. Blackwood; 8, A. Woods.</p>
        <p>Bro. W. Bibby, Past Grand Master of the Order, presented the prizes, and a hearty vote, of 'thanks was passed to the donors of the prizes and the committee for the able mann*r in which the main had been conducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>ODDFELLOWS' HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS' HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>The eighth annum bowling handicap of the "Constitution" Lodge, No. 31, Independent Order of Oddfellows, Manchester Unity, will take place this -(Saturday) afternoon on the King and Queen -Hotel bowling green,- Padgate- lane, commencing at 2.15.</p>
        <p>Preliminary Round. S. B. Mellor v. S. Williams. Frank Lloyd v. John Wood (Mersey-street) S. Houghton v. T. Davies. W. Taylor v. W. H. Ball.</p>
        <p>First Round. J. B. Mellor v. J. W. Rigby. R. Kay v. J. H. Wild. T. Ford v. A. Pennington. • • R. Tilling v. T. Wood. J. Lawton v. J. W. Pemberton. W. Leigh (Beatrice-street) v. P. Lester. T. E. Janies v. S. Houghton or T. Davies. M. Aukland v. F. Lloyd or J. Wood. J. Wood (Fennel-street) v. N. J. Clarke. A. Lawton v. T. Watson. F. Hambletun v. S. B. 31ellor or S. Williams S. Wray v. W. Leigh (Mersey-street). A. Owen v. H. Kay. W. Taylor or W. H. Ball v. D.'\Ellison. J. Daintith v. J. R. Horne. H. Taylor v. W. Robinson. Time limit: 15 minutes allowed Preliminary Round only.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>BOWLERS' FOOTB XLI,S.</head>
        <p>BOWLERS' FOOTB XLI,S.</p>
        <p>Major Crosfield's Thanks For Works' Association's Gift.</p>
        <p>Following on the sending of four footballs to the 4th South Lancs. Regiment at the Front, as the result of a collection made on the bowling green of the Horse and Jockey Hotel a week last Saturday, on the occasion of the final of the War Fund Handicap, the following letter has been received by Mr. Patrick, the secretary of the Warrington Works' Bowling Association, from Major George Crosfield Many thanks for your letter and.for the footballs, which have duly arrived, and are beit4g played with this afternoon, with much enjoyment. Please con- vey the 'thanks of Colonel Fairclough and the officers of the. 114th South Lancs. Regiment- to the members of the Warrington Amateur Bowl- ing Association, and to any others who so ' sportingly ' raised the money for the football's last Saturday week, and let them know how much we appi7teiate their kindness."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>FUTURE OF FOOTMLL.</head>
        <p>FUTURE OF FOOTMLL.</p>
        <p>Tile Association And Ns Leagues To</p>
        <p>Confer.</p>
        <p>At a meeting in London next Monday the council of the Football •Association receive a report of the meeting held earlier in the clay between representatives of the Football 1,14; ne Land the Southern League and offices o the Football Association. They will take into con- sideration what action is desirable at the pre- sent time.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee will report that, although the amount derived from the semi- final and final tics diminished considerably this year, they do not recommend the ,council to make a grant to the four clubs concerned from the funds of the Association.</p>
        <p>SOUTHERN LEAGUE AGAINST COM- PETITIONS AND PROS.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. IT. Lawson (Swindon) presided in London nye; a meeting of the management Committee of the Southern League, at which the following resolutions were passed : /.-That the Football Association be requested to decline to register professionals, during the season 1915-16, subject to provision being made for the reinstatement as amateurs of all pro- fessionals during that period. 2.—That on April 30th, 1916, all clubs and players revert to the position as at April 30th, 1915.</p>
        <p>3.—That no competitive football, whether on the league or cup-tie principle, be allowed dur- ing the season 1915-16. 4.—That clubs be allowed to play friendly game 4 as they may -arrange to suit their local conditions.</p>
        <p>IRISH FOOTBALL LEAGUE. Next Season's Competitions Abandoned.</p>
        <p>The Management dommittee of the Irish Football League, met at Belfast on Wednesday and discussed the decision of the recent Black- pool confdence regarding football for next season.</p>
        <p>A resolution was unanimously agreed to that League competitions as at present constituted be abandoned until the end of April, 1916. It was left open to Belfast and Dublin clubs to consider the question of holding local com- petitions,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <p>SIR JOHN FRENCH'S DISPATCH.</p>
        <p>German Methods Of Barbarism</p>
        <p>lOW OUR LINE \%S BROKEN BY POISON 6S.</p>
        <p>Divisions Of New Army At The</p>
        <p>Front.</p>
        <p>Sir John French's dispatch, issued on Mon-</p>
        <p>_ day, covers ten weeks Of fighting up to mid- June. This is the period when the poison gas -methods were at their worst, our troops being unprepared for this form of barbarism. The outstanding passages in the dispatch are as follows:</p>
        <p>Poison Gas. I much regret that during the period under report the fighting has been characterised on the enemy's side by a cynical and barbarous disregard of the well-known usages of civilised war and a flagrant defiance of the Hague Con- vention. All the scientific resources of Germany have apparently been brought into play to produce a gas of so virulent and poisonous a nature that any human being brought into contact with it is fi-rst paralysed and then meets with a lingering and agonising death. As a soldier I cannot help expressing the deepest regret and some surprise that an Army which hitherto had claimed to be the chief exponent of the chivalry of war should have stooped to employ Such devices against , brave and gallant foes.</p>
        <p>Enemy's Artillery. • Throughout the whole period since the first break of the line on the night of April 22nd, all the troops in this area [the area subjected to poison gas] has been constantly subjected to a violent artillery bombardment from a large mass of gulp with an unlimited supply of ammunition. It proved impossible whilst under so vastly superior fire of artillery to dig efficient trenches, or to properly re- organise the line, after the confusion and demoralisation caused by the first great 'gas surprise and the subsequent almost daily gas attacks. The New Army.</p>
        <p>Since the date of my last report some divisions of the new Army have arrived in this country. I made a close inspection of one division, formed up on parade, and have at various tames seen several units belonging to others. These divisions have as yet had very little experience in actual fighting; but, judging from all I have seen, I am of opinion that they ought to prove a valuable addition to any fighting force. As regards the infantry, their physique is excellent, whilst their bearing and 'appearance on parade reflects great credit on the officers and staffs responsible for their training. The units appear to be thoroughly well officered and commanded. The equipment is in good order and efficient. .. •</p>
        <p>The Spirit of the Army. In spite • of the constant strain pnt upon them by the arduous natire- of the fighting which they are called upon to ,carry, out daily and almost hourly, the spirit which animates • all ranks of the Army in France remains .high, and confident. They meet every demand made upon them with the utmost cheerfulness. This splendid spirit is particularly mani- fested by the men in hospital, even amongst those who are mortally wounded. The inevitable question which comes from lips hardly able to utter a sound is, "How are things going on at the front?"</p>
        <p>At a Provincial Territorial Dinner Waitress to Chairman (the Mayor of the town): "The Captain thanks you, sir, for the honour, but he says he's too bad a speaker to respond to the toast." Chairman: "Oh, tell him we're all had speakers." Waitress: " I've told him that, sir."— " Pwich:' • •</p>
      </div>
