      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>NATIONAL REGISTER.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL REGISTER.</p>
        <p>Local Public Bodies Moving In</p>
        <p>The Matter.</p>
        <p>As a preliminary to the preparation of the local edition of the National Register, the Town Clerk requests that any lady or gentle- man who is willing to render., voluntary ser- vice shall comnrunicate with him, stating his or her present occupation, the hours they are willing to devote, whether they are open to deliver and collect registration papers or prefer clerical work in the office in filling up forms, indexing, and the preparation of the register. The enumeration will be taken during the second week in August, but much preliminary work will have to be done, and the preparation of the register will require considerable time; hence it is hoped that the response to the appeal for clerical assistance wilt not be tardy. RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL MAKE PRELIMINARY ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>At a preliminary meeting, convened by Mr. Furness, ,chairman, of the, Warrington Rural District Council War Committee, held on Wed- nesday, the subject of the National Register Bill was considered.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness explained that the exact ques- tions to be answered are not yet settled, but it was being urged upon Local Authorities to agree upon divisions and to accept the services of women in the delivery and collection of the registration papers, and in the indexing and compilation of the register. It was agreed by the councillors present that each parish form a district and voluntary effort,.be,psed in carrying out the work as far as is practical.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>A\ OI NDED MAN'S WALK.</head>
        <p>A\ OI NDED MAN'S WALK.</p>
        <p>Two-Mile Journey To Dressing-Station</p>
        <p>After He Was Hit.</p>
        <p>Writing from the Ist Eastern General Hos- pital, Cambridge, to Mr. Farrar, the secretary of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Brotherhood, Private Joseph Bond—a member of the Brotherhood who has been wounded—says :—" I got a pretty nasty knock during the big battle on the 16th of last month. . . I still have the bullet in my stomach. . . You would scarcely believe what a terrible business this war is! . After I got hit I had to walk a distance of three miles before I could get my wound properly dressed. I was almost on the point of dropping many a time, but I set ray teeth and plodded along."</p>
        <p>ask the Director of iducation to convey to Air. Robson on behalf of all present, their sincere sympathy with him in his illness,, and their best wishes for his complete recovery. (Ap- plause). He could not .let that opportunity pass without saying how very well, established in the respect and admiration of the people of the town the schools had become. He thought that they were now beginning to reap the reward of the arduous days of some few years ago, when, in spite of some prejudice and some opposition and in spite, also, of that policy of avoiding all public eXpense which some of their friends in the Council were so particularly fond of espousing, those who had the best.interests of education went on their Way, with the result that, to-day, they had now, among the other educational institutions, that well-established, well-managed and most useful Secondary School. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>..,: ..„%77,..7?:::::: -.. , --"'''''''..--,i':ii':'.,,i:.,ii4.4ii:.,':40,1:t41'.1,..i..?.,P.i5.;,- • 7..qk,?::,....,, P.A.i4,:::it::-.,,,,::.-,..Nt.1!::.:.:.,: 4.4k.'44*.t;,..5,!.-:1':-.-• ''''s ' sT ' „ Ir., .:::::,:,,,,,,....• .- ' .y......,..ut,Q,:: :-.. ii'A::','....4*.t!',''''' -. ' :' .....-:4:**44::.-'er-r:i..!,.•:::. . :::,4,:ii.e.:.•,:::::'tk.: . ~-.:,4:,."1'10-*Rkkt::,,'''' • ,- &gt; • :,,,,,,.: 4,p::,.‘,,,.4, 47.1.4.1.0.::;.',:::•'...f..f.:'....,.&lt; .4i.',...'''''' "' .:•...:.;.;.-.i';'.i.ii'..,'...'.:,„,!*„..: „ „;.,..i,,,:•..„..:i'.7:..0.',..;!„. . ' .-1..:;:.,,,.., .....v. .....,.,..,,,....,..:.4*.f::.. ........,..... ......„ -:...• ...,...„.,..,.. 't -.,,T?..-:,..'-',•,,::,.:.-.;.,',!: :-.:1-.': '' .4%.,k...:V....:K4N„.. .......•,.,. I .., 4.!.•;„'.. ' ' ,''',%'':::::--'''''iit:.44-0iN".0444:3,44. ''.7 :.''.'n.,:'] i.,.. .t-- ' •' ; 'f.--:i.',...,-,i1f1.#44t.. :*Ni.g.;;:tlm:tc,Lio.ta.,o ~,Agt.ii4NtF:fi......-,Niii.'l;t-it:47:Ak,ttl 1tt117.-A.,*.ig,4*.,i,t4iitti:igit,i7": A014Ei.1;;,-5t,tki,,,.,,,, ,t,:kit.t*itni'itill#:4l4l,o4l ...., ... ',1.:i..,..1.,::::.::,,,,?).;t0him''"' • ---,,:,,,,,..4::f.,;'.. • NIA- • ~.....,</p>
        <p>PRIVATE VICTOR FLETCHER. An "Old Boy' who died for his country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>Medical.</head>
        <p>Medical.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 17, 1915. THE GIRL'S OPPORTUNITY.</p>
        <p>Openings That liv'ar's Ravages Will Form For Her.</p>
        <p>responsible for the carrying on of the school ought to feel very proud of it and of the scholars it had turned out. To those who were still in attendance, she would like to say "When you go forth into the world do all you can to uphold the honour of the old school and to spread wide its fame, for a school which can show such a record as youls does is, indeed, a school to be proud df." Let them' remember, the Councillor continued, that in their hands, and in the hands of such as them, would be the destinies and the prosperity of England. It was a proud thing to grow up in that know- ledge; to know that each one of them could add, at least, some little to the honour-and the glory of 'England—boys and girls alike! Eng- land looked to the coming generation to carry on in the future the glorious traditions of her past. She would urge parents to remember that the conditions of England in the future— the conditions that wohld surround their boys and girls would be different from those con- ditions in which they themselves grew up in days of peace.</p>
        <p>The Misses Sophie Cross, Ethel Oakley, Mildred Isaac, Marian Littlewood and Mary Greenough and Master Albert Greenough. The singing was accompanied by an orchestra consisting of the following :-Ist violins: Messrs. W. Brough and H. Geary; 2nd violins: Edith Lister and Rhoda Grant; viola: Mr. Johnson; violoncello: Mr. Balmford ; bass: Mr. Whitfield; cornet: Mr. Rutter; and clarionet : Mr. Hipkiss. The next item on the programme was a clever performance ot a French fairy-tale playlet entitled "Le Chevalier du Guet," in which'the dramatis personse were :—Le Chevalier du Guet, Miss Dorothy Percival; Le Chevalier du Romarin, Miss Ella Middleton; Le Roi, Miss May Maginn; La Reine, Miss Annie Weston; Le Bouffon, Miss Ethel Birrell; La Princess Blanchette, Miss Charlotte Furness; Sa Dame d'Honneur, Miss Florence Bissell; Tic, Miss May Johnson; and Toe, Miss Madge Knowles. The last item on the programme was the school song, "Forty tears On," which was lustily sung by the scholars. Interesting examples of the Pupils' work in various directions were on exhibition in the different classrooms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>PRIZE DAY AT THE SECONDARY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>PRIZE DAY AT THE SECONDARY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>In the presence of a large assemblage of parents and friends of the scholars the annual prize distribution in connection with the War- rington Municipal Secondary School took place ' on Thursday afternoon, in the Borough Gymnasium. The gathering was, appropri- ately, presided Over by Councillor H. Roberts, the chairman of the Education Committee, which body brought the school into being and under whose care it has become the beneficial educational institution that it is now, on all hands, admitted to be. Apart from the all- present shadow of war—accentuated in this case by the fact that at least one "old boy" (Private Victor H. Fletcher, of the Warring- ton Territorials)- has laid down his .life for King and country—an added gloom hung over. the proceedings, by reason ,of • the enforced absence through serious illness of the esteemed Headmaster (Mr. E. S. A. Robson), whose devotion and untiring Oorts in the past have in no small measure conduced to the present successful position held by the institution. Supporting the Chairman on the platform were Mr. A. J. Jolley (the vice-chairman of the ,Education Committee), Councillor Margaret Ashton (of Manchester City Council and Edu- cation Committee), Mrs: C. Jackson Holmes, Mrs. 11. Roberts, Aldermen Bennett and Smethurst, and Messrs. J. Jarman, C. Plinston, and Mr. J. Moore Murray Director of Educa- tion), together with the Acting •Headmaster (Mr. J. S. Broome). As C,Ouncillor Margaret Ashton took her place on the platform she was presented by Miss Edith Bailey, on behalf of the teachers and scholars, with a beautiful bouquet of 'pink carnations.</p>
        <p>REAPING THE REWARD.</p>
        <p>women of the town were prepared to take, and were taking their proper part at this time of such great and enormous importance.</p>
        <p>Women's Part in the War.</p>
        <p>The women of England, all round, had taken a magnificent part and were still taking a magnificent parts not merely in rendering aid through ambulance classes and societies, but in their general adaptability to any circum- stances in which their labour and efforts might be required. They had demonstrated that courage, personal bravery and self-sacrifice were not the monopoly of the men, (Hear, hear.) Indeed, if anything, he thought the women of England had rather given the men a lead. There had been no contention where the women, had been concerned—no women's strikes and no difficulties created by them. On the contrary, they had played the patriotic part, and been content to waive any and every difference of opinion in the presence of the great national crisis. (Applause.) Women to- day had done what, perhaps, some among the menfolk had never expected to see them do-!-- they had done great work. "And," added Councillor Roberts, amid applause, "after having, practically all my life, opposed the entrance of women into the franchise, I am to-day an absolute convert. The Chairman Converted to Women's Suffrage. "In face of the innumerable benefits they have conferred upon our brave fellows in the Army, and in face of the work they have done out of it, I could no longer argue against the demand of the women for thefranchise, and, what is more, I am not going to argue against it, but it is my intention for the remainder of my life to do all that I can to promote all that the principle of the equality of the sexes de- mands. ' To turn to the men, between eighty and ninety of the lads who have passed through the school—it is only a comparatively new school, too—are serving their country in vari- ous branches of her Forces. In the Royal Navy, in the Infantry, in the Cavalry, in the R.A.M.C., in the A.S.C., in the Ordnance branch, on the mine-sweepers and as wireless operators 'old boys' of the school are doing their duty on behalf of their country and to the infinite credit of the town and of the old school of which they are, doubtless, so proud."</p>
        <p>THE ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>Councillor Roberts on the Prejudices of the</head>
        <p>Councillor Roberts on the Prejudices of the</p>
        <p>Past.</p>
        <p>The speeches incidental to such an occasion were sandwiched in between the singing of the National Anthem, a most creditable perform- ance of an extremely tuneful cantata and a well-acted French fairy-tale playlet. The Chairman began his introductory speech by saying that he had received a letter from Mr. E. S. A. Robson, which conveyed the in- formation that acting upon his doctor's advice, he weis not able to be with them that after- noon. He (the Chairman) was sure that he was not merely expressing his own regret, but was also expressing .that of all present, parents and scholars alike, when he said how very sorry they were for Mr. Robson's absence, and how sincerely they hoped that he might be spared to them for a long time to come. He was a teacher of whom they were all particularly Proud, and he had done great service to the cause of education through, the instrumentality of that school—a service which would bear fruit for many, many years still to come. He would</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0300" n="art0300">
        <head>Proud cf the Work of the Scho9l.</head>
        <p>Proud cf the Work of the Scho9l.</p>
        <p>They were proud of the work of that •school and of the results of its work. Parents were becoming accustomed now to the fact that the successful scholars of that school were now- adays entering into spheres of occupation which were certainly closed to them. hitherto— closed to the children of the class to which those attending that school belonged, up to a very few years ago! It might not, perhaps, be unwise to remind 'Chose present that all occupa- tions in the Civil Service of the country were, until comparatively recently, practically closed to the children of working people because of the lack of opportunity they, had owing to the want of sufficient and proper educational facili- ties. Those difficulties had now been removed and, as a result, that school was figuring in a remarkable way, in regard to the proportion of places obtained by examination in the Civil Service by boys and, even girls, from the school. (Applause.) The authorities had now added a University scholarship to the prizes which they had annually to award to poholars of that school, and in that connection he thought that he was only doing justice to the school and its teaching staff when he said that they were especially glad to note that the first Youth they sent up, a little over a year ago, under the University scholarship scheme—young Mason— had done particularly well. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0301" n="art0301">
        <head>Three Year Course Too Short.</head>
        <p>The Three Year Course Too Short.</p>
        <p>Three ayears at that school was, he (the speaker) thought, too short, and he wished he could sufficiently impress upon the parents the desirability of making a little more sacrifice, if necessary, in order that their children might have a fourth year at the school, instead of being taken away at the end of the third year. He was quite sure that parents would be more than repaid for .any sacrifice they might be called upon to make if they allowed their chil- dren to stay a fourth year—repaid in the pros- pects of additional success and usefulness which the children would secure. He was pleased to be able to say that the better places in the com- mercial houses of the town were now being given, by preference, to the successful scholars from that Secondary School. The school, the speaker went on, had played a very useful and a very praiseworthy part in the war. The girls had .provided a very large number of comforts for the troops. They had assisted, also, in collecting no less than £l4O for the various funds which had been opened for the purpose of making those additional supplies for the troops, and they had shown that the girls and young</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0302" n="art0302">
        <head>Forty-five Per Cent. of the Old Boys With the</head>
        <p>Forty-five Per Cent. of the Old Boys With the</p>
        <p>Colours.</p>
        <p>The Chairman's speech ended, to the accom- paniment of ringing cheers, the Acting Head- master presented the annual report, as follows:</p>
        <p>"During the past session there have been 239 pupils in the school, consisting of 101 boys and 138 girls, of whom nine are student teachers. In the previous year there were 212 pupils, of whom ten were student teachers. Owing to the war, the school year has been exceptional in many ways. We have raised—pupils and staff—over £l4O in aid of the various war funds, and th 6 girls, .under the able guidance of our assistant mistresses, have ,made numer- ous useful articles of apparel for our brave soldiers and sailors. A very popular and pleas- ing fund was the Christmas Present Fund, by means of which every old boy of the school who had enlisted was provided with a Christmas gift from the•school. Some 85 old boys have 'responded to their country's call-45 per cent.— and it is gratifying to notice that nearly every branch of His 'Majesty's, Forces has its repre- sentative among our old boys, thus showing the breadth of ,the training the boys receive in the school. This year Has alio seen the change of Qur athletic field. It is unfortunate that the new field is 60 far away, but it is hoped that the better conditions will induce our pupils to continue to figure exceedingly well in sports. The athletic clubs—football, hockey, cricket and tennis—are all in a healthy condition—the two former having had a most successful season. Our annual swimming gala, held in September, was also a pronounced success. Best Examination Successes in the' History of</p>
        <p>the School.</p>
        <p>"All` the pupils of the school are under agreement to remain for at least three years. It is to be regretted that a large number con- tinue to leave at the end of the third year; and here may I plead for alonger stay in the school? Educatioon has its recompense, which cannot be measured financially. It is only those who remain in the school for/four or five. years who reap the higher benefits of education—a trained intelligence, a self-reliant character, and a fuller life. And, further, it is our experience that the longer a boy remains in the school, the better Choice he hos of ulti- mately occupying a good position. The exam- ination successes of the school during the year have been, perhaps, the best in its history. "All the pupils who sat for the Preliminary Certificate Examination for Teachers passed. The successes in Civil Service Examinations attracted the attention of the Chief Inspector of Secondary Schools, and he has expressed his appreciation. Past and present students will be glad to hear of the following old pupils who have achieved good results in pursuing a higher education : James R. Dewar, Inter B.Sc. Reginald C. Essenhigh, Open. Scholarship to the Royal College of Art. London. William E. Mason, Ist M.B. Manchester University, and who has been awarded the Dauntesey Senior Medical Scholar- ship. William Swaine, who has graduated B.Sc. with Honours in Physics. A Lady Teacher in the War Zone.</p>
        <p>" This.year of war has not only made demands upon the usefulness of our old students, but it has now requisitioned the services of a highly- valued and, respected member of our staff. The teacher in question leaves to go to France as an interpreter in a semi-official capacity, and we wish her God'-speed in her new sphere of work. In concluding the report I wish to thank the pupils of the school for having maintained their industry and attention to study during the somewhat exacting times, and also their parents, who have so ably supported them, especially in connection with the various efforts which have been promoted for the war funds; and Mr. Robson wishes me on his behalf to thank all his colleagues on the staff for their loyal co-operation and untiring efforts through- out this memorable year."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0303" n="art0303">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL SCHOLARS.</head>
        <p>THE SUCCESSFUL SCHOLARS.</p>
        <p>Councillor Margaret Ashton, who was ac- corded 'a hearty round of applause by the scholars and others present, then distributed the prizes in' accordance with the following list, offering a few words of congratulation to each recipient : Form I.—Ada Freeth, John Edgar and Ruth Parsons.</p>
        <p>Form ll.—John Shaw, Wilfred Simcock and Annie Duckworth.</p>
        <p>Form 111.—Elsie Hatch, Roger Leigh and William Burrows. Form IV.—Frederic Davies, Alice Wilding and Dorothy Atherton. Form Vb.—Elsie Wheatcroft, Robert ‘% hit- field and Ada Leigh._</p>
        <p>_ Form Va.—Leonard Lygo, Muriel Chidlaw and James Robinson.</p>
        <p>Form Vl.—Kathleen Brown, Aubrey Downes and Bessie Lee.</p>
        <p>Bursars.—Edith Bailey, Rhoda ' Grant and Ella Done.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of her pleasant task of</p>
        <p>handing over the prizes, Councillor Margaret Ashton graciously shook hands with and com- plimented Mr. William Swaine, an "old boy," who ha l; recently taken his B.Sc. degree at Manchester University, and who was ,wearing his newly-won academicals.</p>
        <p>A SCHOOL TO BE PROUD OF. Lady Councillor's High Praise. The lady Councillor then addressed the com- pany. She could, she said, say with perfect be present at any other school prize distribu- tion, for she thought that the record ,of work and achievftent accomplished by that school was an extremely fine one. All who were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0304" n="art0304">
        <head>War and the Future Position of Women.</head>
        <p>War and the Future Position of Women.</p>
        <p>The parents of to-day were confronted by greater *responsibilities towards their children, and particularly towards their girls, by reason of the altered conditions which: would prevail after the war. Girls would need to begin to support themselves in the future. They could no longer look so confidently to being provided with a protector and a happy home of their own. Many of those who would have formed the happy homes and have taken care of the women of the future would be gone, sacrificed in the great war, and consequently many must be denied that happiness which was the best happiness that a woman could have. Therefore, the sirls of the future must be more indepen- dent, and iiarents must see to it that their girls were fully and properly equipped for the struggle of life. They must not allow them simply to drift into the old, cheap employments, which, in the past, they had been allowed to avail themselves of as a makeshift until marriage came and save them other thoughts and occupations. The girls of to-morrow would have to make their own way, just as the boys of the past had had to do. As to the boys of the future, there would be grand openings; the way would be cleared for many, many more than in the past, as it was clearing for the girls, and to both better positions would be open than ever before. But the parents must think ahead!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0305" n="art0305">
        <head>Word of Warning to the Parents.</head>
        <p>A Word of Warning to the Parents.</p>
        <p>The girls must be skilled in the trades and occupations which they would be called upon to follow; they must be properly trained so that what they earned in early life would enable them to put by something against their old age, which for many of the single women of the past had been an existence of penury and sorrow. It was the duty of the parents to see that, henceforth, their girls were as well-fitted to carry, on the battle of life as were their boys. There must be no putting of them into situations as glorified eirand girls, or setting them to cheap, ill-paid employment! Throughout her life she had been struggling to open up professions for girls, and, in these days, more and more of the professions and industries were opening up their doors to the women, and, consequently, it was up to the parents to see to it that their daughters were fitted to walk in and take the places which /awaited them. The nation was at last awak- ening to the value of the woman to the nation. (Hear, hear.) The jlext few years would be the women's' years, and on the shoulders of the women of the future, as well as on those of the men of the future, would rest the wel- fare of the England they all loved 60 well. To boys and girls alike her advice was ", work for the ,glory of England and make the best of your opportunities whilst you are yet young and can make such use of them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0306" n="art0306">
        <head>MR. A. J. JOLLEY-DEMOCRAT!</head>
        <p>MR. A. J. JOLLEY-DEMOCRAT!</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley then proposed a vote of thanks to Councillor Margaret Ashton for her address, jocularly remarking that some people regarded him as a crusted old Tory, and his friend, Councillor Roberts, as a most progres- sive Liberal, and yet all his life, he had been an advocate of Universal Suffrage. (Laughter and applause.) 4e was a democrat by intel- lectual conviction, and, for good or ill, this country was, the most democratic country in the world, although he did not suppose that their Yankee friends would admit it—but that was 'only due to their ignorance. (Laughter.) When the pducation Authority started that school some twelve years ago they were met with very violent antagonism, but the success with which it had met, and the benefits which it Had conferred, had refuted all the argu- ments of those who were opposed to its insti- tution„ and he had, no hesitation in saying that to-day the school was regarded by everybody who counts in the town as 'One of its most valuable assets. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0307" n="art0307">
        <head>WHAT MIGHT BE, IF</head>
        <p>WHAT MIGHT BE, IF</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Jarman, in seconding, mentioned that Warrington had not yet got a lady councillor, and hoped that *hen they did get one the manners of the Council would rise to a higher level than they appeared to have reached at the • present moment. (Laughter.) Possibly the presence of a lady in the Council Chamber would prevent some of - the things .that . were now said, being said. (Renewed laughter.) The proposition waS put and -carried with acclamation, Councillor Margaret Ashton briefly returning her thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0308" n="art0308">
        <head>ALDERMANIC EULOGY.</head>
        <p>ALDERMANIC EULOGY.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman, remarking that there was no position that Councillor Roberts occupied that he did not also adorn. (Hear, hear.) His heart was in his work as Chairman of the Education Committee, and he was a chairman of whom the whole committee were proud. He (the speaker) was an old-fashioned Tory, and, consequently, like Mr. Jolley, a believer in Universal Suffrage, listened with much plea- sure to the Chairman's avowal on the subject of Women's Suffrage. Mr. Jolley was on the side of the angels. (Laughter and 'applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <head>"NOT QUITE CONVERTED."</head>
        <p>"NOT QUITE CONVERTED."</p>
        <p>Alderman Smethurst, in seconding, said that Councillor Roberts had rendered invaluable r- vice to the cause of education in the town, and the Education Committee could not have a better chairman. The committee had had very good chairmen, but they had had node better than Councillor Roberts. "At the same time," added the Alderman, "I must say that I am not quite converted to what Councillor Roberts has told us, to-day, he is converted to." (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <head>AN "OLD BOY" WHO GAVE HIS LIFE.</head>
        <p>AN "OLD BOY" WHO GAVE HIS LIFE.</p>
        <p>The proposition having been carried with applause, the Chairman, in reply, referred to the school roll of honour and to the fact that one of the old boys—Private Victor Fletcher— had been killed. "I saw him," said Mr. Roberts, "when he was saying good-bye at Bank Quay Station, and I hoped and prayed that he might come back. Now, somewhere in some little wayside place in France, his body rests quietly. He gave of his best; nay he gave his all; as so many others are doing, and he has left an inspiring example behind him, the genesis of which may, to some extent, be at- tributed to the principles of service and of loyalty inculcated in this school." Referring to what had been said by Mr. Jarman "about another body," Councillor Roberts said that perhaps the presence, of Mrs. Jackson Holmes on the Education Committee had largely helped to restrain "the very, very small and trouble- some minority." As a whole the local educa- tional body was a very sound and well behaved one and; by comparison with another local pub- lic body, "took the honours." (Laughter and applause.) • THE ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>The major portion of the entertainment sus- tained, to the delight of the company, by the scholars, was devoted to a performance of the tuneful cantata by M. C. Gillington and T. Mee Pattison, "The Shepherd's Holiday." This was sung in excellent style and every number contained in it was warmly applauded. Those who sang solos, or took part in duets were:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <head>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</p>
        <p>ENTERTAINED AT THE JUNIOR LIBERAL</p>
        <p>CLUB. On Thursday evening the members of the Junior Liberal Club gave a party of wounded heroes a right hearty welcome in their ,rooms at Horsemarket Chambers, when a capi- tal programme was arranged by the Honorary Secretary (Mr. P. Peacock, jun.). Several gentlemen very kindly offered their services and the concert commenced- with Mr. Tomkinson at the , piano, who, by request, played several soldiers' favourites, such as "Tipperary," and the men fairly -let out their voices with the choruses.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Taylor delighted the audience with a song, "Saving the Colours." Then fol- lowed Dick Scarisbrick, the famous Warring- ton entertainer and character comedian, with a flute sole played on a cigar box and long sweeping brush, ventriloquism, Stump speeches and his latest "one-armed" flute player and ballet dancer. His mannerisms were exception- ally smart and laughable, and fairly took the "khakis" by storm; so much so that they would not part with him without two and sometimes three encores. He certainly made a big "hit." The same remark applies to the agent of the Liberal Association tMr. E. A. Sanders), whose stump speeches and recitations delighted the soldiers. • Mr. T. Boscow was very popular with various songs, rendered in his usual capable style, in- cluding "Boys of the Ocean Blue," "Two Eyes of Grey," "Mary's Choice," and "The Trumpeter." Messrs. Brobbin and •Handley gave two very fine selections on the concertina. Their play- ing was wonderful, and the turn was indeed immensely popular and delightful. The sol- diers helped to make the evening a bigger suc- cess, Private Paching, Driver Rosenberg add Bombardier Broomfield singing a few songs, which were highly successful. The Secretary extended an invitation to the wounded at the Whitecross Hospital, to come to the club at any time to play billiards, &amp;c., without any, charge. He hoped to be able to do something further for them in the near future.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Walker (vice-chairman), in the un- avoidable absence of the popular Chairman (Mr. George Toulmin), also spoke, hoping they had enjoyed themselves. When the time came for them to go back to the trenches he wished them a speedy and safe return. COuncillor Gough spoke Of the exceptional pluck and valour of our men and the brutality of the Germans. He hoped the war would soon end and that when the war was over the wives and families of the gallant fallen would be well provided for as they richly deserved. He .thanked them for what they had done for us out at the Front, and wished them God-speed and a safe return.</p>
        <p>The National Anthem concluded a really most delightful evening.</p>
        <p>THANKS TO THE WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Mrs.'Edelsten, and the committee of the War Hospital Fund, desire to thank the following for various gifts for the wounded soldiers The Women's Liberal Association, the Train- ing College, the Working Men's Mission, Hamilton-street Infants' School, Thewlis-street Infants' School, the Penketh Workin Party, Mrs. Evans, Mrs. Frost, Mrs. Griffiths, Mrs. Locker, Mrs. Mitchell, Mrs. Owen, Mrs. Venn, Mrs. Webster, Miss Chamberlain (Padgate), Miss Clayton, Miss Dannett, Miss Gill, Misses Lidiard,. Miss Winstanley, the Misses Wood, and. Captain. Sumnierfield. Grateful letters of thanks have been received from Pazakerley, Whitecross, and the Lord Derby Military Hospitals.</p>
        <p>Attention is drawn to the notice on the front Page-</p>
        <p>SACRED MUSIC.</p>
        <p>The members of the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Choir paid a visit to the Raddon Court Red Cross Hospital on Sunday afternoon and saw, a number of appropriate sacred selec- tions. Solos were rendered by Mrs. A. E. Hoyle, Miss Eva Tanner (the Temperance Queen) and Mr. J. Dakin; whilst Mr. F. H. Banner offi- ciated as accompanist. The arrangements were carried out by Mr. P. A. Spruce.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S CRICKETERS TO VISIT WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>Rylands's Recs. C.C. are selecting a very at- tractive side for the return match with ti.e wounded soldiers at Lord Derby's Military Hos- pital, Winwick, on Wednesday next, at 2 P.m.</p>
        <p>Rylands's will be represented from the follow- ing players:— W. H. Bell, W. Bennett, G. Woodcock (War- rington C.C.), Bert Renwick (All Blacks R.F.C.), S. Carter (Leigh R.F.C.), J. Roberts (Little Lever C.C.), J. Heywood (Manchester United A.F.C.), F. Horrocks and A. Bramhall (Warrington R.F.C.), E. Sanders (captain), J. Wrench, F. Lemon, T. Knight, J. Constable and G. McGowan.</p>
        <p>The regimental band of the 2nd South Lancs. is expected 'to be in attendance.</p>
        <p>CONCERT AT WHITECROSS HOSPITAL</p>
        <p>In the Whitecross Hospital on Wednesday evening a concert for the soldiers., arranged by Mr. J. W. Jowett, was given. The following artistes ably acquitted themselves :—Miss Hipkiss (soprano), Mrs. Smallshaw (contralto), Mr. J. Hannan (tenor), Mr. F. Jubb (flute), Mr. H. Lee (euphonium), Driver Rosenberg and Private Calverly. The actompanists were Mrs. H. Berry, Messrs. Lee and J. W. Jowett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <head>POPULAR SERGT.-MAJOR.</head>
        <p>POPULAR SERGT.-MAJOR.</p>
        <p>Birthday Present At The Lord Derby</p>
        <p>Hospital. A unique and interesting event took place on Monday evening, at Winwick, when a smoker was held and a presentation made to Sergt.- Major Caple, of the R.A.M.C., on the occasion of his birthday. It is less than three months since the Sei4geant-Major was drafted to the above hospital to take charge of the 19th Co. Western Command R.A.M.C. at the above hos- pital. But during that time he has made a fine name for himself. A magnificent drill in- structor, fair, upright and urbane, yet brook- ing no slackness or carelessness, in dress, drill and company work, he has become, deservedly popular amongst alr sections of the Company, and the name of the detachment for smartness and efficiency has travelled far.</p>
        <p>The presentation was made by Lieutenant G. J. Lander, who testified to the respect and con- fidence of all in the deservedly popular officer, and wished him long life and many happy returns.</p>
        <p>The presents consisted of a splendid and valuable "Sam Browne" belt, a letter wallet, and a beautiful Ceylon ebony sergeant-major's walking stick, which was surmounted by a mas- sive silver knob, bearing on it the inscription: "Presented to Sergeant-Major Caple, on the occasion of his 38th birthday, as a mark of respect and esteem, by the St. John Ambulance Brigade recruits W. 19th Co. R.A.M.C., at die Lord Derby War Hospital, Warrington." Felicitous and timely remarks were made by Lieutenant W. Tuson. A splendid programme was rendered by mem- bers of the detachment, augmented by selections on the bagpipes by Mr. Luke Challis, of Man- chester. After a hearty vote of thanks, pro- posed by Sergeant Leonard and seconded by Corporal Tattersall, to officers and artistes, the proceedings concluded with the singing of the National Anthem..</p>
        <p>Corporal Goddard acted as M.C., and Private Searle, of the Welsh Guards, officiated at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>FINE FARE AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The "piece de resistance" in a bill of all' round excellence is provided by Alan Borth" wick and Co. in a screamingly funny sketch, entitled " Half-a-Dollar." Truly, it is as in- geniously conceived and as liunioursome trifle as was ever devised to tickle the risible raculties of vaudeville lovers, and all who rnisl it have at least one thing to be sorry for. Another turn of outstanding merit is provided by the Real licKays, a lad and his lassie wllO sing and dance and make merry in general, t 0 the great delight of all beholders. The Five Jovers provide yet another first-class line. The) are amongst the smartest, most original and. most daring - bar-athletes we. have evel glimpsed. Mike Cray presents a coined,' juggling act, which is one long roar of laugh- ter, and he has the assistance of a wonderfull! trained little fox-terrier, . who appears Poi; tively to revel in his part of the show. Hqo and Gard, comedy musicians; :Vary Moran, ln clever character cameos and studies from life; and the Stevanos, "eccentric college boys," to say nothing of the usual interesting pictures go to make up a really tip-top entertainment. ,</p>
        <p>Big Attractions for Next Week.</p>
        <p>Heading a fine bill 'at the Hippodrome nest week will be the Three Ernests, in a sparkling</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <p>p</p>
        <p>comedy novelty, whilst other big turns will elude Tom E. Hughes, "the rag-bag of vanit3r9, Jean Schwiller, the Anglo-Russian 'cellist, Iblgjdi 'Hughes, "the man who chalks and talks," B'l, a:. variety of other clever and entertainimfr people.</p>
        <p>,I-- . . , - ,'. ' . - -.'••• e•:....i...:'..". '1 t• •,::••••. ~, r,,,,,,, ie.• ' ~..,.. ~.......::::::::: . t.' . • . Yr4'7.:17;,,... '•,:::::::-:::::]:::i • , . .' ...'73.4., .21,1. '...::::::1•.:...... :, • s ?•:', _ • ::-.; ~...V: .Vit: -.'", \ .....:.:.:-.........,.-.,:' c, :---=---= . •'..1:-.7: -, ~ ' i.F.:::::::....... ;,, ---...-.:...p.,- .. / .....:::::.....:..:. .d.,, 4.:.:: ~, //: 1:',....k:i....'....:............:::::::: ........-- 1 4r ... -:..,,,,;&lt;;',•:.. ~.-,:;*. ..............- -..." - .- .. : ..:-...'der.',4-.-*,-.. ..,-...,.,• ...:::.::-. - .:. .-.........it'......- !. .:(k,:,......:-... „ . . . ...„ .. „,„,, .zt.,. .:. • - - . t... A., . , e ......,„ it‘t'i ..eil, ,-^0.1: ' ''' '• 1e..Z..j1 ......, ....- • TTIE RAG-BAG OF VANITY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <head>MARY PICKFORD DRAMA AT STA%</head>
        <p>MARY PICKFORD DRAMA AT STA%</p>
        <p>KINEMA.</p>
        <p>There are strong programmes at the leading Star Kinema House to-day—for tilt fine drama of life and love, "Behind tl Scenes," with the well-beloved Mary Pickf°T4, in the forefront, is only onO of several shown, all of which are quite up to the 10118' Kinema standard. During the first half Of this week also 111311 Z tip-top films were thrown on the screen. -`,'o accordance with the fashion and demand, surely-inimitable Charlie Chaplin carried before him. "Champion Charlie," an e%cWri.l; ciatingly funny boxing farce, showed that master-comedian is still going better. °lt: better. " The Brute," was a long and esting story of a married woman's duel A°,11.10 story, showing how the fates unloosed t Gordian knot which would not yield to her' wavering touch. "Josie's Legacy was aYe funny film of fickleness. " Cambria's alorY p showed' much pretty, and familiar Welsh 113 atav°,ll,l; tam n scenery. A capital issue of Animated Gazette" pictured war preparati°ea abroad and at home, and concluded 16°10,- highly _amusing cartoon—" The Police Dog</p>
        <p>from the gifted pen of Dudley Buxton.</p>
        <p>For Next Week. of The "star" film on the first three da). next week will be "The Soul's Awakenlle whilst at the week-end a subject bearing 0 topical.title, "It's a Long Way to TipPeralY will form the big attraction.</p>
