        <p>doubt he would look after . the boy to the best of his ability. The boy had been earning q. week in a Blackburn mill before he came to Warrington. The Chairman said that largely on the father's account the Bench. would be as lenient as possible, as he was prepared to pay what wat''' owing. "As .for you," the Chairman added, addressing the boy, "I want you to understand that you have just missed being sent to a. reformatory. If you come here again, you will go to a reformatory till you are 19. The father was then bound over in the Slink of £5.</p>
