        <p>WOUNDED. ACTING-CORPORAL R. BROBBIN. Twice Wounded In A Charge On Enemy's Trenches. A field-card received on Sunday morning by Mrs. Brobbin, of Steel-street, conveyed to her the news that her husband, Acting-Corporal Robert Brobbin, of) the Rifle Brigade, had been wounded in, action. "Put the Top Hat On It!" On Tuesday, a letter 'written under date July 9th, arrived from Acting-Corporal Brobbin himself. It contained the following : "We took some trenches first thing in the morning. I got a slight bullet-wound in the left elbow as soon as we got to the enemy's trenches. We were waiting to be relieved, when, at about 10 p.m., I received a bayonet wound through the lower parts of both of my legs, and, of course, that 'put the top hat on it!' I am going on very well under the cir- cumstances, so don't worry." • Well-known, In Bowling Circles. Acting-Corporal Brobbin, who is 34 years of age, formerly worked in the glycerine depart- ment at Messrs. J. Crosfield and Son's. He was educated at the Heathside School. He is very well-known in bowling circles, and is a member of the Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club. Several relatives of his are serving with the</p>
